window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"andrae-crouch-the-blood-will-never-lose-its-power":["A slow hymn of devotion, sung by Andraé Crouch with his group the Disciples over piano and a light rhythm section. The melody is simple and wide-spaced, built to be sung by whole congregations, and the harmony under it moves in warm, gently surprising chords. Crouch's voice is not a powerhouse; it is light, slightly husky and intimate, and the arrangement leaves space around it. It sounds like a hymn that has always existed, which is exactly the effect he wanted.","Crouch wrote the song as a young man in Los Angeles, the son of a Church of God in Christ pastor, and recorded it on the Disciples' debut album, Take the Message Everywhere, for the new Light Records. He was working at the edge of two worlds: the Black Pentecostal church he grew up in, and the white evangelical youth movement of Southern California, which would soon become the Jesus Movement. The song travelled through both, ending up in hymnals of many denominations.","The song's strength is structural. The verse melody stays within a narrow range while the chorus opens up, rising to its highest note on the title phrase, so that a congregation reaches its loudest point exactly where the meaning is. Underneath, Crouch uses chord changes from the jazz and pop songs he loved, small chromatic slides between familiar harmonies that make an ordinary hymn shape sound modern without unsettling anyone singing it.","Within a decade the song had become one of the most widely sung gospel hymns written in the twentieth century, recorded by hundreds of choirs and soloists, Black and white. That reach was itself a kind of argument: Crouch showed that new gospel songs could cross the colour line in church at a time when radio and record shops still divided the market sharply. Hymnal editors now treat it as a standard alongside Dorsey's 'Precious Lord'.","Listen once to the original Disciples recording, with its spare piano, and once to any large choir version, to hear how well the melody carries a crowd. Notice how the chorus lifts. Then play Thomas A. Dorsey's 'Take My Hand, Precious Lord' to hear the earlier songwriting tradition Crouch inherited and quietly modernised."],"andrae-crouch-live-at-carnegie-hall":["Andraé Crouch and the Disciples in concert in New York, a small group rather than a choir, with electric guitar, bass, drums and keyboards behind a handful of singers. The set moves between slow worship songs and fast numbers that sound close to the soul and pop-rock of the early seventies, with crisp backbeats and bright vocal harmonies. Crouch talks to the audience between songs like a youth pastor, and the crowd sings back on the songs it already knows.","By 1973 Crouch had become the most visible figure in contemporary Christian music, playing to Black churches and to the young white audiences of the Jesus Movement alike. A Carnegie Hall date was a statement: gospel had played there before, notably with Mahalia Jackson in the fifties, but a band with electric guitars and a rock drummer playing church songs on that stage was new. Light Records released the concert as a double album.","Listen for the guitar. Most gospel of the time was keyboard music, but Crouch's group used electric guitar in a way borrowed from soft rock and soul, playing short chord stabs and fills rather than solos. The vocal arrangements are tight four- and five-part writing, closer to the Fifth Dimension than to a church choir, and Crouch often lets the group sing a cappella for a few bars before the band drops back in.","Traditionalists objected to the beat and the guitars, as they had to the Hawkins Singers; some white evangelical radio stations, for their part, found the group too Black and too funky. Crouch kept both audiences anyway. Historians of Christian music now see this period as the moment two separate religious music industries briefly met in one band, and the Carnegie Hall album as one of its best documents.","Notice how the evening alternates fast and slow songs, and how Crouch uses the slow ones to bring the hall into near silence. Listen for the moments when the audience joins without being asked. Compare Mahalia Jackson's Live at Newport 1958 to hear how much the sound of a gospel concert changed in fifteen years."],"andrae-crouch-soon-and-very-soon":["A brisk, joyful song about the believer's coming meeting with God, built on a short melody that repeats and rises in steps. Crouch's group sings it with handclaps and a bouncing rhythm section, and the tune is so simple that a room learns it on the first hearing. Underneath the cheer is a song about death, which gives it a double colour: celebration on the surface, farewell beneath. It closes in a call-and-response vamp that can be stretched as long as a congregation wants.","The song appeared on Crouch's 1976 album This Is Another Day for Light Records, at the height of his fame. It quickly passed from his records into the congregational repertoire, becoming a favourite at homegoing services, the Black church's name for funerals. In 2009 it was sung at the public memorial for Michael Jackson in Los Angeles, with whom Crouch had worked, carrying it to a vast television audience that had never heard of its writer.","The melody climbs one step higher each time the main phrase returns, a device as old as the spiritual and the field holler, and Crouch places the rise so that the third statement lands at the top of a comfortable congregational range. Listen also to the bass line, which walks upward in step with the melody, so the whole band seems to be ascending together. It is a song about going up, written so that it physically does.","Few songs of the seventies passed into church use as quickly or as permanently. It has been sung across denominations and continents, and its use at funerals has given it an emotional weight far beyond its cheerful surface. Some critics of contemporary gospel found Crouch's catchiest songs too slight; the counter-argument, which has largely won, is that simplicity is exactly what congregational songs need to survive.","Listen first for the step-by-step climb in the melody and the bass. Then hear a recording from a homegoing service or the 2009 memorial, where tempo and mood shift completely, and notice how the song bears both joy and grief. Pair it with Mahalia Jackson's performance of 'Take My Hand, Precious Lord' at Martin Luther King's funeral."],"andrae-crouch-mercy":["A lush, carefully produced album of worship songs and up-tempo numbers, with large choirs, layered keyboards, real horns and the polished studio sound of nineties R&B. Crouch sings less than on his early records and arranges more: songs open quietly on piano or synthesiser pads, then gather choirs and band in long crescendos. The writing ranges from hymn-like ballads to grooves close to new jack swing, held together by his gift for melodies that a congregation can pick up immediately.","Crouch had stepped back from recording through much of the eighties, while his arranging career boomed: he led the choirs heard on Michael Jackson's 'Man in the Mirror' and Madonna's 'Like a Prayer', and worked on film scores. Mercy, released on Quincy Jones's Qwest label, was his return as a recording artist, and it was received as the comeback of an elder statesman.","Listen to how the choirs are recorded: multiple choirs overdubbed and panned across the stereo field, so that a single entry sounds like a cathedral full of singers. Crouch had learned this layering partly in pop sessions, where he stacked his choir behind superstars, and here he turns it back on his own songs. The horn writing, too, sounds like his film work, swelling under the voices rather than punching like a soul section.","The album won wide praise in the gospel press and was a Grammy winner, confirming Crouch's position as a patriarch to the generation of Kirk Franklin and the Winans he had helped launch. Some critics found its production glossy compared with the raw Disciples records. Its standing is respectable rather than legendary; it matters most as the bridge from Crouch's seventies innovations to the big-budget gospel of the late nineties.","Start with a ballad and listen to how long Crouch waits before the full choir enters. Notice the horns behind the voices. Then play Madonna's 'Like a Prayer' and Michael Jackson's Bad album, where his choir work is heard in pop, and Kirk Franklin's Nu Nation Project to hear who inherited the sound."],"shirley-caesar-no-charge":["A spoken and sung story about a child who hands his mother a bill for his chores and receives in reply a list of everything she has done for him, free. Shirley Caesar begins almost conversationally, half talking over a slow piano and organ groove, acting out both voices, then breaks into song as the story turns toward its moral about a mother's love and, by extension, God's. Her voice is sharp, flexible and full of grit, shifting from comedy to tears within a few bars.","The song was written by the country songwriter Harlan Howard and had been a country hit for Melba Montgomery in 1974. Caesar, by then a solo star after years with the Caravans, recognised in it the shape of the sermonette, the short spoken story she had made her signature, and recorded it for Hob, a New York gospel label. Her version took a Nashville tearjerker into Black church repertoire, where it has stayed ever since.","Listen to how Caesar adds to the written song. She expands the spoken sections, inserts asides and lets the band vamp while she tells the story, so a three-minute country song becomes a six-minute performance. That habit comes straight from the Pentecostal preaching she grew up hearing in Durham, where a speaker builds rhythm in speech until it tips into singing. The music swells exactly when her speech starts to swing.","Her recording became one of the best-known gospel performances of the seventies and a staple of her concerts, though it was often treated as a novelty outside the church. It is now a favourite example of the long traffic between Black gospel and white country music, which share hymns, themes and audiences more than their marketing ever admitted. Caesar's sermonette style has since been studied as an art form in its own right.","Listen first to Melba Montgomery's country version, then to Caesar's, and notice what she adds: the acted voices, the pauses, the moment speech turns into song. Then hear Rev. C. L. Franklin's 'The Eagle Stirreth Her Nest' to hear the preaching tradition her sermonettes come from."],"shirley-caesar-hold-my-mule":["A live sermon-song about an old farmer whose church tells him to stop shouting during services, and who decides to praise God his own way anyway. Shirley Caesar tells the story in a rising preacher's cadence while the band answers her phrases, the choir waits, and the crowd laughs and shouts. As the story reaches the farmer's defiant joy, the tempo jumps, the organ starts to roar and the whole performance turns into a holy dance, voices and drums racing together.","Recorded live in Chicago with the Thompson Community Singers, the performance shows Caesar at the height of her powers as a stage preacher, two decades into her solo career and by then also an ordained minister and Durham city councillor. In 2016 a short passage from it, a spoken list of foods, was remixed into a dance track and became a viral video challenge, bringing a gospel sermon from 1988 to millions of teenagers who had no idea where it came from.","Listen to the organist, who never plays a written part but follows Caesar's speech word by word, punching chords at the ends of her phrases. This is the Pentecostal practice of 'tuning up' a sermon, in which the preacher's spoken rhythm gradually becomes musical pitch and the band locks onto it. The jump into the fast shout section happens on her cue, not the band's, and you can hear them scramble joyously to follow.","Inside the church the performance was admired as a model of the sung sermon, and Caesar's comic timing compared to the great preachers. The 2016 viral remix sparked a small debate: some saw sacred material stripped of its meaning, while Caesar herself embraced it, appearing in videos and welcoming the attention. Scholars now cite the episode as a case study in how internet culture recirculates older Black vernacular performance.","Hear the whole performance, not just the famous excerpt, and listen for the point where speech turns to song and then to dance. Follow the organ. Then compare Rev. C. L. Franklin's recorded sermons and Aretha's Amazing Grace, which grew from the same practice, to hear how preaching and singing are one art in this tradition."],"clark-sisters-is-my-living-in-vain":["A slow, searching song in which a believer asks whether her life of faith has been worth it and answers that it has. The four Clark sisters sing in close, sliding harmony over Hammond organ and a patient rhythm section, trading leads and decorating each line with fast runs and bends. The opening is hushed and almost bluesy; the end piles the voices into a dense, shimmering chord and a long vamp of runs that seem to tumble over each other.","The Clark Sisters were the daughters of Mattie Moss Clark, the formidable choir director of the Church of God in Christ in Detroit, who trained them from childhood. This song was written by Elbernita 'Twinkie' Clark, the group's organist and chief writer, for the album of the same name on Sound of Gospel, a small Detroit label. It became one of their signature songs and a standard in Pentecostal churches.","Listen to the harmonies, which use clusters of close notes that classical choirs would call dissonant and that the sisters bend in pitch as they hold them. That sound, along with their lightning-fast vocal runs, became the template for a generation of R&B singers: much of what is heard in nineties and later melisma, from Mariah Carey to Beyoncé, traces back through the Clarks. Twinkie's Hammond comments on every line like a fifth voice.","The song established the Clarks as the most influential female group in modern gospel, and its vocal style was studied and imitated by secular singers who rarely credited it. For years the sisters' recognition lagged their influence, partly because their records were on small labels. Critics and singers now place them at the root of contemporary vocal technique, and the song is a test piece for gospel and R&B vocalists.","Listen for the moment the four voices lock into their final chord, and notice how they bend it rather than holding it still. Then hear their 'You Brought the Sunshine' for the dance-floor side of the group, and Beyoncé's records to hear where the vocal runs went."],"clark-sisters-you-brought-the-sunshine":["A bright, up-tempo song of thanksgiving built on a funk groove: a slapping bass line, crisp drums, a chirping synthesiser and Twinkie Clark's Hammond organ. The sisters sing in tight unison and harmony, trading call-and-response lines, and the chorus repeats like a dance-floor hook. Halfway through the song breaks down to bass, drums and handclaps before the voices return in a stacked, ecstatic vamp. It sounds like a disco or post-disco record that happens to be about God.","The Clark Sisters recorded the song for a 1981 album on Sound of Gospel in Detroit, the city of Motown, P-Funk and, by then, early electronic dance music. It crossed out of the church in an unusual way: DJs in New York clubs began playing it, and it reached R&B radio, making the Clarks one of the few gospel acts of the era to be heard in secular nightlife. For a group from a strict Pentecostal home, that was a remarkable journey for a single song to make.","Listen to the synthesiser line, which Twinkie Clark plays like a Stevie Wonder keyboard figure, and to the breakdown, which sounds designed for a DJ to mix even though it was made for a church audience. The vocal hook is sung in a deliberately simple unison so that the complex harmonies around it stand out. The song's structure, intro groove, verses, breakdown, rebuild, is essentially that of a twelve-inch dance mix.","Some in the church were uneasy at hearing a gospel song in nightclubs; most were delighted at the reach. For dance-music historians the record is part of the gospel undercurrent in New York club culture, where DJs like Larry Levan treated soaring church voices as the music's spiritual core. It is now among the best-loved gospel recordings of the eighties, constantly covered by choirs.","Hear it loud, and listen for the breakdown where everything drops out but bass and drums. Notice the synthesiser. Then play Larry Levan's Paradise Garage recordings and Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life to hear the club and soul sounds it shared a city's air with."],"mighty-clouds-of-joy-mighty-high":["A gospel quartet singing over a full disco production: four-on-the-floor drums, swirling strings, horns and wah-wah guitar. The song is about the joy of faith lifting a person higher than anything else could, and the Mighty Clouds sing it with the rough, shouting lead and stacked harmonies of the quartet tradition, Joe Ligon's gravelly voice cutting through the polished arrangement. The contrast is the point: a sound from the Pentecostal church dropped into the clean, glossy groove of mid-seventies dance music.","The Mighty Clouds of Joy had formed in Los Angeles in the mid-fifties and were among the leading gospel quartets of the sixties. In the seventies they signed with ABC and worked with soul producers to reach wider audiences, and 'Mighty High' became their biggest crossover, played on R&B radio and in discos. Their records reached listeners who had never set foot in a Pentecostal church, and the group kept performing for those new audiences while still singing in churches.","Listen past the strings to the vocal arrangement, which is pure quartet tradition: one lead voice preaching against a group that answers in tight blocks, with the bass voice anchoring the bottom. That call-and-response structure is exactly what disco borrowed from gospel in the first place, so the record is less a betrayal than a homecoming. The production simply makes explicit how close the two musics already were.","The record split opinion. Many gospel listeners thought it worldly, and some radio stations hesitated to play it; disco fans heard it as just another good dance record. Later historians of disco, tracing the genre's roots in Black church music, have treated it as a revealing document, a moment when a gospel group walked through the door disco had opened. The group continued in traditional style for decades afterward.","Notice the lead voice, still the rough quartet shout, against the smooth strings. Then hear the Swan Silvertones' 'Mary Don't You Weep' to hear the quartet style unadorned, and Donna Summer's 'Love to Love You Baby' to hear the disco production at its purest."],"the-winans-introducing-the-winans":["The debut of four Detroit brothers, Marvin, Carvin, Michael and Ronald Winans, singing in smooth, close harmony over keyboards, guitar and a light soul rhythm section. The sound is closer to the Commodores or the Spinners than to the old gospel quartets: sweet tenor leads, falsetto flourishes, gentle grooves and ballads that could sit on quiet storm radio. The songs keep a clear gospel message, but the textures are those of early eighties R&B.","The brothers came from a large, devout family in Detroit; their parents and younger siblings, including BeBe and CeCe, would all record. Andraé Crouch heard them and brought them to Light Records, producing this first album. It arrived at a moment when gospel and R&B were drifting apart commercially, and the Winans set out to close the gap, making church music that sounded like the soul records young people actually played.","Listen to the stacked harmonies. The four brothers sing in voicings borrowed from jazz and soul vocal groups, with the top part often in falsetto, and they use the slick, precise blend of studio R&B rather than the rough edges of the quartet tradition. Crouch's production keeps the band understated so that the voices carry everything, which is why the album still sounds intimate rather than dated.","Traditional listeners heard too much R&B; younger listeners heard their own music speaking about faith. The album launched a family dynasty that would dominate contemporary gospel for twenty years. Critics now place it at the start of the urban contemporary gospel sound, the bridge between Crouch's seventies innovations and the full R&B production of the late eighties and nineties.","Listen to the harmonies on the ballads and compare them with Boyz II Men's Cooleyhighharmony a decade later; the family resemblance is direct. Then hear the Winans with Anita Baker on 'Ain't No Need to Worry' to hear how far they took the sound."],"the-winans-aint-no-need-to-worry":["A soulful, mid-tempo song of reassurance that God will carry a believer through trouble, sung by the Winans with Anita Baker as guest lead. The groove is slow and warm, with electric piano, fat bass and a quiet backbeat, and Baker's low, smoky voice sits over the brothers' high harmonies. Each side takes verses; together they build to a final section where her improvisations and their harmonies wind around each other in a long fade.","By 1987 the Winans had moved to Quincy Jones's Qwest label and were making records with full R&B production. Anita Baker had just become one of the biggest soul singers in America with Rapture. Both came from Detroit churches, and the collaboration, on the Winans' album Decisions, was a public declaration that the line between gospel and R&B was thinner than the industry pretended.","Notice how the arrangement leaves gaps for Baker to fill. She sings slightly behind the beat, as in her own ballads, while the brothers sing on it, so the song has two time-feels at once. The keyboard harmonies are the extended chords of quiet storm radio, and nothing in the instrumentation signals church; only the subject does. That restraint is what let it be played on secular stations.","The record won wide praise and awards, and it is often cited as one of the most successful gospel and R&B collaborations of the decade. A few church voices objected to the soul production, as they had to everything the Winans did. Today it is remembered as the moment urban contemporary gospel fully arrived, polished, adult and radio-ready.","Listen to how Baker and the brothers handle the beat differently. Then play Anita Baker's Rapture to hear her own world, and the Winans' 'It's Time' to hear the group take on new jack swing three years later. Their own debut, Introducing the Winans, shows the starting point."],"the-winans-its-time":["A hard-hitting call to spiritual readiness set to a full new jack swing production: swung drum-machine beats, punchy synthesiser stabs and a bass line built for dancing. The Winans sing in their sweet four-part blend over machinery that sounds like Guy or Bobby Brown, and the chorus is a chant designed to be shouted back. It is gospel sung in the exact musical language of 1990 urban radio.","The track came from the Winans' album Return and was produced with Teddy Riley, the architect of new jack swing, who had built hits for Guy, Bobby Brown and Keith Sweat. Hiring him was a calculated move to reach teenagers who heard gospel as old people's music. It was a bold bet: new jack swing was the sound of street fashion and nightlife, and some churches resisted.","Listen to the drum programming, which uses the swung sixteenth-note shuffle that defined Riley's sound, and notice how the brothers' harmonies sit right on top of it, crisp rather than loose. The production would have fitted unchanged on a Guy album; only the words and the purity of the harmony give the game away, years before gospel and hip-hop production merged fully.","Reaction split along predictable lines: younger listeners and radio embraced it; many church leaders objected that the music was too close to the clubs. In hindsight it is a direct ancestor of Kirk Franklin's 'Stomp', which made the same move with greater success seven years later. Critics now treat it as a key experiment in gospel's long argument about how far to borrow from secular style.","Play it next to Guy's first album and Bobby Brown's Don't Be Cruel to hear how exactly it fits its moment. Then jump forward to Kirk Franklin's 'Stomp' to hear the experiment perfected, and back to the Winans with Anita Baker to hear how smooth the group sounded before Riley arrived."],"bebe-and-cece-winans-heaven":["A sleek, adult R&B album by the youngest Winans siblings, BeBe and CeCe, singing duets of love and faith over keyboards, programmed drums and smooth bass lines. BeBe's warm tenor and CeCe's light, pure soprano blend almost like a single voice, and the songs move from slow ballads to mid-tempo grooves. The production is pristine and late-eighties, close to Whitney Houston or Luther Vandross, with the gospel message carried mostly in the words rather than the sound.","BeBe and CeCe had sung on the Christian television programme The PTL Club before launching as a duo. Heaven, their second album, was released jointly by Sparrow, a Christian label, and Capitol, a mainstream major, a sign of how far the industry thought gospel could now travel. Whitney Houston, a friend, sang on one track, 'Hold Up the Light', underlining the duo's link to the pop world.","Listen to the duet writing. Instead of the usual call-and-response, BeBe and CeCe often sing in close parallel harmony through whole phrases, so the listener hears a single blended line. Their breath, phrasing and vibrato are matched with a precision that suggests years of singing together in a family. The arrangements also use space carefully, leaving whole bars with only keyboard and voice.","The album was a major success on both gospel and R&B charts and established the duo as stars beyond the church. Some gospel critics found it too smooth and too secular in sound. Others argued it was simply the next step in what their brothers had started. It remains one of the defining crossover gospel records of the late eighties.","Notice how the two voices blend in parallel harmony. Listen for Whitney Houston on 'Hold Up the Light'. Then play Whitney Houston's debut album to hear the pop world it borrowed from, and CeCe's Alone in His Presence for her later, plainer worship style."],"cece-winans-alone-in-his-presence":["A quiet album of old hymns and new worship songs, sung by CeCe Winans with piano, soft strings and a minimal rhythm section. After years of glossy R&B duets, the sound here is stripped down and intimate: her light, clear voice sings close to the microphone, with little ornament, and the arrangements build slowly from solo piano. Familiar hymns sit beside contemporary praise songs, all treated in the same unhurried, prayerful manner.","This was CeCe Winans's first solo album after years in the duo with her brother BeBe. Rather than chase a pop crossover, she chose hymns and worship songs, part of a broader turn in nineties Christian music toward praise and worship repertoire suited to congregations. The album was released on Sparrow, a Christian label, and became a major seller and a Grammy winner.","Listen to how much space the arrangements leave. Often the piano plays a single sustained chord while Winans sings a whole phrase, and the strings enter so softly you notice them only when they stop. Her phrasing on the old hymns keeps the gospel habit of bending notes and slightly delaying the melody, but she holds back the big runs, saving them for a few climactic moments where they land with great effect.","The album was praised for its restraint and devotional seriousness and helped launch the praise and worship boom in Black gospel. A few critics wanted more of the R&B energy of her duo records. Its influence has been long: it is often cited as a model for intimate worship albums, and many of its arrangements are sung in churches today.","Hear it in a quiet room and listen for the moments when Winans finally lets loose. Notice how the strings support rather than lead. Then compare BeBe & CeCe's Heaven to hear how much she pared back, and Richard Smallwood's 'Total Praise' for a choir hymn in the same devotional spirit."],"cece-winans-believe-for-it":["A live worship song about trusting God for what has not yet happened, sung by CeCe Winans with a congregation and band. It opens with soft keyboards and a simple, repeating melody, then builds over seven minutes through a series of rising choruses until the band is at full volume and the crowd is singing with her. The style is modern worship music, the anthemic, slowly rising form of contemporary church song, coloured by Winans's gospel phrasing.","Recorded live at a church in Nashville, where Winans and her husband lead a congregation, the song appeared on an album of the same name. Gospel had by then merged with the global contemporary worship movement, which uses long, building songs designed for congregations to sing together. The release reached gospel and Christian audiences alike and won several Grammys, making Winans one of the most decorated gospel artists in history.","Notice the structure: there is almost no verse-chorus alternation in the old sense, but a long series of repetitions, each slightly louder and higher, with the band adding one element at a time. This is the build of modern worship music, designed to bring a congregation gradually from quiet to full voice. Winans's ornaments on the repeats are what mark it as gospel rather than evangelical worship pop.","The song was a major success in both gospel and contemporary Christian markets, and its awards confirmed Winans as an elder of the tradition four decades into her career. Some gospel critics have worried that the worship style has flattened the distinctive sounds of Black gospel; others hear in Winans's version a successful merger. The debate about gospel's place within broader worship music is very much alive.","Follow the build and notice when each instrument joins. Listen to how Winans decorates the repeats. Then compare her Alone in His Presence, a quieter worship album from 1995, to hear how the form of church song has changed, and Marvin Sapp's 'Never Would Have Made It' for another long live build."],"commissioned-go-tell-somebody":["A Detroit male vocal group singing gospel over the synthesisers and drum machines of mid-eighties R&B. The harmonies are smooth and tight, close to New Edition or the Force MDs, and the grooves are bright and danceable, with synth-bass lines and gated drums. Ballads alternate with up-tempo songs, and the leads are shared among several singers, each with a different colour. The gospel message runs through every song, but the sound belongs to Detroit radio of the time.","Commissioned grew out of the Detroit church scene that also produced the Winans and the Clark Sisters, and it was led by the bassist and singer Fred Hammond, who would become one of the most influential figures in later gospel. The group signed with Light Records and aimed squarely at young listeners, matching the sounds of secular R&B. It became the training ground for several later gospel stars, including Marvin Sapp.","Listen to the bass. Fred Hammond's playing, busy and melodic, is the engine of the group's sound and anticipates the funk-driven style of his later solo work. The harmonies use the close voicing of eighties R&B groups, with a falsetto top line, and the arrangements often drop the instruments out for a few bars of pure voices, a nod to the quartet tradition hidden inside the drum-machine production.","Commissioned were embraced by young church audiences and dismissed by some older ones, much like the Winans. The group's greatest legacy may be its alumni: Fred Hammond and Marvin Sapp both became major solo artists. Critics now see Commissioned as a crucial link in the urban contemporary gospel chain, between the Winans and the praise-and-worship and hip-hop-influenced styles of the nineties.","Listen to the bass lines first, then the harmonies. Compare New Edition's Heart Break to hear the R&B vocal groups they shared a sound with. Then play Fred Hammond's 'No Weapon' and Marvin Sapp's 'Never Would Have Made It' to hear where the group's members went."],"fred-hammond-no-weapon":["A slow, steady song of spiritual protection, sung by Fred Hammond with his choir Radical for Christ. The arrangement is built on a deep, rolling bass line, simple keyboards and drums, with the choir singing a firm, unhurried chorus. Hammond leads in a warm baritone, and the song builds by repetition rather than dramatic leaps, ending in a long chant that a congregation can sing indefinitely. Its quiet confidence is what people remember.","After leaving Commissioned, Fred Hammond formed Radical for Christ, a choir that combined traditional gospel singing with funk, R&B and later hip-hop-influenced grooves. 'No Weapon' appeared on his 1996 album The Spirit of David. The nineties were a period of explosive growth for gospel sales, and Hammond became one of the leading figures of the new praise and worship style, aimed at congregational singing.","Listen to the bass line, which Hammond, a bassist first, makes the centre of the arrangement. It moves in long, smooth phrases that support the slow chant of the choir and gives the song its sense of steady strength. The chorus is set in a narrow range so that everyone can sing it, the same principle Andraé Crouch used decades before. Its plainness is entirely deliberate.","The song became one of the most widely sung praise songs of the nineties, heard in churches across the country. Critics of praise and worship music sometimes found such songs repetitive; Hammond's admirers argued that repetition is the point of congregational song. Hammond himself is now regarded as one of the most influential figures in modern gospel, both as performer and producer.","Follow the bass line all the way through and notice how it holds the song together. Then listen to Commissioned's Go Tell Somebody to hear Hammond's earlier group, and Hezekiah Walker's 'I Need You to Survive' for another congregational song of the same era."],"marvin-sapp-never-would-have-made-it":["A long, slow ballad of thanksgiving for surviving grief and hardship, sung live by Marvin Sapp with choir and band. It begins with soft piano and organ and Sapp's quiet, conversational singing, and builds patiently over eight minutes. His voice moves from tender to raw, and in the final section he improvises over a repeating choir chant, stretching phrases, preaching between lines and pushing into a cracked, powerful upper register as the band rises beneath him.","Sapp, a former member of Commissioned and a pastor in Grand Rapids, Michigan, wrote the song in the period after his father's death, and recorded it live for the album Thirsty. It became one of the biggest gospel hits of the decade, crossing to R&B radio and lifting Sapp to national fame. Its subject of endurance resonated far beyond church audiences during years of economic hardship.","Listen to the final section, where the written song is over and Sapp is improvising. He uses the old gospel technique of 'vamping', repeating a short phrase while the choir holds a chant, and each time he varies the rhythm and pitch, building intensity. The band follows him exactly, dropping out and returning on his cues. It is the same practice heard in James Cleveland's long choir performances, in a twenty-first-century setting.","The song was a huge success and is often named among the defining gospel recordings of the 2000s. It made Sapp one of the best-known gospel singers in the country. Some critics have noted its length and repetitiveness; most hear in its long build exactly the cathartic shape of a Black church service. It is now standard repertoire for gospel soloists.","Hear the full live version, not the radio edit, and wait for the final section where Sapp improvises. Notice how quiet it begins. Then compare James Cleveland's 'I Don't Feel Noways Tired' to hear an earlier generation's version of the same slow build."],"take-6-take-6":["Six male voices and nothing else: no band, no piano, only vocal harmony so dense and agile that listeners often assume instruments are hidden in the mix. One singer imitates a walking bass, the others stack chords drawn from jazz, and the leads trade off in phrases of extraordinary precision. The repertoire mixes spirituals such as 'Get Away, Jordan', new gospel songs like 'Spread Love' and 'A Quiet Place', and a Christmas song, 'Mary'.","The group formed at Oakwood College, a Seventh-day Adventist school in Huntsville, Alabama, where a cappella singing was a strong tradition. Signed to Reprise, a mainstream label, they released this debut to unexpected acclaim from jazz critics and pop audiences as well as churches. It won Grammys in both gospel and jazz-related categories, a rare crossover that announced a new kind of gospel group.","Listen to the chords. Take 6 use the harmonies of big-band jazz arrangers like Gil Evans, with added notes and close clusters, and move them at speed, so that each voice is singing what would normally be a horn line. The bass voice, singing in rhythm with no instrument to lean on, is the foundation of everything. Arranger Mervyn Warren and the group wrote harmonies that most vocal ensembles had thought impossible.","The album was greeted as a revelation, by jazz critics as much as gospel ones, and established Take 6 as the most influential a cappella group of their time. Later a cappella groups, from collegiate ensembles to Pentatonix, owe them a direct debt. Some gospel listeners found them too polished and jazz-inflected; the album nonetheless remains a landmark in vocal ensemble writing.","Listen to one song with your attention only on the bass voice, then again only on the highest. Notice how no instrument is needed. Then compare the Fisk Jubilee Singers' early recordings and the Golden Gate Quartet to hear the older a cappella traditions they extend."],"kirk-franklin-why-we-sing":["A gentle, mid-tempo song about why believers sing, built on a theme taken from the old hymn 'His Eye Is on the Sparrow'. Kirk Franklin's choir, the Family, sings with warmth and polish over piano, organ and an easy rhythm section, and Franklin speaks and directs throughout, adding encouragement between phrases. The song grows from a quiet, almost reflective opening to a full, rich choral climax, but never becomes a shouting song; its tone is tender rather than ecstatic.","Franklin was in his early twenties, a Fort Worth choir director and church musician, when he recorded his debut album with the Family for GospoCentric, a small new label. 'Why We Sing' became its breakout single and carried the album to remarkable sales for a gospel record, crossing to R&B radio. It made Franklin the most visible young figure in gospel almost overnight.","Listen for the old hymn tune. Franklin weaves the melody of 'His Eye Is on the Sparrow', a gospel standard famous through Ethel Waters and Mahalia Jackson, into his own song, so the new piece seems to grow out of the old one. His spoken interjections, a habit that became his trademark, were partly a result of his not being a strong lead singer; he turned that limitation into a style.","The album was a sensation, and the song in particular was praised for its warmth and its respect for tradition. Franklin's speaking over the music was later parodied and criticised, but it became part of his signature. Critics now see the song as the beginning of a career that would redefine gospel's commercial scope in the nineties.","Listen for the hymn melody inside the song. Notice Franklin's voice speaking between the choir's lines. Then compare Mahalia Jackson's recordings of 'His Eye Is on the Sparrow' to hear the original, and Franklin's 'Stomp' to hear how dramatically he changed direction four years later."],"kirk-franklin-stomp":["A gospel choir over a funk sample and a hip-hop beat, a song about the joy of worship that makes you want to dance. The groove is built on a sample from Funkadelic's 'One Nation Under a Groove', with a loud, steady beat and heavy bass. The choir, God's Property, sings bright, shouted hooks, and Franklin calls out and hypes the crowd. The remix adds a rap verse by Cheryl 'Salt' James of Salt-N-Pepa. It sounds like a party record.","God's Property was a young Dallas choir that Franklin worked with, and 'Stomp' came from their 1997 album on B-Rite, distributed by Interscope, a label better known for gangsta rap. The song and its video were played on MTV and urban radio, an extraordinary reach for a gospel record. It came at the height of the hip-hop era, and Franklin argued that gospel had to speak young people's musical language to reach them.","Listen to the sample. Funkadelic's 'One Nation Under a Groove' is itself a song about collective liberation through dancing, so its use under a gospel choir turns George Clinton's secular utopia into a sacred one. The beat is constructed exactly like a West Coast hip-hop production of the time, and the choir is arranged in short, chanted hooks rather than long phrases, so it fits the rhythm like a rapper's chorus.","The record was a major crossover hit and sparked fierce debate. Many church leaders denounced it as worldly; young people and many pastors welcomed it as evangelism. It is now regarded as the record that brought gospel and hip-hop into lasting partnership, opening the way for later artists from Lecrae to Chance the Rapper and Kanye West's gospel work.","Play Funkadelic's 'One Nation Under a Groove' first, then 'Stomp', and hear how the sample changes meaning. Watch the video if you can. Then play the Winans' 'It's Time' for the earlier attempt, and Chance the Rapper's Coloring Book to hear where gospel hip-hop went."],"kirk-franklin-the-nu-nation-project":["A long, ambitious album that mixes choir gospel with R&B, hip-hop, funk and pop, bringing in guest stars and big productions. It includes 'Revolution', a hard-hitting track produced by Rodney Jerkins, and 'Lean on Me', a huge ballad featuring Mary J. Blige, Bono, R. Kelly and Crystal Lewis. Between these are worship songs, testimonies and spoken interludes. The overall sound is glossy, layered and constantly changing, like a mixtape with a church at its centre.","Following the success of 'Stomp', Franklin had become a pop-culture figure, and The Nu Nation Project was his bid to bring gospel fully into the mainstream. It was a major seller and a Grammy winner, released at a time when R&B and hip-hop dominated American charts. The guest list, crossing gospel, R&B, rock and Christian pop, showed how far Franklin's reach extended.","Listen to the interludes between songs, where Franklin speaks about his own struggles and invites listeners into his story. These spoken passages turn the album into something like a sermon broken into chapters. On 'Lean on Me', notice how each guest is given a verse matched to their style, Bono's rock rasp against Mary J. Blige's soul, before the choir unites them all.","The album was enormously successful and praised for its ambition, though some found its mix of styles and guest stars overwhelming or commercial. It confirmed Franklin as the central figure in nineties gospel. Critics now treat it as a high-water mark of the gospel crossover era, before the industry's later fragmentation.","Start with 'Revolution' for the energy and 'Lean on Me' for the guest stars. Then listen to the whole album in order to feel its shape as a kind of extended service. Compare Mary J. Blige's My Life and U2's Achtung Baby to hear the worlds two of the guests came from."],"kirk-franklin-i-smile":["A bright, warm song about choosing joy in hard times, built on a bouncing piano figure, a relaxed groove and a big choir chorus. The verses are sung by a lead vocalist over light keyboards and drums, and the choir enters on the chorus with an uplifting, sunny hook that repeats and rises. Franklin's production is clean and contemporary, balancing old choir harmonies with modern pop-R&B textures. It feels like a deliberately simple, open-hearted song.","'I Smile' came from Franklin's 2011 album Hello Fear, released during a period when gospel was competing with the new worship-music industry and with streaming. The song became one of the biggest gospel hits of its decade and crossed onto R&B radio. Franklin had by then been a gospel star for nearly twenty years, and the song showed his ability to write simple, durable melodies.","Listen to the piano figure that opens the song and returns throughout; it is a short, rhythmic pattern borrowed from pop and R&B piano writing rather than church accompaniment, and it drives the whole arrangement. The choir writing is in close, bright harmony with a lifting melodic shape on the chorus, the same uplift Franklin had used since 'Why We Sing'. The song's plainness masks careful craft.","The song was widely embraced and won awards, becoming one of Franklin's best-known later songs. Some critics found it slight; others praised exactly that simplicity. It is now a common choice for church choirs and for weddings, funerals and celebrations alike, showing the kind of everyday usefulness that Franklin's best songs have.","Listen for the piano figure and how it returns. Notice how the chorus lifts. Then play 'Why We Sing' to hear the young Franklin, and CeCe Winans's 'Believe for It' to hear how gospel's sound continued to change in the decade after this record."],"sweet-honey-in-the-rock-good-news":["Five women singing without instruments except for occasional hand percussion, in deep, earthy harmonies drawn from spirituals, gospel, blues and African song. The voices are strong and individual, with a low bass voice anchoring rich, open chords. The songs range from spirituals and freedom songs to new compositions on social justice, apartheid and the lives of working people. The sound is raw and immediate, more like a church meeting or a protest rally than a polished concert.","Sweet Honey in the Rock was founded in Washington, DC, in 1973 by Bernice Johnson Reagon, who had sung with the SNCC Freedom Singers during the civil rights movement. The group carried that tradition of sung protest into the 1970s and 1980s, linking spirituals, gospel and political song. This album, on the independent folk label Flying Fish, came at a moment of renewed activism around South Africa and nuclear weapons.","Listen to the bass voice, often sung by a woman in an unusually low register, and to the way the singers move between unison and harmony. The arrangements draw on the congregational singing of the Black church in the South, where songs were 'lined out' and harmonised freely, and on African vocal traditions, where voices interlock rhythmically. Hand percussion, when it appears, marks time like feet on a church floor.","The group was widely praised by folk, feminist and political audiences, though gospel radio rarely played it. Critics have long admired the group's blend of beauty and protest. Its influence is felt in later vocal groups and in political choirs around the world. Reagon's work as a scholar of African American song, at the Smithsonian and elsewhere, adds to its cultural weight.","Listen to the bass voice first, then to how the other voices weave around it. Notice the hand percussion. Then compare Pete Seeger's 'We Shall Overcome' and the Fisk Jubilee Singers to hear the protest and spiritual traditions the group draws on."],"sweet-honey-in-the-rock-ellas-song":["A slow, steady song written by Bernice Johnson Reagon in honour of the civil rights organiser Ella Baker, about the long work of seeking justice and freedom. Five unaccompanied voices sing a simple, chant-like melody in unison and harmony, the lead voice rising over a quiet, rocking bed of sustained chords. The pace never hurries. The song repeats its central refrain many times, building quiet intensity, and ends almost as it began, as if the struggle continues.","Ella Baker was a key organiser behind the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, where Reagon had been active in the early 1960s, and was known for her insistence on grassroots, collective leadership rather than charismatic leaders. Reagon wrote the song using ideas from Baker's own speeches, and Sweet Honey recorded it on their 1983 album We All... Everyone of Us. It has since become an anthem for activists.","Listen to how little the melody moves; it is built to be learned in one hearing and sung by a crowd, like the freedom songs of the sixties that Reagon helped lead. The harmonies beneath are sustained and close, giving the impression of a group holding steady together, which mirrors Baker's idea of collective leadership. The repetition is the meaning: change comes through endurance, not a single climax.","The song has become one of the best-known protest songs of its generation, sung at marches and gatherings for decades. Critics and scholars praise it as both beautiful and politically sharp, and it is often taught as an example of the continuity between the civil rights era and later movements. Its reach far outstrips Sweet Honey's commercial profile.","Listen first for the steady pulse, then notice how the harmony thickens as the song goes on. Hear it as a song meant for many voices, not just five. Then compare the Staple Singers' 'Freedom Highway' and Pete Seeger's 'We Shall Overcome' to hear the protest-song lineage it carries forward."],"richard-smallwood-total-praise":["A slow, grand hymn of worship for choir, piano and strings. It begins quietly, with piano and voices in rich, sustained chords, then builds through several sections to a powerful climax and closes with a long, slowly expanding cadence of repeated amens. The harmonies are lush and the melodic lines long and arching, closer to an anthem in the European choral tradition than to a gospel shout. Its dignity and depth make it instantly recognisable.","Richard Smallwood, a classically trained pianist from Washington, DC, studied at Howard University, where he combined Bach, Chopin and Rachmaninoff with the gospel of his childhood church. He recorded 'Total Praise' live with his choir Vision for the album Adoration: Live in Atlanta. It became one of the most widely sung choral works in the Black church and has crossed into concert choir repertoire and even classical programs.","Listen to the final section, where the closing amens unfold in a series of slowly building chords, each a little fuller and higher. This technique of a long, climaxing cadence comes from the European choral tradition, from Handel and the Romantic anthem, and Smallwood places it in a gospel context. The piano writing, with its rolling arpeggios and harmonic richness, betrays his classical training.","The song was quickly adopted by choirs across denominations and is now one of the most performed gospel choral works in the world, sung at funerals, graduations and concerts. Critics and choral directors praise it as a rare bridge between gospel and the classical choral tradition. Some gospel listeners find it more anthem than gospel; most hear in it a fusion of the two.","Listen through to the closing amens and notice how the chords build. Then compare Handel's 'Hallelujah' chorus and Rachmaninoff's All-Night Vigil to hear the choral grandeur Smallwood draws on, and Walter Hawkins's Love Alive for the gospel choir tradition behind him."],"hezekiah-walker-i-need-you-to-survive":["A slow, steady song about how members of a community need one another, sung by Hezekiah Walker's Love Fellowship Crusade Choir. A simple melody is repeated by the choir in warm, full harmony over a gentle piano, organ and rhythm section. The song builds gradually by repetition, the choir swaying and the congregation joining, until the final section where voices swell and the lead singer improvises over the chorus. Its simplicity and unity are its power.","Hezekiah Walker, a pastor and choir director from Brooklyn, recorded the song live for the album Family Affair II: Live at Radio City Music Hall. His mass choir, built from his church community, was one of the most popular in gospel. Released in the year after the attacks of September 11, 2001, its message of mutual dependence perhaps carried special weight in New York.","Listen to how the melody is constructed to be learned instantly by a congregation: a narrow range, repeated phrases, and a steady pulse. The choir harmony is close and warm, with each section holding long notes under the melody, creating a feeling of embrace. The lead improvisation at the end follows the old gospel practice of the vamp, but here it stays gentle, emphasising community rather than individual display.","The song became one of the most popular congregational songs in Black churches in the 2000s and remains a staple. Critics praise its simplicity and its theme of community. Some find it repetitive; most hear in its repetition exactly what makes it work as a shared song. It is often sung as a closing song at services and gatherings.","Listen to how the song builds through repetition. Notice the warmth of the choir's harmony. Then compare Fred Hammond's 'No Weapon' and James Cleveland's 'Peace Be Still' to hear other congregational songs built on patient repetition, and notice how Walker keeps the lead singer modest."],"hezekiah-walker-every-praise":["An energetic praise song about giving all worship to God, built on a driving beat, keyboard stabs and a choir chorus that repeats and builds. The verses are short; the chorus is a chant listing names and attributes of God, sung by the choir in bright, powerful harmony. The song rises in key and intensity through its final section, becoming a full congregational shout. It is designed for dancing and celebration, with a clean, modern production.","The song came from Walker's album Azusa: The Next Generation, named after the Azusa Street Revival of 1906, a founding event of Pentecostalism. Walker, by then a veteran gospel star and pastor, aimed at a younger audience with contemporary production. The song became one of the biggest gospel hits of the 2010s, widely sung in churches and played on radio.","Listen to the key changes in the final section, where the music shifts upward several times, a classic gospel technique for building excitement. The production uses programmed drums and synth sounds from contemporary R&B and pop, but the call-and-response between lead and choir is pure church tradition. The song's naming of God's attributes echoes the practice of litany in many religious traditions.","The song was hugely popular and won awards, and it is now one of the most widely sung praise songs in Black churches. Critics praise its energy and simplicity, though some find it formulaic. It confirmed Walker's ability to adapt to changing styles across three decades and remains a staple of church celebrations.","Listen for the key changes in the final section and how they build excitement. Notice the call-and-response. Then compare Kirk Franklin's 'Stomp' for another danceable praise song, and Walker's 'I Need You to Survive' to hear his gentler, slower side a decade earlier."],"james-taylor-sweet-baby-james":["Eleven short songs, half an hour of music, built around one voice and one acoustic guitar. James Taylor sings low and close, almost conversationally, and fingerpicks patterns that sound simple until you try them. Around him a small band plays with restraint: soft drums, a supple bass, piano, a little pedal steel and fiddle. The title song is a cowboy lullaby in a lilting waltz time; Steamroller is a deadpan blues parody; Fire and Rain sits at the heart of side one.","Taylor's first album had come out in 1968 on the Beatles' Apple label in London and gone almost unnoticed while he struggled with his health. Back in America, producer Peter Asher brought him to Los Angeles late in 1969 and recorded this follow-up quickly and cheaply for Warner Bros. The session players, including drummer Russ Kunkel and Carole King on piano, became the core of the Laurel Canyon sound. Released in early 1970, it caught a public tired of noise and protest.","Listen to how much of the arrangement is left out. Kunkel often plays with brushes or barely at all, and several songs have no drums for long stretches, so the guitar carries the rhythm alone. Taylor's own picking is the real engine: he keeps a steady thumbed bass under moving chords, a trick learned from folk and country players, which lets a solo voice and guitar sound like a full band. Oh! Susanna, the Stephen Foster minstrel tune, is slipped in as a quiet curio.","It made Taylor a star within months and put him on the cover of Time in 1971 as the face of a new, inward-looking songwriting. That success later counted against him: punk-era critics used his name as shorthand for soft, self-absorbed music. Judgment has since settled in his favour, with musicians praising the guitar craft and the plain honesty of the writing, though some still find the mood too even across the whole record.","Start with the title track and notice the way the guitar rocks like a cradle under the melody. Then play Fire and Rain and Country Road back to back to hear the range from grief to restless travel. Compare it with Carole King's Tapestry, made by many of the same hands a year later, and with Nick Drake's quieter English records of the same moment."],"james-taylor-fire-and-rain":["A song about the death of a friend and the singer's own breakdown and recovery, told in three verses with a returning chorus. The sound is spare: Taylor's acoustic guitar rings out open, bright chords, Carole King's piano enters with soft rolled figures, and drums arrive late and gently. There is no big climax. The melody stays in a narrow, speaking range and the chorus simply repeats, which makes the song feel like a person thinking aloud rather than performing.","Taylor wrote it in pieces in 1968 and 1969, partly in London while making his first album and partly during a stay in a psychiatric hospital in Massachusetts. It appeared on Sweet Baby James and was released as a single in 1970, when American radio was still dominated by louder rock. Its success showed record companies that quiet, autobiographical songs could sell, and it opened a door for a whole generation of acoustic writers.","Each verse turns to a different hurt, and the music barely changes to mark it; the sameness is the point, as if the singer is circling a wound. Listen also to the guitar voicings, which keep a high open string ringing above shifting chords, giving the song its pale, unsettled shimmer. The bass, played slow and sliding, moves almost independently of the guitar and supplies most of the sense of motion.","It became one of the most covered songs of its era and a fixture of every list of great singer-songwriter recordings. For years listeners treated it as a love song, and Taylor has spent decades patiently explaining its real subjects in interviews. Some critics have found its calm too composed for such painful material; others hear that composure as exactly what makes it honest.","Listen once just for the guitar and piano and how they answer each other without crowding. Then follow the chorus each time it returns and notice how the same words seem to weigh more as the song goes on. Hear it next to Joni Mitchell's Blue, made a year later, which takes the confessional mode further and rawer."],"jackson-browne-late-for-the-sky":["Eight long songs, most of them slow, with Jackson Browne's piano at the centre and David Lindley's slide guitar and fiddle weaving keening lines around his voice. The songs run five or six minutes and unfold patiently, more like spoken letters than pop singles. The title track opens on a broken relationship, For a Dancer meditates on a death, and the closing Before the Deluge widens out into a hymn about a generation's lost ideals. The mood is twilight from first track to last.","By 1974 Browne was the house poet of David Geffen's Asylum label in Los Angeles, already known for songs others had recorded and for co-writing Take It Easy with Glenn Frey of the Eagles. He made this third album with his touring band rather than session stars, which gives it a lived-in, unified sound. The cover, a car parked under a night street lamp beneath a bright daytime sky, borrows the paradox of René Magritte's painting The Empire of Light.","Lindley's lap steel is the record's second voice: listen to how often it finishes Browne's phrases, sliding up to a note just after he leaves it. The album is also sequenced as a single arc, from personal loss at the start to collective loss at the end, so that Before the Deluge reads as the answer to the title song. Martin Scorsese used the title track in Taxi Driver two years later.","Reviewers took it as Browne's most complete statement, and many still rank it as his finest record. The objection, then and since, is that it is earnest and humourless, the sound of a young man taking himself very seriously. Defenders reply that its seriousness is the point and that few records of the decade examine love and mortality so steadily. Its reputation has grown among younger songwriters.","Begin with For a Dancer and listen to how the fiddle and piano carry the grief while the voice stays plain. Then hear the title track and the closer together as bookends. Compare with Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark, released the same year in the same city, which handles similar subjects with more wit and jazz colour."],"jackson-browne-the-pretender":["A fuller, more polished record than its predecessor, with strings, harp and a bigger rock band behind Browne's piano and guitar. Eight songs move from the anxious opener The Fuse through tender ballads to the title song, a long, building portrait of a man settling for a comfortable, compromised life. Linda Paloma brings in a mariachi ensemble with harp and guitarrón; Here Come Those Tears Again is the nearest thing to a hit; Daddy's Tune is a reconciliation with a father.","Browne began the album in a period of personal crisis: his wife, Phyllis Major, died in March 1976, while it was being made, and he finished it in grief. He brought in Jon Landau, Bruce Springsteen's producer and former critic, to give the sessions a firmer shape. Released late that year on Asylum, it arrived as the Los Angeles soft-rock scene was at its commercial peak and just as punk was beginning to sneer at it.","Here Come Those Tears Again was co-written with Nancy Farnsworth, Phyllis Major's mother, a remarkable fact once you hear the song's restraint. Listen to the title track's slow build: it begins almost conversationally and adds instruments verse by verse until the final choruses swell with the full band, so the music enacts the drift into resignation the song describes. Linda Paloma is the first time Browne turned to the Mexican music of his Los Angeles childhood.","It sold better than anything Browne had made and was widely praised, though some reviewers felt Landau's polish smoothed away the intimacy of Late for the Sky. The title song has become one of the decade's emblematic portraits of middle-class compromise, discussed alongside Springsteen's writing. The argument over which of the two albums is Browne's peak has never been settled and probably never will be.","Go straight to the title track and let it build over its six minutes; then go back to Sleep's Dark and Silent Gate, the starkest song here. Notice how differently strings and lap steel are used than on Late for the Sky. Pair it with Springsteen's Born to Run for two views of American aspiration from opposite coasts."],"linda-ronstadt-heart-like-a-wheel":["Ten songs by other writers, sung by one of the most powerful voices in popular music. The record swings between rock and country: You're No Good builds from a slinky groove to a guitar-heavy climax; the Everly Brothers' When Will I Be Loved is bright and twangy; Anna McGarrigle's title song is a hushed ballad with strings; Hank Williams's I Can't Help It is sung in close harmony with Emmylou Harris. Ronstadt's voice can go from a near whisper to full chest power within a phrase.","Ronstadt had spent years in Los Angeles moving between labels, bands and producers without a hit album. For this one Peter Asher, the producer who had shaped James Taylor's sound, took charge, with multi-instrumentalist Andrew Gold playing much of the backing. It was her last album for Capitol before she moved to Asylum, and its success made her one of the best-selling female singers of the decade and the chief interpreter of the new Los Angeles songwriters.","Ronstadt did not write songs, and that is the hidden art here: she was a brilliant chooser and arranger of other people's material. Listen to how You're No Good, an early-1960s soul song, is slowed and stretched until it becomes something closer to a rock epic, with a long guitar coda layered by Gold. Her harmony with Harris on the Hank Williams song is a preview of the Trio records the two would make with Dolly Parton.","It was a commercial breakthrough and a critical success, and it secured her reputation as an interpreter rather than an author, which some rock critics held against her at the time. That bias has faded: she is now widely credited with bringing country material into the rock mainstream and with launching the careers of writers she covered. Some listeners still find Asher's production too clean for the rawness of her voice.","Start with the title song to hear the voice at its quietest, then jump to You're No Good to hear it at full force. Listen for the moment in the Hank Williams song where the two voices lock into a single sound. Then hear Emmylou Harris's Pieces of the Sky, made the following year, as its close cousin."],"linda-ronstadt-simple-dreams":["Ten songs that range from hard rock to old ballads. It's So Easy, a Buddy Holly rocker, is driven by crunching guitars; Roy Orbison's Blue Bayou is slowed to a swaying dream; Warren Zevon's Poor Poor Pitiful Me and Carmelita bring a sardonic edge; the Rolling Stones' Tumbling Dice is reborn as a rock shuffle. Between them, the traditional I Never Will Marry is sung with Dolly Parton over a sparse acoustic accompaniment. The record is short, punchy and confident.","By 1977 Ronstadt was among the biggest concert draws in America, now on Asylum and still working with producer Peter Asher and a crack Los Angeles band including guitarist Waddy Wachtel. The record arrived in the year of punk in London and disco in New York, and it represents the commercial peak of the polished West Coast sound. Her championing of Zevon, then little known, helped his career greatly.","Listen to the gap between the rock songs and the folk song in the middle: I Never Will Marry is almost unaccompanied, and Ronstadt drops all the power of her voice to blend with Parton's high, bright tone. Blue Bayou is taken a little slower than Orbison's original, and its final notes are held so long they almost become a call. Listen too for the way Tumbling Dice is tightened into a straight, hard rock song, trading the Stones' loose swagger for drive.","It was enormously successful, and most reviewers admired the singing while some complained about the predictability of the Asher formula. Later critics of the West Coast scene used records like this as evidence of its over-produced gloss. Its defenders point out that Ronstadt had chosen boldly, putting an unknown songwriter, a Buddy Holly song and an Appalachian ballad on one of the decade's biggest records.","Play Blue Bayou and Orbison's original back to back to hear her slower, more sorrowful approach. Then hear It's So Easy next to Buddy Holly's own rockers. End with I Never Will Marry, which shows where Ronstadt's later work with Parton and Emmylou Harris would go."],"carly-simon-no-secrets":["Carly Simon's third album pairs her piano and warm, slightly husky alto with a glossy rock band and lush strings. Ten songs move between confident mid-tempo rockers and ballads. You're So Vain, a song mocking a self-absorbed former lover, opens with a rippling bass and builds into a sing-along chorus with a second male voice in the background. The Right Thing to Do is a tender love song; Embrace Me, You Child looks back on childhood.","Simon had been one of the Simon Sisters folk duo and was now an Elektra solo star after two successful albums. For this one the label sent her to London with producer Richard Perry, who favoured big, bright, radio-ready sound. She married James Taylor late in 1972, around the time of the album's release, and the pair became the public face of the singer-songwriter era. You're So Vain dominated radio that winter.","Listen in the choruses of You're So Vain for the second voice shadowing hers: it is Mick Jagger, uncredited, who happened to be in the studio. The opening bass figure was played by Klaus Voormann, the German artist who designed the Beatles' Revolver cover. The identity of the song's subject became a decades-long guessing game; Simon revealed in 2015 that at least one verse concerned the actor Warren Beatty.","It was Simon's biggest commercial success, and it established her as more than a folk-pop balladeer. Critics have long regarded it as a model of early-1970s studio craft, though some felt Perry's gloss blunted the sharper edges of her writing. You're So Vain is now valued as one of the few hits of its time in which a woman turns a cool, mocking eye on a man rather than pining for him.","Begin with You're So Vain and listen for the bass at the start and the second voice in the chorus. Then play The Right Thing to Do to hear the gentler side of the same record. Compare its production with Carole King's Tapestry for two very different approaches to the piano-led singer-songwriter album."],"carly-simon-thats-the-way-ive-always-heard-it-should-be":["A song about a young woman's dread at being expected to marry, as she looks at the quiet unhappiness of her parents' and friends' marriages. The arrangement is muted and minor-tinged, with piano and acoustic guitar, a soft bass and a string section that swells gradually. Simon sings in a low, controlled voice, almost reporting rather than complaining. There is no conventional chorus; the tension simply builds through the verses until the final resigned turn.","This was the first single from Simon's 1971 debut album on Elektra, co-written with her friend Jacob Brackman, a journalist and screenwriter. It appeared at the height of the American women's movement, and its frank ambivalence about marriage was unusual on pop radio. It became an unexpected hit and launched a solo career that would carry her through the decade.","Listen to how the strings creep in: at first they are barely there, then they rise under each verse, so the arrangement grows heavier just as the singer's sense of being trapped deepens. The final verse brings a sudden shift in perspective that reverses everything before it, and Simon sings it in the same calm tone, which makes the surrender more chilling than any outburst could.","Reviewers were struck by its unusual subject and bleak mood, and it won Simon a Grammy for best new artist. It has since been taken up by feminist critics as one of the first mainstream pop songs to question marriage from a woman's point of view. Some find it too subdued to make its case; most hear its restraint as its strength.","Listen first to the strings and how slowly they rise. Then follow the story through each verse until the final turn. Hear it next to Joni Mitchell's songs on Blue and Carole King's Tapestry for three different women's views of love in 1971."],"cat-stevens-tea-for-the-tillerman":["Eleven songs built on two strummed acoustic guitars, Cat Stevens's own and Alun Davies's, with a lean bass, sparse drums and occasional strings. The guitar playing is percussive and urgent, full of sudden stops and accelerations. Where Do the Children Play? opens with a question about progress; Wild World is a warning to a departing lover; Father and Son is a dialogue in which Stevens sings both parts, the father low and calm, the son high and impatient.","Stevens had been a teenage pop star in London in 1967 before a severe illness with tuberculosis in 1968 forced a long convalescence. He returned with a new, quieter, introspective style. Paul Samwell-Smith, formerly of the Yardbirds, produced this album for Island in Britain and A&M in America. It caught the same wave as James Taylor in America, and Stevens painted the cover himself.","Listen to Father and Son for the voice change: Stevens drops into a low, mild register for the father and leaps an octave for the son, so the argument is carried by pitch as much as words. The guitars, too, change character between the two voices, calmer under the father and more agitated under the son. Del Newman's string arrangements are used sparingly, so their arrival in Sad Lisa and Into White feels like weather changing.","It was a major success on both sides of the Atlantic and remains Stevens's best-known album. Critics praised its craft but some found its spiritual searching naive. Those reservations have softened, especially after Stevens's later conversion to Islam gave the album's questions an unexpected outcome. Wild World has been read by some as condescending to the woman it addresses; the debate about it continues.","Start with Father and Son and listen for the two voices. Then hear Where Do the Children Play? for its insistent guitar rhythm. Compare with James Taylor's Sweet Baby James, from the same year, which shares the acoustic intimacy but none of the urgency."],"cat-stevens-teaser-and-the-firecat":["Ten songs that are lighter and more playful than its predecessor, still built on two acoustic guitars but with more colour: a Greek bouzouki in Rubylove, a gospel-style choir in Peace Train, a simple piano in Morning Has Broken. Moonshadow is a jaunty tune about finding comfort in loss. The record moves from childlike charm to quiet spiritual longing, ending with the yearning Bitterblue and the hopeful Peace Train.","Made quickly after the success of Tea for the Tillerman, again with Paul Samwell-Smith producing and Alun Davies on second guitar. Stevens also wrote and illustrated a children's book with the same title, about a boy and a cat who try to return the moon to the sky. Morning Has Broken is a Christian hymn with words by Eleanor Farjeon, set to an old Gaelic tune; its success made Stevens briefly a hymn singer on pop radio.","The piano on Morning Has Broken was played by Rick Wakeman, soon to join the progressive rock band Yes; he improvised the introduction and the interludes, turning a plain hymn into a small piano piece. Listen to Rubylove, sung partly in Greek with bouzouki, a nod to Stevens's Greek Cypriot father. The record's light touch masks careful arranging: notice how instruments are added one at a time through Peace Train.","It was another big success, and Peace Train and Moonshadow became staples of the era. Critics admired its charm while finding it less substantial than Tea for the Tillerman. Over time Morning Has Broken has become the song most associated with Stevens, often sung in schools. Peace Train has acquired new resonance as a statement of the faith he later chose.","Start with Morning Has Broken and listen for Wakeman's piano. Then play Moonshadow and Peace Train to hear the album's lightness. Compare with Nick Drake's Pink Moon for a darker, more inward record from the same British scene. Any standard remaster serves; the original vinyl sounds especially warm."],"gordon-lightfoot-if-you-could-read-my-mind":["A song about the slow end of a marriage, told through images of ghosts, old movies and paperback novels. Gordon Lightfoot's warm baritone sits over a fingerpicked twelve-string and six-string guitar pattern that rolls steadily like a turning wheel. A string section enters softly and lifts the later verses without ever overwhelming the voice. The melody rises and falls in long phrases, and the song has no chorus in the usual sense, only a returning thought.","Lightfoot was already a respected Canadian folk songwriter whose songs had been recorded by Ian and Sylvia, Peter, Paul and Mary and others. This came from his first album for Reprise, recorded in Los Angeles in 1970 and later retitled after the song. It was his breakthrough as a performer in the United States, and it came out of the breakup of his first marriage.","Listen to the guitar pattern underneath: two guitars weave a single shimmering figure that never stops, like the song's circling thoughts. The strings enter almost unnoticed and slowly thicken, so by the last verse the song feels fuller without ever getting louder. The narrator's imagery comes from cinema and pulp fiction, a deliberate distancing from the real pain at the song's heart.","It became Lightfoot's first big American hit and remains one of his best-loved songs. Critics praised its craft and restraint. It has been covered by many singers across folk, country and pop. Some listeners find its mood too gentle for its subject; others hear in that gentleness a rare honesty about a marriage that ended without villains.","Listen first to the guitars and how they never stop moving. Then follow the strings as they rise. Hear it next to Joni Mitchell's Blue, another Canadian's view of love's end from the following year. Seek the original 1970 studio recording rather than later live versions, where the strings and the careful balance of the two guitars are clearest."],"gordon-lightfoot-wreck-of-the-edmund-fitzgerald":["A ballad about the sinking of a Great Lakes freighter in a November storm. Over six and a half minutes, Lightfoot tells the story in many verses set to a single repeating, modal melody, with no chorus. An electric guitar plays a short, mournful riff between verses, and a pedal steel moans in the background like wind. The drums and bass keep a steady, rolling pulse that suggests waves. The effect is of an old sea ballad recorded with a modern band.","The Edmund Fitzgerald, an ore carrier, sank on Lake Superior on 10 November 1975 with all twenty-nine crew. Lightfoot read about it in a magazine and wrote the song within weeks; it appeared on his 1976 album Summertime Dream on Reprise. Lightfoot said the melody came from an old Irish dirge. The single became one of his biggest hits, unusual for a long story-song with no chorus.","Listen to the guitar riff between verses: the same brief phrase recurs like a ship's bell, marking the passage of time. Lightfoot later changed a line in live performance after a 2010 documentary suggested the sinking was not caused by crew error, as an early theory had suggested. The Mariners' Church in Detroit, mentioned in the song, still rings its bell twenty-nine times each year in the crew's memory.","It became a hit and has remained the best-known modern maritime ballad. Critics have praised its fidelity to the old ballad tradition, in which a real disaster is turned into song soon after the event. Some find its length and repetition hard going; others hear in that repetition the dirge-like solemnity that gives it power.","Listen for the guitar riff between verses and the pedal steel in the background. Follow the story as a ballad, not a pop song. Hear it alongside Woody Guthrie's Deportee for another modern ballad about a real disaster. Notice how little the melody changes, and let that sameness work like a dirge."],"harry-nilsson-nilsson-schmilsson":["Ten songs that range from rollicking piano rock to orchestral ballad to novelty. Gotta Get Up opens with a bouncy piano vamp; Without You, a Badfinger song, is a towering ballad sung with full-throated anguish over strings; Coconut is built on a single chord and a deadpan comic story; Jump into the Fire is a long, hard-driving rock workout. Nilsson's voice, with a range of several octaves, is the album's main instrument.","Nilsson was a Los Angeles songwriter and singer admired by the Beatles but with little commercial success of his own. He recorded this in London with producer Richard Perry, who pushed him toward a harder, more commercial sound. Without You, originally recorded by the British band Badfinger, became a huge international hit and won Nilsson a Grammy for best male pop vocal.","Listen to Jump into the Fire near its end: the bassist, Herbie Flowers, detunes his strings while playing, so the bass line slides downward in pitch, a startling, comic effect. Coconut uses just one chord for its whole length, its humour and drama carried entirely by different voices Nilsson invents for each character. Without You itself had been a little-noticed album track for Badfinger until Nilsson and Perry rebuilt it around piano, strings and a final soaring chorus.","It was Nilsson's biggest commercial success and a critical favourite. Its blend of rock, balladry and humour baffled some reviewers, who found it uneven. Later critics have come to admire precisely that variety. Without You is now so well known through later versions that many forget it was Nilsson's recording that made it a standard.","Start with Without You for the voice at full power, then play Coconut for its humour, then Jump into the Fire for its rock energy. Listen for the bass detuning near the end. Then hear Nilsson Sings Newman for his gentler, more intimate side."],"harry-nilsson-nilsson-sings-newman":["Ten songs by Randy Newman, with Newman himself at the piano and Nilsson's voice multitracked into choirs of harmony. The record is short, intimate and odd: Vine St. opens with a fragment of a fake pop record before shifting into a quiet piano song; Yellow Man is a jaunty satire; Caroline is a bittersweet waltz; Dayton, Ohio 1903 is a nostalgic vignette. Nilsson's layered vocals turn the songs into miniature chamber pieces.","Nilsson had been championing Newman's songs since the late 1960s, when Newman was a struggling songwriter with one poorly selling album. They recorded this in Los Angeles in 1969, with Nilsson building his vocal parts from many separate takes. It sold poorly but helped draw attention to Newman, whose own career took off soon after.","Listen to how the vocals are built: Nilsson stacks his own voice in harmonies, echoes and counterpoints, so a single singer becomes a small chorus. He reportedly spliced together many takes for each song, an elaborate technique for its time. Newman's piano is understated, leaving space for the voices. The album's ten songs are arranged to flow almost without pause, like a song cycle.","It was a commercial failure but was named record of the year by Stereo Review. Critics have long admired it as a pioneering vocal production and as an early showcase for Newman's songs. Some find it too slight; others hear it as one of the most inventive records of its time, a precursor to later multitrack vocal experiments.","Listen with headphones to hear the layers of Nilsson's voice. Start with Vine St. and Caroline. Then hear Newman's own Sail Away and compare his gruff voice with Nilsson's angelic one on the same kind of song. A quiet room and full attention reward this record more than most."],"laura-nyro-eli-and-the-thirteenth-confession":["Thirteen songs that switch tempo, key and mood within a single number, with Laura Nyro's piano and soaring, gospel-inflected voice at the centre. The arrangements mix soul horns, jazz chords, Broadway strings and doo-wop backing vocals. Luckie opens in buoyant gospel style; Lonely Women is a slow, bluesy lament; Eli's Comin' builds from a whispered warning to a full-throated rave-up; Stoned Soul Picnic is a joyous, swirling invitation. The whole record moves like a single flow of feeling.","Nyro was just twenty when she made this, her second album and first for Columbia, in New York with arranger Charlie Calello. She had been booed, or believed she had been, at the Monterey Pop Festival in 1967, and this record was a defiant leap forward. Her songs were already hits for other artists: the 5th Dimension recorded Stoned Soul Picnic and Sweet Blindness, and Three Dog Night recorded Eli's Comin'.","The original LP came with a lyric sheet scented with perfume, a detail that suits the album's sensual, synesthetic style. Listen to how often a song stops dead and restarts in a new tempo: Nyro treats a song as a series of emotional scenes rather than verses and choruses. Her piano voicings come from jazz and gospel, and her phrasing owes much to the girl-group records of her New York childhood.","It sold modestly but was hugely admired by musicians; Elton John and Todd Rundgren both cited Nyro as an influence. Critics at the time found it overwrought; later ones hear it as a pioneering fusion of soul, jazz and the singer-songwriter form. Its reputation has risen steadily, and Nyro is now recognised as one of the most original songwriters of her era.","Start with Eli's Comin' and listen to how it builds. Then hear Lonely Women for the slow blues side. Compare with Gonna Take a Miracle, her later album of soul covers, to hear the roots of her style. Then play the whole record straight through, as Nyro intended."],"laura-nyro-gonna-take-a-miracle":["Ten soul, doo-wop and Motown songs from Nyro's youth, sung with the vocal trio Labelle: Patti LaBelle, Nona Hendryx and Sarah Dash. The arrangements are sparse at first, often just Nyro's piano and voices, then filled out with a rhythm section and horns. Monkey Time and Dancing in the Street are fused into a single medley; Spanish Harlem is slow and gentle; Nowhere to Run and Jimmy Mack are lively and celebratory. The title song closes the record.","After four albums of her own songs, Nyro wanted to return to the music of her New York adolescence, the street-corner harmony singing of the early 1960s. She recorded in Philadelphia at Sigma Sound with producers Kenny Gamble and Leon Huff, the architects of the Philadelphia soul sound. Labelle were then a little-known trio; within a few years they would have a huge hit of their own.","Listen to the a cappella passages, where the four voices blend without accompaniment, a direct homage to the doo-wop groups Nyro grew up hearing. Patti LaBelle's soaring high notes often answer Nyro's lead, creating a dialogue between two very different voices. Gamble and Huff's Philadelphia rhythm section, light on its feet and crisp on the beat, gives the faster songs their bounce without ever crowding the voices.","It sold modestly and was Nyro's last album for several years. Critics have come to regard it as a pioneering cross-racial collaboration and a loving tribute to the girl-group and soul traditions. Some find it a minor work compared to her originals; others hear it as a joyous celebration that shows exactly where her style came from.","Start with the medley of Monkey Time and Dancing in the Street, then hear the a cappella opening of I Met Him on a Sunday. Compare it with the Shirelles' and the Supremes' originals to hear what Nyro and Labelle add."],"john-martyn-solid-air":["Nine songs that blend folk, jazz and blues, with John Martyn's slurred, horn-like voice and his acoustic guitar at the centre. Danny Thompson's double bass is almost a second lead voice. The title song is a slow, smoky jazz ballad; May You Never is a brisk, warm blessing; I'd Rather Be the Devil turns a Skip James blues into a swirling, echo-drenched trance. The sound is intimate, late-night and loose.","Martyn was a Scottish-raised guitarist who had come up through the British folk clubs and signed with Island Records. He made this in London in 1972 with producer John Wood and players from Fairport Convention and Pentangle. The title song was written for his friend Nick Drake, Island's labelmate, who was then suffering from depression and would die in 1974.","Listen to I'd Rather Be the Devil: Martyn plays his acoustic guitar through an Echoplex, a tape-delay unit, so each note repeats and overlaps into a pulsing, hypnotic wash. The song is Skip James's 1931 Devil Got My Woman, transformed almost beyond recognition. Martyn's vocal phrasing, blurring words into sounds, owed more to jazz saxophonists than to folk singers, and he spoke of wanting his voice to sound like a horn.","It sold modestly at first but grew into a cult classic, now regarded as one of the finest British albums of the 1970s. Critics praised its blend of folk and jazz. Some find the slurred vocals hard to follow; most hear them as part of its seductive, dreamlike mood. May You Never has been widely covered.","Start with the title song for its slow, smoky mood, then hear I'd Rather Be the Devil for the Echoplex effects. Compare the latter with Skip James's original. Then hear Nick Drake's Pink Moon, the record of the friend the title song was written for."],"john-martyn-one-world":["Eight songs that move from dub-inflected grooves to ambient soundscapes, with Martyn's echo-laden guitar and slurred voice at the centre. Dealer and Big Muff are rhythmic and funky; Couldn't Love You More is a tender ballad; Small Hours is a long, slow instrumental-like piece built from sustained guitar tones and quiet voice. The overall sound is spacious and nocturnal, with reverb and delay as important as the notes.","Martyn made this in 1977 at Island boss Chris Blackwell's farm in Berkshire, recording partly outdoors at night. Big Muff was co-written with the Jamaican producer Lee Scratch Perry, reflecting Martyn's interest in reggae and dub, which Island was then promoting heavily. Steve Winwood played keyboards on some tracks, and Danny Thompson's double bass was again central.","Small Hours was recorded outdoors beside a lake at Blackwell's farm in the middle of the night, with the microphones picking up the natural reverb and, faintly, the sounds of the surrounding countryside. Listen for these in the background. Martyn's guitar tone throughout uses the Echoplex not as a trick but as the very fabric of the sound, layering delays into chords.","It sold modestly but has grown in reputation as a precursor to ambient music and trip-hop. Critics praised its atmosphere. Some found its blend of folk, dub and jazz unfocused; later listeners hear it as ahead of its time. Small Hours in particular is often cited as an influence on later ambient and post-rock artists.","Start with Small Hours, ideally at night, and listen to the space around the sound. Then hear Big Muff for the dub groove. Compare with Brian Eno's Another Green World, also from the mid-1970s, for another approach to studio-built atmosphere."],"judee-sill-judee-sill":["Eleven songs that blend country, folk and Baroque counterpoint, with Judee Sill's clear, plain voice and her own guitar and piano. Many songs use religious imagery, mixing Christian, mystical and cowboy themes. Jesus Was a Cross Maker is a gospel-tinged ballad; Lady-O is a delicate, lilting love song; The Lamb Ran Away With the Crown is upbeat and mysterious. Her own string and vocal arrangements give the record an unusual, almost sacred sound.","Sill had a troubled youth, including time in reform school and prison, before turning to songwriting in Los Angeles. She was the first artist signed to David Geffen's new Asylum label, and this was its first release. Graham Nash produced Jesus Was a Cross Maker. The Turtles had already recorded Lady-O. She called her style country-cult-baroque.","Listen to the harmonies and string parts: Sill studied Bach chorales and wrote her arrangements using Baroque voice-leading rules, so inner lines move independently and resolve in ways that pop songs rarely do. The result is a sound that seems to float. Her lyrics draw on mystical texts, Rosicrucian ideas and the imagery of the American West.","It sold poorly despite the support of Asylum and critics. Sill has since been rediscovered and is now widely regarded as one of the most original songwriters of her era. Critics praise her unusual fusion of sacred and secular music. Her reputation has grown steadily since reissues in the 2000s.","Start with Jesus Was a Cross Maker and Lady-O, then listen to the strings on The Archetypal Man. Compare with Bach's chorales in the St Matthew Passion to hear where her voice-leading comes from. Listen through headphones so the inner voices of the arrangements stand apart, and notice how few of the songs end where you expect them to."],"judee-sill-heart-food":["A set of songs more lavishly arranged than her debut, with Sill's own orchestral and choral writing. There's a Rugged Road is a gentle country song; The Kiss is a slow, devotional love song with sweeping strings; The Donor closes the album with a choir of Sill's overdubbed voices singing a Latin prayer for mercy over a hushed, organ-like accompaniment. The record moves from country to near-sacred music.","Sill wrote and conducted the orchestral arrangements herself, a rare achievement for a pop songwriter, and recorded in Los Angeles for Asylum. Despite Asylum's support, the album sold poorly, and Sill's career declined. She later struggled with ill health and died in 1979. Heart Food was her last album released in her lifetime.","Listen to The Donor: Sill multitracked her voice into a full choir singing the Kyrie eleison, the opening prayer of the Latin Mass, in counterpoint that recalls Renaissance and Baroque church music. The arrangement builds slowly over several minutes. Elsewhere, her string parts follow Bach-like voice-leading, with each line moving independently. She studied orchestration on her own.","It sold poorly but has since been recognised as a remarkable achievement, especially The Donor. Critics praise its ambition and beauty. Some find its religious imagery heavy; most hear it as sincere and unique. Reissues and tributes have brought Sill a new generation of admirers.","Start with The Kiss for its strings, then listen to The Donor at the end, ideally without distraction. Compare The Donor with the Kyrie of Bach's Mass in B minor to hear the sacred tradition Sill was reaching for. Play The Donor last, as Sill sequenced it."],"tim-buckley-happy-sad":["Six long songs that blend folk, jazz and blues, with Tim Buckley's flexible, wide-ranging voice and his twelve-string guitar. David Friedman's vibraphone adds a cool, shimmering sound; John Miller's double bass and Carter Collins's congas provide a loose, swinging rhythm. Strange Feelin' is built on a modal jazz groove; Love from Room 109 at the Islander includes the sound of ocean waves; Gypsy Woman is a long improvisation.","Buckley had made two folk-rock albums for Elektra in Los Angeles. For this one he moved toward jazz, influenced by Miles Davis and Charles Mingus, with producers Jerry Yester and Zal Yanovsky. It was his most successful album commercially but also signalled a move away from conventional songs that would alienate his audience.","Listen to Strange Feelin': its bass figure echoes Miles Davis's All Blues, from Kind of Blue, a nod to Buckley's jazz inspiration. Love from Room 109 at the Islander includes the sound of surf recorded on the Pacific coast, mixed in with the music. Buckley's voice moves from a soft croon to wordless cries, prefiguring his later experimental work.","It was his most successful album and is admired as a pioneering folk-jazz fusion. Critics praise its atmosphere. Some find the long improvisations meandering; others hear them as the beginning of Buckley's most adventurous period. It is often cited as an influence on later singer-songwriters.","Start with Strange Feelin' and compare it with All Blues on Kind of Blue. Then hear Love from Room 109 for the ocean sounds. Compare with Van Morrison's Astral Weeks, another late-1960s folk-jazz fusion. Let the long songs breathe; this record rewards an unhurried evening rather than a quick sampling of tracks."],"tim-buckley-starsailor":["Nine songs that range from free jazz to avant-garde vocal experiments, with Buckley's voice pushed to extremes: shrieks, swoops, wordless cries and multitracked layers. The title track is a choir of overdubbed voices in dense, dissonant clusters. Song to the Siren, the gentlest track, is a haunting ballad with echoing guitar. Monterey and Come Here Woman are wild, rhythmic workouts with horns.","Buckley had left Elektra and signed with Straight, the label run by Frank Zappa and Herb Cohen. He recorded in Los Angeles with guitarist Lee Underwood and with Buzz and Bunk Gardner, formerly of Zappa's Mothers of Invention, on horns. The album was a commercial failure, and Buckley soon turned to more conventional funk-rock. He died in 1975.","Listen to the title track: Buckley overdubbed his voice many times into a dense, shifting cluster, an effect closer to avant-garde classical vocal music than to rock. Song to the Siren, with lyrics by Larry Beckett, was later covered by This Mortal Coil with Elizabeth Fraser of the Cocteau Twins, bringing it to a new audience.","It was a commercial failure and baffled many listeners. Later critics have come to see it as a landmark of avant-garde rock and a showcase for one of the most extraordinary voices in popular music. Song to the Siren has become a standard. Debate continues over whether the wilder tracks are visionary or self-indulgent.","Start with Song to the Siren for the gentlest introduction, then try the title track. Compare the title track with Cathy Berberian's performance of Berio's Sequenza III for another extreme exploration of the voice. Give the stranger tracks a second hearing; their logic emerges slowly, like that of free jazz."],"jeff-buckley-grace":["Ten songs that blend alternative rock, folk, soul and devotional music, with Jeff Buckley's extraordinary voice ranging from a whisper to a falsetto wail. Mojo Pin opens with a hushed intro before exploding into rock; Grace is a dramatic, sweeping song; Last Goodbye is a lush, string-laden rock ballad; Hallelujah is a solo guitar and voice cover of Leonard Cohen's song; Corpus Christi Carol is a medieval carol sung in a high, pure tone.","Buckley had built a reputation singing covers at Sin-é, a small café in New York's East Village, before signing with Columbia. He recorded Grace at Bearsville Studios in Woodstock, New York, with producer Andy Wallace and his band. It was his only completed studio album; he drowned in 1997 in the Wolf River Harbor in Memphis while working on a second album.","Listen to Corpus Christi Carol: Buckley learned it from Benjamin Britten's setting of the medieval English carol in A Boy Was Born, and sings it in a high, choirboy-like voice. Lilac Wine comes from Nina Simone's version. Buckley's father was the singer Tim Buckley, whom he met only once, and Dream Brother has been read as a reflection on that absent father.","It sold modestly at first but has become one of the most acclaimed albums of the 1990s. Critics praised Buckley's voice and the album's range. Its reputation grew after his death, and it is now often cited as a major influence on later rock and pop singers. Some find its emotionalism excessive; most hear it as sincere.","Start with Hallelujah for the voice alone, then play the title track and Last Goodbye. Listen to Corpus Christi Carol for the high, pure singing. Compare with Tim Buckley's Starsailor to hear the family voice. The expanded Legacy Edition adds outtakes and live tracks that show how the songs grew on stage."],"jeff-buckley-hallelujah":["A song about love, faith and desire, sung over a single clean electric guitar. Buckley's interpretation of Leonard Cohen's song is slow and intimate, nearly seven minutes long, with his voice moving from a soft murmur to a soaring falsetto. The guitar plays gentle arpeggiated chords throughout, leaving space for the voice. There is no band, just one performer, and the recording captures every breath.","Leonard Cohen wrote and recorded the song in 1984, but it was obscure until John Cale recorded a version in 1991 with piano. Buckley learned Cale's version and made it a centrepiece of his performances at Sin-é before recording it for Grace. His recording became the definitive version for many listeners, especially after his death.","Listen to the guitar: Buckley plays a clean, slightly reverberant electric guitar, not acoustic, which gives the recording its glassy, floating sound. He follows John Cale's choice of verses rather than Cohen's original. Near the end, his voice rises to a long, held falsetto note that has become the most imitated moment in the song's history.","It was not released as a single at the time but grew in popularity after Buckley's death, eventually becoming one of the most celebrated covers in rock. Critics praise its beauty and intensity. Some argue it overshadowed Cohen's original and spawned countless imitations; others credit it with bringing Cohen's song to a wide audience.","Listen with headphones to hear the breath and the guitar. Compare with Cohen's original, which is quite different in tone, and then with John Cale's piano version to hear where Buckley's approach came from. Notice how long Buckley waits before letting his voice open up, and how quietly he ends."],"karen-dalton-its-so-hard-to-tell-whos-going-to-love-you-the-best":["Ten blues and folk songs, sung in Karen Dalton's cracked, aching voice with her twelve-string guitar and banjo. Her singing has a slow, dragging phrasing, like Billie Holiday, with a timbre that sounds old and worn. Little Bit of Rain is a gentle Fred Neil song; It Hurts Me Too is a slow blues; Ribbon Bow is a sad, simple ballad played on banjo. The backing is sparse, a quiet bass and drums.","Dalton was a central figure in the Greenwich Village folk scene of the early 1960s, admired by Bob Dylan and Fred Neil. She was famously reluctant to record. This, her first album, was made for Capitol with producer Nick Venet; it was reportedly cut very quickly. It sold poorly. Dylan later wrote warmly about her in his memoir Chronicles.","Listen to how she places her words late, behind the beat, stretching syllables like a jazz singer. Her twelve-string guitar is played with a heavy, rolling strum that keeps time like a slow heartbeat. She often sings in a register that sounds deliberately strained, giving even gentle songs a sense of weariness. All the songs are by other writers, but her interpretations transform them.","It was largely ignored on release but has been rediscovered and is now regarded as a classic of the folk revival. Critics praise her voice and interpretations. Some find her singing too idiosyncratic; others hear it as uniquely expressive. Reissues in the 2000s brought her a new audience of musicians and listeners.","Start with Little Bit of Rain, then hear Ribbon Bow for the banjo. Compare her voice with Billie Holiday's late recordings on Lady in Satin. Then hear In My Own Time, her second and last album. Listen for how far behind the beat she lets each line fall."],"karen-dalton-in-my-own-time":["Ten songs, more fully arranged than her debut, with a band including keyboards, fiddle and horns behind Dalton's voice and banjo. When a Man Loves a Woman is slowed to a mournful crawl; Katie Cruel is a traditional ballad played on fiddle and banjo; How Sweet It Is is a soulful cover; Something on Your Mind is a gentle folk song. Her voice is again the focus, cracked and achingly expressive.","Dalton made this in Woodstock, New York, at Bearsville Studios, with producer Harvey Brooks and local musicians from the scene around The Band. It was released on Just Sunshine, a small label founded by Woodstock Festival promoter Michael Lang. It sold poorly, and Dalton never recorded another album. She died in 1993.","Listen to Katie Cruel: Dalton sings it unaccompanied except for fiddle and banjo, in a style close to old Appalachian ballad singing. In a Station is by Richard Manuel of The Band; Dalton's version is slower and more desolate than the original on Music from Big Pink. Her versions of soul songs strip away their optimism.","It was largely ignored on release but has been rediscovered, especially after a 2006 reissue. Critics praise its arrangements and Dalton's voice. It is now regarded as one of the great lost albums of the era. Some find the arrangements too busy for her voice; others hear them as supportive.","Start with Katie Cruel, then hear When a Man Loves a Woman. Compare In a Station with The Band's original on Music from Big Pink. Then return to her debut for her sparser sound. The 2006 reissue is the easiest edition to find and sounds clean and close."],"bill-withers-just-as-i-am":["Twelve songs, mostly short and spare, with Bill Withers's warm, unadorned baritone and his acoustic guitar over a light rhythm section and occasional strings. Ain't No Sunshine is a two-minute lament for an absent lover, famous for a single phrase repeated many times; Grandma's Hands is an affectionate portrait of his grandmother; Harlem is a brisk song about the neighbourhood. He also covers the Beatles' Let It Be and Fred Neil's Everybody's Talkin'.","Withers was thirty-two and working in an aircraft parts factory in California when he made this, his debut, for the small Sussex label. It was produced by Booker T. Jones, of Booker T. and the M.G.'s, who brought in Stephen Stills and members of the M.G.'s. The cover shows Withers holding his lunch box. Ain't No Sunshine became a hit and won a Grammy.","Listen to Ain't No Sunshine: in the middle the music drops away and Withers repeats one phrase over and over, a section he meant to fill with lyrics but left as it was on the advice of the musicians. The strings, arranged by Booker T., are used sparingly and make the song feel even barer. Grandma's Hands later supplied the basis for other records.","It was a success and established Withers as a major songwriter. Critics praised its simplicity and honesty. It is now regarded as a classic of 1970s soul. Some found it too plain at the time; later listeners value exactly that plainness. Ain't No Sunshine has become one of the most covered songs of its era.","Start with Ain't No Sunshine and listen for the repeated phrase. Then hear Grandma's Hands. Compare with Otis Redding's Dock of the Bay for another quiet, introspective soul song. Then play Harlem and Hope She'll Be Happier to hear how much feeling Withers draws from very few notes, and notice how often the guitar alone carries the song."],"bill-withers-still-bill":["Ten songs with a funkier, tighter band sound than the debut. Lean on Me is a gospel-tinged song of friendship over a rolling piano; Use Me is a sly, keyboard-and-drums groove; Who Is He (And What Is He to You)? is a tense, jealous funk song; Kissing My Love is built on a crisp drum pattern. Withers's voice is relaxed and conversational throughout, and the band plays with spare, deep-pocketed precision.","After the success of his debut, Withers produced this himself with members of the Watts 103rd Street Rhythm Band, including drummer James Gadson. He recorded in Los Angeles. Lean on Me, inspired by his childhood in a West Virginia coal-mining town, became a huge hit and later an anthem of solidarity.","Listen to James Gadson's drumming on Kissing My Love and Use Me: crisp, spare and deep in the groove, it was later sampled widely in hip-hop. Lean on Me uses a simple rising and falling piano figure, like a hymn. Who Is He is built on a tense, repeated guitar and bass riff that creates mounting suspense.","It was Withers's most successful album and is now regarded as a soul classic. Critics praised the band and the songs. Lean on Me has become one of the most performed songs in American life, sung at schools, churches and rallies. Some found the album uneven; most hear it as a model of economy.","Start with Lean on Me, then hear Use Me and Kissing My Love for the drumming. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, from the year before, for a more orchestral approach to soul. Notice how little the band plays, and how much that leaves for the voice."],"gil-scott-heron-pieces-of-a-man":["Eleven songs that blend soul, jazz and spoken word, with Gil Scott-Heron's deep, rich voice and a crack band. The Revolution Will Not Be Televised opens with a funky bass and flute groove under Scott-Heron's half-spoken, satirical poem about media and protest; Lady Day and John Coltrane is a joyful tribute to jazz; Home Is Where the Hatred Is, about addiction, is a slow, mournful soul song; Pieces of a Man is a tender ballad about a father.","Scott-Heron was a twenty-two-year-old novelist and poet from Tennessee and New York who had released a spoken-word album the year before. This, his first album with a full band, was produced by Bob Thiele for his Flying Dutchman label in New York, with Brian Jackson on piano, Ron Carter on bass, Bernard Purdie on drums and Hubert Laws on flute and saxophone.","Listen to Ron Carter's bass on The Revolution Will Not Be Televised: a jazz musician from Miles Davis's quintet playing a funk groove, it gives the song its urgency. Brian Jackson's piano and arrangements, often uncredited in later discussions, are crucial to the album's sound. Lady Day and John Coltrane refers to Billie Holiday and Coltrane as healing forces.","It sold modestly at first but has become one of the most influential albums of its era. Critics praise its blend of politics and music. Scott-Heron has been called a godfather of hip-hop, a title he resisted. The Revolution Will Not Be Televised has become a widely quoted phrase. Some find the jazz-soul arrangements dated; most hear them as essential.","Start with The Revolution Will Not Be Televised, then hear Lady Day and John Coltrane and Pieces of a Man. Compare with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, from the same year, for another soul album about America. Listen to the bass and drums as closely as the words."],"gil-scott-heron-winter-in-america":["Nine songs and pieces, intimate and sparse, built around Brian Jackson's piano, electric piano and flute, with Scott-Heron's voice and sometimes his own piano. Peace Go with You, Brother is a slow, gospel-tinged farewell; Rivers of My Fathers is a meditation on ancestry; The Bottle, about alcoholism, is an upbeat, flute-led groove; H2Ogate Blues is a long, spoken satire of the Watergate scandal over a blues accompaniment.","Credited to Scott-Heron and Brian Jackson, this was recorded in 1973 near Washington, D.C., and released in 1974 on Strata-East, an independent, musician-run jazz label. It came out as Watergate was unfolding and as American cities were struggling with poverty and unrest. The title captured a mood of disillusion after the 1960s.","Listen to Brian Jackson's Fender Rhodes electric piano: its warm, bell-like tone defines the album's sound and became a signature of 1970s jazz-soul. The Bottle became an underground club hit, spreading through DJs rather than radio. H2Ogate Blues includes a spoken introduction about the blues as a response to crisis.","It was released on a small label but became an underground success. Critics now regard it as one of Scott-Heron's finest albums, and it is often cited as a key influence on neo-soul and conscious hip-hop. Some find H2Ogate Blues too long; others hear it as a vital political document.","Start with The Bottle for its groove, then hear Rivers of My Fathers and Peace Go with You, Brother. Listen for the electric piano. Compare with Stevie Wonder's Innervisions, from the year before, for another politically charged soul album. Then hear the whole album as one long, late-night set."],"joanna-newsom-the-milk-eyed-mender":["Twelve songs built almost entirely from one woman and a concert harp. Joanna Newsom plucks interlocking patterns in both hands, the left hand often walking a bass line while the right spins figures above it, and over this she sings in a high, cracked, wildly elastic voice that swoops, yelps and drawls. Peach Plum Pear swaps the harp for a clattering harpsichord; Sprout and the Bean is a light, skipping tune; Sadie is slow and grave. The sound is bare, close and unmistakable from the first phrase.","Newsom grew up in Nevada City, California, studied the harp from childhood, and briefly studied composition at Mills College before leaving to make her own songs. Early home recordings reached the Chicago label Drag City, which released this debut in March 2004, produced with her friend Noah Georgeson. It arrived amid a scene that the press loosely called freak folk, alongside Devendra Banhart, and her voice made her instantly famous and instantly divisive in indie-rock circles that had never heard a harp front a band.","Listen to the harp lines as counterpoint rather than accompaniment: her training is in the Celtic and classical harp, and the left hand often moves independently of the right in the way a Baroque keyboard player's would. Several songs, including Three Little Babes, borrow shapes from old Anglo-American ballads, and the record ends with En Gallop, the earliest of the songs, which already holds the rhythmic restlessness of everything to come.","Few debuts split listeners so cleanly. Many critics praised the originality of the writing and the harp playing; just as many reviewers wrote mainly about the voice, which some heard as childlike affectation and others as fearless. Over time the argument has settled in Newsom's favour, and the album is now heard as the seed of the large-scale work that followed rather than as a curiosity of the freak-folk moment.","Give the voice two or three songs before deciding; it grows familiar quickly and then becomes the point. Start with Bridges and Balloons, then Sadie for the slow, serious side, then Peach Plum Pear for the harpsichord. Follow the harp's lowest line through a whole song. Afterwards compare Alan Stivell's Celtic harp records and Kate Bush's early albums for other private worlds built on unusual voices."],"joanna-newsom-ys":["Five songs running between seven and seventeen minutes, each a long, through-composed narrative with almost no repeated choruses. Newsom's harp and voice carry everything, while a full orchestra, written by Van Dyke Parks, comments, swells, mutters and occasionally sweeps in like weather. Emily opens at twelve minutes; Monkey & Bear is a fable told in a lurching waltz; Sawdust & Diamonds is harp and voice alone; Only Skin, the longest, adds a second male voice near its end; Cosmia closes in a slow blaze of strings.","Released by Drag City in November 2006 and named after the legendary drowned city of Breton folklore. Newsom wrote the songs after a hard year that included a serious illness, the loss of people close to her and the end of a relationship. She recorded harp and voice with Steve Albini in Chicago, Parks then wrote and recorded orchestral arrangements in Los Angeles around the finished takes, and Jim O'Rourke mixed the result. Bill Callahan, her partner at the time, sings on Only Skin.","Because the orchestra was added after the harp and voice were recorded, the strings and winds are always reacting to Newsom's rubato rather than setting a beat, which is why they seem to breathe with her. Emily is addressed to her younger sister, an astrophysicist, and is full of accurate talk of meteors and meteorites. Only Skin quietly brings back melodic shapes from earlier songs, binding the record together.","Reviews were rapturous and the album appeared near the top of many end-of-year lists; it was widely compared with Joni Mitchell's Hejira and with Van Dyke Parks's own Song Cycle. Some listeners found the length and the density of the language exhausting. Two decades on it is regularly named among the most ambitious records of the century so far and the high point of the whole folk revival of the 2000s.","Set aside an hour, use headphones, and do not shuffle. Begin with Sawdust & Diamonds to hear the songs in their bare state, then go back to Emily and listen for the way the orchestra waits, sometimes for minutes, before it enters. Read the words afterward rather than during. Then compare The Smile Sessions for Parks's orchestral humour and Hejira for long-lined songwriting."],"joanna-newsom-have-one-on-me":["A triple album of eighteen songs lasting just over two hours, spread across three discs that each run like a separate evening. The writing is looser and warmer than on Ys: Newsom alternates harp and piano, her voice is lower and smoother, and the band arrangements by Ryan Francesconi bring in strings, horns, banjo and a drum kit. The title song is an eleven-minute portrait of a nineteenth-century dancer; Good Intentions Paving Company is close to a country-soul song; Baby Birch is long and devastating.","Drag City released the set in February 2010 after nearly four years of silence, during which Newsom had lost her voice for a period to vocal nodules and had to relearn how to sing, which accounts for the changed timbre. She insisted that the triple album be sold at roughly the price of a single record, taking the cost onto her own share, because she wanted listeners to hear the whole thing as one work rather than buying fragments.","The final song, Does Not Suffice, quietly reuses the melody of the opening song, Easy, so the two hours end where they began, but with the meaning turned around. The title track's subject is Lola Montez, the dancer and adventurer whose affair with King Ludwig I of Bavaria helped bring down his reign. Listen for the recurring imagery of birds and of California, which links songs across all three discs.","Most critics welcomed the album warmly, finding it more approachable than Ys and admiring the range of songwriting, and it sold well for a two-hour folk record. A few felt that the length diluted the impact and that a single disc would have been stronger. The album has aged well; many listeners now hold it as her richest and most human record, and Good Intentions Paving Company is probably her most popular song.","Treat it as three albums and hear one disc at a sitting. Start the first disc with Easy and stay through Good Intentions Paving Company, where the drums arrive. Later, come back for Baby Birch, which builds from near silence. Pay attention to the piano songs, a new colour for her. Compare Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark for a piano-driven turn toward warmth, then Laura Marling's Once I Was an Eagle."],"joanna-newsom-divers":["Eleven songs, shorter and more compressed than on her earlier albums, in which harp and piano share the lead with old synthesizers, Mellotron, a marxophone and a clavichord, with arrangements by several hands including Nico Muhly and Dave Longstreth. Sapokanikan bounces on a jaunty piano; Leaving the City surges on drums; the title song is a long, still harp ballad; Waltz of the 101st Lightborne races through a time-travel fantasy. The palette is brighter and stranger than before, but every song turns on loss and time.","Released by Drag City in October 2015, five years after Have One on Me, when Newsom was living in New York and newly married to the actor Andy Samberg. She co-produced the record with longtime collaborator Noah Georgeson and spent years on it. Its preoccupations, mortality, the unequal lengths of human lives, and history buried under city streets, run through Sapokanikan, named for a Lenape settlement that once stood where Greenwich Village is now.","The album is a loop. The last song, Time, as a Symptom, breaks off in the middle of a word, and that unfinished word completes itself when the first song begins again, so on repeat the record never quite ends. Birdsong recurs between songs as a structural thread. Sapokanikan is studded with references to Shelley's Ozymandias and to the forgotten dead of the Manhattan grid.","Critics gave Divers some of the strongest reviews of Newsom's career, praising its concision and the density of ideas packed into each song; it made many year-end lists. Some listeners who loved the orchestral sweep of Ys found the synthesizer colours less to their taste. It has since come to be heard as the most tightly constructed of her albums and, after a long silence that followed, her last for some years.","Begin with Sapokanikan, then the title track, then Leaving the City; afterwards play the whole thing twice in a row to hear the loop close. Listen for the birds in the gaps and for the clavichord, a delicate early keyboard rarely heard in popular music. Compare Kate Bush's Aerial for another domestic, time-haunted album, and Sufjan Stevens's Carrie & Lowell, released the same year, for grief handled very differently."],"iron-and-wine-the-creek-drank-the-cradle":["Eleven songs recorded at home on a four-track machine: a whispered double-tracked voice, a fingerpicked acoustic guitar, a little banjo and slide, and the hiss of the tape under everything. Sam Beam sings so softly he seems to be trying not to wake someone. The songs are short and circular, with Southern imagery of creeks, rooster crows, mothers, Bibles and dirt roads. Lion's Mane opens it, Upward Over the Mountain is the emotional centre, and Bird Stealing Bread is a perfect miniature.","Beam was teaching film and cinematography at a college in Miami and writing songs in his spare time, recording them alone at home. A demo reached Sub Pop, the Seattle label best known for grunge, which released this debut in September 2002 more or less as he had recorded it. The lo-fi sound connected him to the bedroom recordings then popular in indie rock, but the songs reached back to Appalachian and gospel forms and to Nick Drake.","The double-tracked vocal is the secret of the sound: Beam sang most lines twice, and the two slightly mismatched whispers make one ghostly voice. Upward Over the Mountain is written as a son speaking to his mother about leaving and being changed, and it frames the album's quiet family drama. Listen for small domestic noises and tape artefacts left in; the record was never cleaned up for release.","The album was a sleeper success, spread by word of mouth and warm reviews that compared Beam to Nick Drake and Elliott Smith. Some critics found the uniform hush monotonous over forty minutes. It became a template for a decade of bearded, home-recorded folk, and is now regarded as one of the founding records of 2000s indie folk alongside the early Sufjan Stevens and Bon Iver albums.","Play it quietly, late at night; it is designed for low volume. Start with Upward Over the Mountain and Bird Stealing Bread, then listen to the whole record. Try to separate the two voices in the double-tracking. Then hear Nick Drake's Pink Moon for the closest ancestor, Elliott Smith's self-titled album for another whispering voice, and Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago for what followed."],"iron-and-wine-our-endless-numbered-days":["Twelve songs that keep the hush of the debut but trade the four-track hiss for a clean studio sound. Sam Beam's voice is still close and soft, now joined by his sister Sarah's harmonies, and the guitar picking is clearer and more intricate, ringed with banjo, slide, piano and brushed drums. Naked as We Came is a two-minute song about love and death that became his best known; Sodom, South Georgia and Passing Afternoon show him writing slow, shapely melodies with a gospel undertow.","Released by Sub Pop in March 2004, this was Beam's first album made in a proper studio, produced by Brian Deck in Chicago with a small band. The same year Beam's whispered cover of the Postal Service's electronic song Such Great Heights, issued on a single, was used in the film Garden State and in advertising, bringing him a far wider audience than most folk singers of the period could expect.","Many songs are about the passage of years in a single household, and the album's title captures that tension: days that feel endless but are counted. Passing Afternoon, which closes the record, is built from a handful of repeating images of seasons and children. Listen also for the slide guitar, used sparingly as a second voice, and for how often the songs fade before their final cadence rather than resolving it.","Reviews were very warm, with most critics hearing a clear step forward in craft while keeping the intimacy. A minority missed the mystery of the tape hiss. Naked as We Came became a fixture at weddings and funerals alike, and the record is now often named as the most perfectly realised of his early, quiet period, before the larger arrangements that came next.","Start with Naked as We Came, then Passing Afternoon, then play the album through. Notice how the second voice in the harmonies is Sarah Beam's rather than his own double-tracking. Compare with The Creek Drank the Cradle to hear what a studio adds, and with Simon and Garfunkel-style close harmony. Then try Fleet Foxes' first album, which absorbed a lot from this record."],"iron-and-wine-the-shepherds-dog":["Twelve songs in which the whispering folk singer becomes a bandleader. Layered percussion, shakers, hand drums, organ, piano and electric guitar weave dense, rolling textures under Sam Beam's still-soft voice. Pagan Angel and a Borrowed Car opens on a loping groove; Boy with a Coin rides hand claps and a looped rhythm; House by the Sea has a nearly African guitar feel; Flightless Bird, American Mouth closes the album slowly on a waltz that later became his most famous song.","Sub Pop released it in September 2007, again produced by Brian Deck. Beam had toured and recorded with the Tucson band Calexico on the 2005 EP In the Reins, which opened his ears to fuller arrangements, and he spoke of the album as shaped by the confusion he felt about American politics after the 2004 election. Flightless Bird, American Mouth reached a mass audience in 2008 through the soundtrack of the film Twilight.","The songs are full of people caught between faith and doubt, and between belonging and exile. Listen for how the dense percussion is often recorded close and dry while the voice floats far back in reverb, inverting the old balance. Many of the sound loops were built in the studio from small hand-played fragments.","Critics were largely enthusiastic, many calling it his most adventurous and best record, and it sold more than his earlier albums. Some early fans lamented the loss of the bare sound. The change of direction is now seen as decisive: it showed that the quiet indie folk of the early 2000s could absorb world-music rhythm and studio colour without losing its centre.","Start with Boy with a Coin to hear the new rhythmic engine, then Flightless Bird, American Mouth for the old tenderness. Listen to the percussion as its own story under the songs. Compare Paul Simon's rhythm-driven later records, then go back to The Creek Drank the Cradle to measure how far the sound travelled in five years. Bon Iver's second album took a similar step."],"laura-marling-i-speak-because-i-can":["Ten songs in which a twenty-year-old's voice has dropped into a grave, assured alto, over fingerpicked and strummed acoustic guitar driven hard by a small band of banjo, upright bass and drums, with strings and brass at the edges. Devil's Spoke opens it with a stomping, minor-key urgency; Rambling Man is a resolute anthem; Goodbye England (Covered in Snow) is tender and pastoral; Alpha Shallows and the title song close darkly. The sound is closer to English folk-rock than to London indie.","Marling grew up in Hampshire, left home young for London, and was at the centre of the West London nu-folk scene alongside Mumford & Sons and Noah and the Whale. Released by Virgin in March 2010 and produced by Ethan Johns, this second album was recorded largely live, with members of Mumford & Sons in the band. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and the following year she won the Brit Award for best British female solo artist.","Listen to how the songs are written about women's duties, guilt and the weight of being responsible for others, rather than simple romance. Many guitar parts are in open tunings that let low strings drone beneath the melody, a habit inherited from Bert Jansch and Nick Drake. Ethan Johns kept the room sound, including performers' breaths.","Critics greeted the album as a leap from her charming debut, praising the maturity of the writing and the deep new voice; it topped a few British year-end lists and the Mercury nomination confirmed it. A few heard the folk-revival trappings of her scene as fashionable. It now reads as the start of the run of records that made her one of the most respected British songwriters of her generation.","Start with Devil's Spoke and Rambling Man for drive, then Goodbye England for the gentle side. Listen to how the bass and drums push behind the beat. Compare Sandy Denny's solo records and Joni Mitchell's Blue for women writing seriously over acoustic guitar, and Bert Jansch for the tunings. Ethan Johns's natural production rewards a good stereo."],"laura-marling-once-i-was-an-eagle":["Sixteen songs over an hour, opening with a run of four songs that flow into each other without a break for some sixteen minutes, all in the same open guitar tuning and darkening as they go. Marling's voice and guitar dominate, shadowed by a cello and by hand drums that pulse rather than keep time. An instrumental Interlude splits the album in two; the second half, with songs like Master Hunter and Where Can I Go?, is lighter, more varied and freer in mood.","Released by Virgin in May 2013 and again produced by Ethan Johns, who also plays drums and percussion, the album was recorded quickly, largely live, with Marling playing guitar and singing simultaneously. She had recently ended a relationship and moved toward a period of travel and relocation to Los Angeles. It was her third Mercury Prize nomination and was widely hailed as her most ambitious work.","The long opening suite is held together by a single guitar tuning and a recurring melodic shape, so that the four songs work almost as movements. Listen for Indian and Middle Eastern inflections in the guitar and the hand drums, which recall the raga-touched guitar of the 1960s British folk baroque. The songs track a woman refusing to be cast as victim, ending far from where they begin.","Reviews were outstanding, with several British critics calling it the album of the year and comparing its scope to Joni Mitchell's Hejira. Some found its length and uniformity of mood demanding. It is now often regarded as Marling's central work and one of the best British folk albums of the century, though listeners split over whether it surpasses her later, sparer records.","Play the first four songs straight through without interruption; that is the heart of the album. Notice the cello acting as a second voice, then wait for Interlude and hear how the mood lifts. Compare Joni Mitchell's Hejira and Joanna Newsom's Have One on Me for long-form songwriting, and Bert Jansch's open-tuned guitar for the playing tradition behind it."],"laura-marling-song-for-our-daughter":["Ten songs addressed, broadly, to a daughter Marling did not yet have, about what a young woman should know about love, power and self-respect. The sound is warm and unhurried: fingerpicked guitar and light piano, softly layered harmony vocals, a string section arranged with gentle restraint, and a band that sounds like it is in the room. Alexandra opens brightly; Held Down is a quiet anthem; the title song and For You end the record in near-lullaby simplicity.","Marling brought the release forward to April 2020 because of the first wave of the pandemic, issuing it on Chrysalis and Partisan with little warning. She co-produced it with Ethan Johns and engineer Dom Monks. It was nominated for the Mercury Prize and for the Grammy Award for Best Folk Album, and led to a series of livestreamed concerts during lockdown; Marling later had a daughter of her own.","Alexandra is an answer song to Leonard Cohen's Alexandra Leaving, reimagining the woman in Cohen's song as someone with her own reasons rather than a figure of a man's loss. The string arrangements consciously echo the orchestrations of late-1960s and early-1970s singer-songwriter records. Listen to how the harmony vocals are multitracked Marling herself, building a small choir of one voice.","Critics welcomed it as her warmest and most direct album, and it featured in many 2020 year-end lists; its lockdown timing gave its calm an unexpected resonance. A few longtime admirers missed the darker edge of her earlier work. It is now generally ranked among her finest records and is often the album new listeners are pointed to first.","Start with Alexandra, then go to Leonard Cohen's Alexandra Leaving to hear the conversation. Then listen to Held Down and the title track and notice how little the strings do and how much it matters. Compare Joni Mitchell's Ladies of the Canyon and Nick Drake's Bryter Layter for the string-laced folk tradition the album consciously joins."],"rhiannon-giddens-tomorrow-is-my-turn":["Eleven songs, nearly all associated with women singers across a century of American and European music: Dolly Parton, Patsy Cline, Odetta, Nina Simone, Elizabeth Cotten, Sister Rosetta Tharpe, the blues singer Geeshie Wiley and others, plus one original. Giddens's operatically trained voice moves from country tears to gospel shouting to a cabaret whisper. The band, assembled by T Bone Burnett, is spacious and woody, with fiddle, banjo and an organ. Waterboy surges; Last Kind Words is haunted; the title song is French chanson filtered through Simone.","Giddens had trained in opera at Oberlin Conservatory before co-founding the Carolina Chocolate Drops, the Grammy-winning string band that restored Black musicians to the story of old-time music. A showstopping appearance at a 2013 New York concert celebrating the film Inside Llewyn Davis led T Bone Burnett to produce this, her first solo album, released by Nonesuch in February 2015.","Each choice is an argument: the album is a quiet history of women, Black and white, who carried American music, and of the way songs moved between gospel, country, blues and French cabaret. Last Kind Words comes from a 1930 recording by Geeshie Wiley, a singer about whom almost nothing is known. Angel City, the original, is her only song on the record and points toward the writer she would become.","Reviews were excellent, singling out the range and control of the voice, and the album launched her solo career. Some critics wished for more of her own writing and found the covers too reverent. It now reads as a manifesto for the career that followed, which brought her a MacArthur Fellowship in 2017 and later a Pulitzer Prize for the opera Omar, written with Michael Abels.","Start with Waterboy and then go back to Odetta's version for comparison; then hear Last Kind Words. Listen to how Giddens changes vocal colour completely from song to song. Pair it with Nina Simone's Pastel Blues and Elizabeth Cotten's Folksongs and Instrumentals with Guitar, both direct sources, and then the Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig."],"rhiannon-giddens-freedom-highway":["Twelve songs, mostly originals, that move through the history of Black Americans from slavery to police violence. Giddens plays minstrel-era banjo and fiddle and sings with rough-edged power; the band adds blues guitar, brass, gospel choir harmonies and, on one song, a rap verse. At the Purchaser's Option opens over a hypnotic banjo; Julie is a dialogue between an enslaved woman and her mistress; Birmingham Sunday is a sorrowful cover; the title song, from the Staple Singers, closes in jubilation.","Released by Nonesuch in February 2017 and co-produced by Giddens and the multi-instrumentalist Dirk Powell, largely in Powell's home studio in Breaux Bridge, Louisiana. Giddens had been reading slave narratives and collections of advertisements for enslaved people, and the record appeared during the national argument over police killings and the Black Lives Matter movement, which it addresses directly in one song.","At the Purchaser's Option was prompted by a real nineteenth-century advertisement offering an enslaved young woman for sale with her nine-month-old baby included at the buyer's choice. Birmingham Sunday, written by Richard Fariña, remembers the 1963 church bombing. Listen to the banjo itself: Giddens plays a gourd or fretless style that recalls the instrument's African ancestry, a history she has spent her career reasserting.","Critics welcomed it as her most ambitious and personal record, praising the songwriting and the courage of its subjects; some found the stylistic shifts, especially the hip-hop track, uneven. It sealed her reputation as a musician-historian, and it remains a key album in the conversation about Black roots music and who owns the banjo and the fiddle.","Start with At the Purchaser's Option and listen to the banjo pattern under the voice; then hear Julie as a short play for two characters. End with the title song and then play the Staple Singers' original. Pair with Nina Simone's protest songs and with Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig. Read the liner notes, which give each song's history."],"rhiannon-giddens-there-is-no-other":["A duo record with the Italian multi-instrumentalist Francesco Turrisi, who plays frame drums, accordion, piano and early-music instruments. Twelve songs drawn from many places: American spirituals and ballads, a southern Italian tarantella-style dance, a Gian Carlo Menotti aria, an Oscar Brown Jr. song and originals. The sound is bare, physical and drum-led. Ten Thousand Voices opens with a pulse of frame drum; Wayfaring Stranger is slowed to a trance; Pizzica di San Vito whirls; Black Swan is sung like an old ballad.","Giddens had moved to Ireland, and she and Turrisi recorded in Dublin in a few days with producer Joe Henry. Nonesuch released it in May 2019. The project grew from their shared conviction that the banjo and the frame drum trace routes from Africa through the Mediterranean and Europe to America, so that there is no pure folk music, only exchange.","The title is the thesis: that what we call one tradition is always another's in disguise. Listen for Turrisi, a musician with roots in early music as well as jazz, switching between frame drum, accordion and piano, quietly connecting older European practice to folk. Black Swan comes from Menotti's 1946 opera The Medium, sung here as if it were an Appalachian ballad, which is precisely the point. Many takes were captured live with both players in one room.","Critics praised the album's daring and intimacy, and it was widely admired as a model of cross-cultural collaboration. Some listeners found it more of a scholarly exercise than an emotional record. It began a lasting partnership; the pair's follow-up, They're Calling Me Home, came in 2021, and the duo has become a central part of Giddens's work.","Start with Ten Thousand Voices for the drum and voice, then Pizzica di San Vito for the Italian dance, then Wayfaring Stranger. Listen to how few instruments there are. Pair with Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances, another case of art music made from shared folk dance, and with Alan Lomax's Columbia World Library, which set out the same idea of connected traditions in the 1950s."],"anais-mitchell-young-man-in-america":["Eleven songs, many sung by Mitchell in the voices of men: sons, fathers, wanderers and a shepherd. Her high, slightly nasal voice is set against a full band of guitars, piano, strings and a deep, rolling rhythm section, with a darker, more cinematic sound than her earlier folk records. The title song is a slow, ominous portrait of a restless young man; Shepherd tells a rural tragedy over a long, patient build; He Did is a quiet elegy-like portrait of her father; Coming Down glows.","Released on Mitchell's own Wilderland label in February 2012 and produced by Todd Sickafoose, it was her first album after the concept record Hadestown of 2010, the folk opera that would years later become a Tony-winning Broadway musical. Mitchell grew up on a sheep farm in Vermont, and the album draws on that world and on her father's writing, using the stage-trained skill of singing through characters she had developed on Hadestown.","Shepherd is a retelling of a story from her father Don Mitchell's novel The Souls of Lambs, about a farmer whose wife dies in childbirth while he works in the fields. Listen for the way recurring images of land, inheritance and debt link the songs into a loose cycle about American manhood. The album closes on songs of forgiveness that soften its darker portraits.","Reviews were warm and often admiring, calling it her most fully realised album and noting the confident, filmic production. Some reviewers missed the intimacy of her earlier acoustic work, and the Hadestown project overshadowed it in public memory. Folk listeners still hold it as her finest solo record and the bridge between her songwriting and her theatre writing.","Start with the title song and then Shepherd; follow the story in Shepherd as you would a short film. Notice how often she sings as a man and how her voice changes to do it. Then hear the original Hadestown album for comparison, and Gillian Welch's The Harrow & the Harvest for another meditation on rural American life."],"anais-mitchell-anais-mitchell":["Nine songs of reflective, quietly autobiographical folk-pop, gently produced and warmly sung. Mitchell's voice is clearer and calmer than before, set against fingerpicked guitar, piano, soft drums and a pale wash of keyboards. Brooklyn Bridge opens with a song about nostalgia for an earlier New York life; Bright Star is a gentle love song; Little Big Girl looks at a young woman's ambition; Revenant and Real Life probe memory and ageing; Now You Know closes in a radiant, simple tune.","Released by BMG in January 2022, produced by her Bonny Light Horseman bandmate Josh Kaufman, and recorded with musicians from the Hudson Valley scene, the album was her first collection of new original songs in roughly a decade. In that time Hadestown had grown from a folk album into a Broadway musical that won eight Tony Awards in 2019; Mitchell then moved back toward Vermont during the pandemic and wrote these songs looking back on her years in New York.","Listen to the economy of the arrangements: after years writing for a full Broadway pit, Mitchell and Kaufman deliberately limited each song to a few textures, often a single guitar figure. Several songs, including Brooklyn Bridge and On Your Way (Felix Song), name real places and people from her past. The self-title signals a reset, an album of herself after years of writing in characters' voices.","Critics welcomed the album as graceful and generous, some finding it her most personal record and others thinking it understated after the scale of Hadestown. It placed on some year-end folk lists. It is generally heard as a satisfying coda to the Broadway years and a companion to the more traditional songs of Bonny Light Horseman.","Begin with Brooklyn Bridge, then Bright Star and Now You Know. Listen to the warmth of the production and the restraint of the band. Pair it with Joni Mitchell's Ladies of the Canyon for the comfortable, domestic folk-pop sound, and with Bonny Light Horseman's debut to hear the same players on older material."],"sam-amidon-all-is-well":["Traditional Appalachian ballads, hymns and children's songs, sung in a flat, unhurried, conversational voice over banjo or guitar, then wrapped in chamber arrangements of strings, brass and winds by the composer Nico Muhly. The old songs, including Saro and Wild Bill Jones, are slowed and pulled apart so the arrangements can drift around them like mist. The effect is half folk record and half contemporary chamber music, with long, still introductions and sudden swells.","Amidon grew up in Brattleboro, Vermont, the son of folk musicians who led community singing and taught traditional song, and he played fiddle from childhood. He recorded the album in Reykjavík for Valgeir Sigurðsson's label Bedroom Community, which also released music by Muhly and Ben Frost. It appeared in 2008 and placed him in an unlikely circle where American folk song met New York and Icelandic contemporary composition.","Muhly's arrangements often take a single phrase from the tune and turn it into a repeating pattern that keeps going after the voice stops, so minimalist technique emerges from the song itself. Amidon learned many of these songs from field recordings and from his parents rather than from records. Listen for the different speed of the singer and the ensemble: he often sings slightly behind the arranged pulse, keeping the old songs loose.","Reviews in indie and folk circles were warm, with critics admiring the strangeness of the pairing and the honesty of Amidon's singing. Some traditionalists found the arrangements too fussy; some art-music listeners found the voice too plain. The record is now valued as a pioneering meeting of folk song and contemporary composition, and as the start of Amidon's long reinvention of the American songbook.","Start with Saro and hear how long the ensemble takes to settle before the voice arrives. Listen to the arrangements as a separate piece of music. Compare Harry Smith's Anthology of American Folk Music for the kind of sources Amidon draws on, and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for a similar stillness. Then hear I See the Sign for the next step."],"sam-amidon-i-see-the-sign":["Old ballads, shape-note hymns, a spiritual and one pop cover, recast with livelier arrangements than on All Is Well. Amidon's flat, unfussy voice and banjo are joined by Nico Muhly's strings and brass, a rhythm section, and duets with the English singer Beth Orton. The title song is a jittery revival chant; Way Go Lily has a nursery-rhyme bounce; Relief, an R. Kelly song, is quietly turned into folk lament; Kedron is a shape-note hymn made eerie by its open harmonies.","Released by Bedroom Community in 2010 and recorded in Reykjavík with Valgeir Sigurðsson producing, it was the follow-up to All Is Well and widened the circle of collaborators. Muhly had meanwhile become one of the busiest young composers in New York. The record joined a growing current of folk musicians, from the Punch Brothers to Rhiannon Giddens, who treated American traditional music as a living repertoire rather than a museum.","Kedron comes from the Sacred Harp shape-note tradition, sung in rural Southern churches since the nineteenth century, and its bare fourths and fifths give it a medieval sound that Muhly's arrangement brings out. The R. Kelly cover is a deliberate test: by singing a contemporary R&B song with folk plainness, Amidon shows that the old tradition and pop belong to one continuum of song.","Critics liked the album's warmth and variety and many named it one of the most original folk records of 2010; some still found Amidon's deadpan delivery hard to love. It confirmed him as an influential, if cult, figure whose approach to traditional song inspired later experimental folk musicians, and is often the best introduction to his work.","Begin with the title track for energy, then Kedron for the shape-note eeriness, then Relief to hear an R&B song as an old ballad. Listen to Beth Orton's harmony on the duets. Compare Shirley Collins's Anthems in Eden, another bold dressing of old songs, and Sufjan Stevens's Illinois for related arrangements."],"the-weather-station-ignorance":["Ten songs in which Tamara Lindeman's soft, breathy, conversational voice floats over a band that pulses rather than strums: two drummers, a running bass, rippling piano and electric guitar, with flute, saxophone and strings drifting in and out. Robber opens with a nervous, propulsive groove; Atlantic is about watching the sea while thinking of catastrophe; Tried to Tell You is close to a pop song; Parking Lot and Heart are slower and more fragile. The sound recalls soft rock and jazz-inflected art pop more than acoustic folk.","Lindeman, a Toronto songwriter and former actor, had made four quieter folk albums as the Weather Station before this one, released by Fat Possum in February 2021 and co-produced with Marcus Paquin. She had become involved in climate activism, and the album was written out of grief and fear about the climate crisis and the struggle to feel and speak about it. It arrived during the pandemic's second winter and became her breakthrough.","The double drumming is the engine: two players lock into a light, steady grid, so the songs move like a nervous heartbeat beneath the calm vocal. Lindeman deliberately set personal, anxious lyrics against warm, almost seductive arrangements to mirror the way climate dread sits under ordinary life. Listen to the flute and saxophone as the record's weather, entering and receding without solos.","Critics greeted it as a major leap and among the best albums of 2021; it was shortlisted for the Polaris Music Prize in Canada. Some folk listeners missed the acoustic intimacy of her earlier work, and some found the climate theme stated too gently. It is now heard as one of the defining Canadian albums of its decade.","Begin with Robber and listen to the drums, then Atlantic. Notice how the voice never pushes, even when the band builds. Compare Joni Mitchell's The Hissing of Summer Lawns for jazzy, wide-eyed songwriting, and Radiohead's In Rainbows for anxious, beautiful grooves. Then go back to her earlier folk albums."],"lankum-the-livelong-day":["Traditional Irish and English songs and one or two originals, slowed to a crawl and stacked on enormous drones from uilleann pipes, harmonium, concertina and bowed strings, so that the music sounds closer to doom metal or drone music than to the pub session. Radie Peat's unadorned voice leads the slow ballads; the brothers Ian and Daragh Lynch take others. The Wild Rover, usually a drinking song, becomes a menacing dirge; Katie Cruel is stark; Hunting the Wren ends in a long, swirling instrumental.","Lankum, a Dublin quartet originally called Lynched, grew out of the city's squat and punk scenes as much as from traditional music. Produced by John Murphy and released on Rough Trade in October 2019, this third album was recorded in Dublin and drew wide acclaim in Ireland and Britain. It came as a young generation of Irish musicians, including Lisa O'Neill and Ye Vagabonds, were reclaiming traditional song in an Ireland transformed by boom and bust.","Listen to the drones themselves: the band layers pipes, harmonium and bowed instruments that are slightly out of tune with one another, so the overtones beat against each other and the sound seems to throb. Murphy recorded instruments in reverberant spaces and with close microphones together, so you hear breath and key clicks inside a vast sound. The Wild Rover, slowed and stripped of its chorus energy, reveals a song about poverty and regret.","Critics in Ireland and Britain hailed it as a radical reinvention of Irish folk, and it topped several lists. Some traditional players found the drones and darkness too heavy-handed. It prepared the ground for the wider breakthrough of False Lankum, and is now heard as a founding document of a darker, experimental Irish revival.","Start with The Wild Rover, ideally loud, and let the drone fill the room. Then listen to Katie Cruel. Compare Planxty's The Well Below the Valley for the classic Irish revival, then Swans' The Seer for the drone side. Notice how silence and decay are part of the music. Headphones help reveal the layers."],"lankum-false-lankum":["Over seventy minutes, traditional ballads and two originals stretched into slow, massive structures, separated by three short instrumental pieces called Fugue. Go Dig My Grave opens with Radie Peat's lone voice before drones, distortion and pounding percussion swallow it; The New York Trader is a ghostly sea ballad; Master Crowley's is a reel that disintegrates; The Turn is the band's own. The ending, The Turn, is huge, slow and cathartic. It is folk music played like experimental noise.","Released on Rough Trade in March 2023 and again produced by John Murphy in Dublin, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, bringing Lankum to international festival stages. The title plays on the Child ballad False Lankin, a song of murder and revenge, and on the band's own name. It appeared as Irish traditional music was attracting audiences who also followed doom, drone and ambient music.","The three Fugues are short studies in texture and processed sound rather than tunes, giving the ear somewhere to rest between the ballads. Listen to the long tails of reverb and decay, which Murphy treats as part of the arrangement rather than as ambience. Go Dig My Grave uses a ballad that also exists in American versions, linking Dublin to Appalachia across the Atlantic.","Reviews were rapturous, many calling it the year's best album in any genre; it reached audiences far beyond folk. A few listeners found the length and density daunting, and purists questioned how much tradition remained. It is now considered a landmark of twenty-first-century Irish music and of experimental folk anywhere.","Start with Go Dig My Grave and give it all of its length; the crescendo is the point. Then The New York Trader. Play loud or on good headphones. Compare Planxty's Cold Blow and the Rainy Night, a classic revival, with this, and Sunn O)))'s Monoliths and Dimensions for a cousin in drone. Then go back to The Livelong Day."],"sarah-jarosz-undercurrent":["Eleven songs, sparely arranged around Sarah Jarosz's clear alto and her guitar, octave mandolin or clawhammer banjo, with only bass and occasional percussion or a second voice behind her. The songs are written with folk economy and bluegrass precision, but the feel is intimate and reflective rather than virtuosic. House of Mercy rolls on a mandolin pattern; Green Lights is airy; Comin' Undone and Jacqueline are restless; Lost Dog closes in near silence. Nothing is busy; every note sounds chosen.","Jarosz grew up in Wimberley, Texas, a mandolin prodigy in the Austin bluegrass scene, signed to Sugar Hill while still in high school, and studied at the New England Conservatory. Released by Sugar Hill in June 2016 and produced with Gary Paczosa, this fourth album was her first after moving to New York and her first written largely alone. It won the Grammy Awards for Best Folk Album and, for House of Mercy, Best American Roots Performance.","Listen to the octave mandolin: it is tuned an octave below a standard mandolin, so it gives Jarosz a low, ringing voice somewhere between guitar and bouzouki. Several songs were co-written with friends from the Nashville and New York acoustic scenes but deliberately recorded with few players, the opposite of her more crowded early records. Her earlier album covered Joanna Newsom's Book of Right-On, a hint of her taste.","Critics praised the album's confident simplicity and her growth as a writer, and the Grammy wins brought her a larger audience. Some found it subdued compared with her bluegrass beginnings. It is now generally heard as the moment she became a songwriter first and an instrumentalist second, leading toward her trio I'm With Her and the Grammy-winning World on the Ground.","Start with House of Mercy and listen to the instrument pattern under the voice, then Green Lights and Lost Dog. Notice how much space there is. Compare Alison Krauss's So Long So Wrong for an earlier generation's precision, and Gillian Welch's Revival for duo spareness. Punch Brothers' records show where the instrumental side of her world goes."],"punch-brothers-punch":["The centrepiece is The Blind Leaving the Blind, a four-movement suite of about forty minutes for bluegrass quintet: mandolin, fiddle, banjo, guitar and bass, with Chris Thile singing lead. It moves through slow introductions, fast contrapuntal passages where each instrument has its own line, song sections and long instrumental developments, much closer to a string quartet than to a bluegrass band. A few shorter songs frame it. The sound is acoustic and dry, every instrument distinct, the playing ferociously exact.","Punch Brothers formed in 2006 around Chris Thile, the mandolin prodigy of Nickel Creek, with Gabe Witcher, Noam Pikelny, Chris Eldridge and Greg Garrison. Thile wrote the suite while coming through a divorce, and Nonesuch released the album in 2008. The band was openly aiming to bring chamber-music thinking into bluegrass, as Thile also recorded Bach on mandolin and later received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2012.","The suite uses recurring motifs: a phrase from the first movement returns, transformed, in later movements, the way themes work across a classical symphony. Listeners have long heard Bach's partitas and Bartók's string quartets behind it, and Thile has recorded Bach on mandolin. Listen to the banjo: Pikelny often plays sustained, melodic lines instead of rolling bluegrass patterns, so the instrument behaves more like a harp or a piano.","Critics and musicians were impressed, and the suite was widely discussed as a breakthrough for acoustic music, though some found it more admirable than lovable and wondered whether it was bluegrass at all. It is now seen as a founding document of progressive acoustic chamber music in America, and the launch pad for one of the most technically celebrated bands in any genre.","Hear the suite in one sitting, as you would a chamber piece. Follow one instrument, for example the fiddle, through a movement, then listen again following the banjo. Compare Bach's Violin Partita No. 2 and Bartók's String Quartet No. 4 for the models, and Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky to measure how far bluegrass travelled."],"punch-brothers-the-phosphorescent-blues":["An album about how people connect in the age of the smartphone, built on the band's all-acoustic quintet but sounding as layered as an art-rock record. Familiarity opens with a ten-minute, multi-part song that swings from hushed choral harmony to driving rock; Julep and My Oh My are tuneful and wistful; two pieces are arrangements of piano works by Debussy and Scriabin; I Blew It Off is jaunty. Vocal harmonies are lush, close and often wordless, and the playing remains dazzlingly exact.","Released by Nonesuch in January 2015 and produced by T Bone Burnett, who had worked with members of the band on film soundtracks. By now the line-up had settled with Paul Kowert on bass. The band were touring large venues and their ambitions had widened from bluegrass to art rock, with Radiohead's influence openly acknowledged. Burnett's production gave them a warm, enveloping sound.","Passepied is an arrangement of a movement from Debussy's Suite bergamasque, the same suite that includes Clair de lune, for mandolin, fiddle, banjo, guitar and bass, and it shows how naturally impressionist harmony lies on bluegrass instruments. Familiarity's wordless chorale was built from stacked vocal lines in the studio. Listen for the banjo and mandolin playing short repeated cells in the minimalist manner.","Critics praised its ambition and beauty; it reached high on folk and Americana charts and many year-end lists, though some reviewers found its preoccupation with technology dated or heavy-handed. It is now widely heard as the band's most complete album and the clearest statement of what progressive acoustic music could be in the twenty-first century.","Start with Familiarity and stay for its whole ten minutes, then hear Passepied and play Debussy's Clair de lune afterwards for the source. Listen to the vocal harmonies in Julep. Compare Radiohead's In Rainbows for the art-rock side, and Steve Reich's Music for Pieces of Wood for the minimalist patterns. Punch is their earlier, more austere suite."],"jake-blount-spider-tales":["Old-time fiddle tunes, ballads, blues and spirituals, all drawn from the music of Black and Indigenous Americans, played with deep, driving rhythm on fiddle and banjo by Jake Blount and a small circle of guests. His voice is rich and unhurried; the fiddle saws with the insistent drive of a dance hall. Some tracks are stripped to fiddle and voice, others build to full string-band momentum. The playing is traditional in style but the selection makes a pointed, coherent statement.","Blount, a Black and queer banjo player and fiddler who studied ethnomusicology, released this debut on Free Dirt Records in May 2020, weeks before the George Floyd protests. He had spent years researching the forgotten Black roots of old-time music, a scene long presented as mainly white and rural. The title refers to the trickster spider Anansi of West African and Caribbean stories, who survives by wit, a figure for how this music survived erasure.","Each song was chosen from a documented Black or Indigenous source, many from field recordings by players whose names appear only in archives, so the album is also a work of research. Listen to the rhythm of the fiddle bow, which emphasises off-beats in a style that traces back to African-American dance fiddling and through it to West African string traditions. The liner notes give every song's origin.","Reviews were very strong in folk and roots circles and the album appeared on many 2020 year-end lists; critics praised both the playing and the argument. A few noted that the austere sound might limit its reach. It is now considered an important record in the reclamation of Black string-band music, in a line with the Carolina Chocolate Drops and Rhiannon Giddens.","Listen first for the fiddle's rhythm, then the voice. Pick the songs that are only fiddle and voice and hear how little is needed. Pair with the Carolina Chocolate Drops' Genuine Negro Jig and with Lead Belly's Last Sessions for the older sources. Read the liner notes alongside; they turn the album into a lesson in history."],"jake-blount-the-new-faith":["A concept album of spirituals and old religious songs, imagined as the hymns of survivors in a future ravaged by climate change. Banjo, fiddle and Blount's voice sit alongside drum machines, distorted bass, synthesizers and choral overdubs, with rap verses and guest singers. Some songs stay close to traditional performance; others are rebuilt as stark electronic soundscapes. The effect is of folk music heard through a broken radio from the future, both ancient and brand new.","Released by Smithsonian Folkways in September 2022, the label that holds much of the archive of American traditional music, this was a striking choice: a folk album framed as Afrofuturist speculation. Blount described a story in which displaced Black people in a climate-ravaged future preserve their faith through old spirituals. It came as Black musicians across genres were turning to science fiction to imagine survival.","Many songs were taken from early twentieth-century recordings of Black congregational singing, and Blount kept their call-and-response structure even when the sound is electronic. Listen for field-recording textures and sampled crowd noise used as a choir from the past. The project quietly argues that the spirituals were themselves a kind of future-oriented music, written by people imagining freedom they could not yet see.","Critics praised the concept's daring and the album received strong reviews and folk awards attention; a few traditionalists found the electronic treatment jarring, and some reviewers felt the narrative was hard to follow from the music alone. It is now seen as one of the most adventurous American folk records of its decade.","Start with the most traditional-sounding tracks and then move to the most electronic, listening for the old call and response beneath. Read the story Blount supplies with the record. Compare Sam Amidon's I See the Sign for another experimental take on hymns, and Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome for spirituals as songs of struggle."],"bonny-light-horseman-bonny-light-horseman":["Ten traditional Anglo-Irish and American folk songs, rewritten and rearranged by a trio: Anaïs Mitchell and Eric D. Johnson share lead vocals and harmonies over Josh Kaufman's guitars and a soft, drum-led band. The title song, a ballad of a woman mourning a soldier lost in the Napoleonic wars, opens slowly and swells; Deep in Love is a lovely duet; The Roving and Magpie's Nest are lighter. The sound is warm, unhurried and close to 1970s folk rock, with hints of Fleet Foxes harmony.","The trio first performed together at the Eaux Claires festival run by Bon Iver's Justin Vernon, and the album was released on Vernon's associated label 37d03d in January 2020, partly recorded during a residency in Berlin. Mitchell had just seen Hadestown open on Broadway, and Johnson led the indie band Fruit Bats. The record was nominated for the Grammy Award for Best Folk Album.","Many of the songs were altered: the band added verses, changed melodies or combined fragments of different versions, so this is a new composition from old material, in the way folk singers have always worked. Listen to the title song's source, a ballad associated with Ireland's long tradition of songs about soldiers who did not return. Mitchell and Johnson's voices are balanced so neither dominates.","Critics welcomed it warmly as a graceful renewal of folk-rock and a fine showcase for Mitchell's voice outside theatre; a few found it too smooth or polite for songs about loss and violence. It has become a staple of the Americana-folk scene, and the band continued with further albums.","Start with the title song, then Deep in Love. Listen to how the two voices trade lines. Compare Fairport Convention's Liege & Lief and Planxty's The Well Below the Valley for the classic folk-rock treatment of old songs, and Fleet Foxes' first album for the harmonies. Then hear Anaïs Mitchell's 2022 solo record for the same players."],"adrianne-lenker-songs-and-instrumentals":["Two albums released together. songs is eleven tracks for voice and acoustic guitar alone, recorded in a wooden cabin so that rain, wind and birds can be heard around the music: Two Reverse, Anything, Zombie Girl, Ingydar and Forwards Beckon Rebound are full of lucid, tender detail. instrumentals is two long pieces, music for indigo and mostly chimes, the first a meandering guitar improvisation, the second built from wind chimes and the room. It is about as close and unadorned as recorded folk can be.","When the pandemic cancelled Big Thief's 2020 tour, Lenker retreated to a one-room cabin in western Massachusetts and recorded with the engineer Philip Weinrobe on analog tape, after the end of a relationship. 4AD released the double album in October 2020. It came after Big Thief's two 2019 albums and became her most celebrated solo work, deepening the mythology of her as a songwriter of rare openness.","Weinrobe recorded to tape with simple equipment and made no attempt to seal out the world beyond the walls. Listen to the outdoors: rain on the roof on some songs, wind chimes and birds on others, so the place is the album's other instrument. Some songs here were later reworked by Big Thief, showing how Lenker's solo writing feeds the band.","Reviews were rapturous, critics calling songs one of the year's best albums and praising its emotional honesty; some found the instrumentals self-indulgent. The album has come to symbolise the hushed, intimate recordings of the pandemic year, and many listeners regard it as the purest expression of Lenker's art.","Listen to songs late at night, quietly, in order. Start with Anything and Zombie Girl and listen for the room around them. Save instrumentals for a long walk or a still hour. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for solitude and guitar, and Big Thief's U.F.O.F. for how her songs grow in the band. Bon Iver's For Emma, Forever Ago is another cabin record."],"adrianne-lenker-bright-future":["Twelve songs recorded live by Lenker with three friends, Nick Hakim, Mat Davidson and Josefin Runsteen, around a single room: guitars, an upright piano, fiddle and voices. Real House opens with a slow, bare piano and a memory of childhood; Sadness as a Gift is a gentle country song; Free Treasure and Vampire Empire are older songs reworked; Ruined and Donut Seam are tender; Evol plays with language. The playing sounds unrehearsed, with mistakes and laughter left in.","4AD released the album in March 2024. It was recorded at a studio deep in the woods, again with engineer Philip Weinrobe and onto analog tape, the four musicians playing together without headphones. Lenker made it as Big Thief's profile was growing, and the band's bassist Max Oleartchik left soon after; the solo record served as a quieter counterweight to the band's large, celebrated albums.","Vampire Empire had been played live by Big Thief for some time; here it is slowed and softened, so the same song shows two different characters. Listen for the background: the performers react to one another, a piano chair creaks, voices join late. Runsteen's fiddle often doubles the melody slightly ahead or behind, giving an old-time string-band roughness to otherwise intimate songs.","Critics praised its warmth and the communal feel; the album placed on many year-end lists, though some found it less striking than songs and instrumentals. It has been embraced as an affectionate, communal companion to her solitary earlier work, with Real House widely singled out as one of her finest songs.","Start with Real House and listen to how the piano and voice sit in the room. Then hear Sadness as a Gift and Vampire Empire. Notice the humanity of the performance, the breath and stray sounds. Compare songs and instrumentals for the solo version of her voice, and Gillian Welch's Time (The Revelator) for live, close-knit acoustic intimacy."],"nick-mulvey-first-mind":["Songs built on intricate fingerpicked guitar patterns that owe as much to West African and Cuban music as to English folk, sung in a soft, low voice. The guitar often plays repeating cross-rhythms in which melody and accompaniment interlock; light percussion and bass add pulse, and occasional synthesizer colours deepen the sound. Cucurucu is bright and hypnotic; Fever to the Form and Juramidam are warmer and more expansive. The record's calm, rippling surface hides complicated rhythmic design.","Mulvey, from Cambridge, studied ethnomusicology at SOAS in London, spent time in Havana, and was a founding member of the Mercury-nominated Portico Quartet, playing the hang drum. Leaving the group, he became a singer-songwriter, and this debut was released on Fiction Records in May 2014. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and arrived amid a British folk scene that was looking beyond Anglo-American roots.","Listen for polyrhythm: many of his guitar patterns set three against two or four, a technique he learned from West African kora and guitar traditions, so the songs seem to move at two speeds at once. His years playing the hang drum, a melodic steel percussion instrument, show in the way the guitar patterns sound like bells. Juramidam's title refers to a Brazilian spiritual tradition.","Critics welcomed it warmly, praising the guitar writing and the global influences, and the Mercury nomination widened his audience; some reviewers found the songs too gentle and the lyrics vague. It is now remembered as one of the more musically curious British folk debuts of its decade.","Start with Cucurucu and try to tap the two rhythms in the guitar at once. Then hear Fever to the Form. Compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for interlocking patterns, and Nick Drake's Bryter Layter for English fingerpicking. Then seek out West African guitar records to hear the deeper roots."],"julia-jacklin-crushing":["Ten songs about the aftermath of a breakup and the right to one's own body and space, sung in a clear, steady voice that rarely raises itself. The arrangements are sparse indie-folk: chiming electric guitar, brushed or tight drums, and much empty space. Body opens over a single guitar line; Pressure to Party is the one fast, defiant rocker; Don't Know How to Keep Loving You and Comfort are quietly devastating ballads. The restraint is the force; nothing is overplayed.","Jacklin grew up in the Blue Mountains near Sydney and released her debut in 2016. This second album, released in February 2019 by Polyvinyl and Transgressive and produced by Burke Reid in Sydney, was written after two years of touring and the end of a long relationship. It arrived amid wider conversations about consent and personal boundaries, and several songs speak to them directly but quietly.","Listen to the drums: on several songs they enter only halfway through, changing the meaning of what came before. The vocal is placed very close and dry, as if the singer were standing her ground in the same room as the listener. Comfort, the last song, is sung to a former partner and turns the album's focus from her own hurt to care for someone else.","Reviews were excellent and the album appeared on many year-end lists, especially in Australia and Britain; critics praised its emotional precision. A few found the mood too uniform. It established Jacklin as one of the leading singer-songwriters of her generation, often grouped with Phoebe Bridgers and Big Thief.","Start with Body and Don't Know How to Keep Loving You, then hear Pressure to Party for contrast. Listen to the silences between lines. Compare Phoebe Bridgers's Stranger in the Alps and Big Thief's U.F.O.F. for the shared sound, and Joni Mitchell's Blue for the tradition of plain, unflinching confession."],"wynton-marsalis-black-codes-from-the-underground":["A quintet record built on the acoustic language of the mid-1960s, played with frightening precision. Wynton Marsalis's trumpet leads his brother Branford on tenor and soprano saxophone, with Kenny Kirkland on piano, Charnett Moffett on bass and Jeff \"Tain\" Watts on drums. The tunes, among them the title track, Phryzzinian Man and Chambers of Tain, lean on shifting meters and tempos that speed up and slow down by agreement, so the band seems to stretch and compress time while the soloists keep their footing.","In 1985 jazz on major labels meant fusion, smooth radio sounds or reissues of older acoustic records. Marsalis, a New Orleans prodigy who had come through Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers and was also recording Haydn and Hummel trumpet concertos for Columbia, argued loudly for swing, the blues and acoustic instruments as the core of the music. The title points to the laws passed across the South after the Civil War to restrict the freedom of Black Americans, a sign that he heard the music as history as well as style.","Listen to Watts and Kirkland more than to the horns. The rhythm section is quoting the elastic playing of the Miles Davis quintet of 1965 to 1968, where tempo and pulse are bargained over bar by bar, but Watts adds a heavier, more muscular backbeat of his own. On several tracks the time feel changes under a soloist without warning, and the soloist has to follow it while carrying on with the line.","The album won two Grammy awards in 1986 and made Marsalis the public face of a young-lions revival. Critics split over it then and still do: admirers heard real command and a corrective to commercial fusion, while detractors heard a gifted player re-enacting the Davis quintet rather than moving past it. Many now hear the record as the strongest work of that argument, and Kirkland and Watts as its unsung engine.","Start with the title track and keep your ear on the drums: count the moments the pulse seems to slip and then re-lock. Then play the second Davis quintet's mid-sixties records beside it and listen for what Branford's saxophone does differently from Wayne Shorter's. Any remaster of the original Columbia LP will do; the sound is clean, close and dry."],"wynton-marsalis-blood-on-the-fields":["An oratorio for big band and three singers, nearly three hours long and issued on three discs. It follows two Africans, Jesse and Leona, through capture, the Middle Passage and enslavement toward a hard-won freedom, with Cassandra Wilson as Leona, Miles Griffith as Jesse and Jon Hendricks as Juba, an older man who guides them. The writing moves through work songs, blues, marches, ballads, shout choruses and dense ensemble passages, each section built from a different strand of the Black musical past.","Marsalis wrote it for Jazz at Lincoln Center, the institution he had co-founded in 1987 to give jazz the repertory standing of an orchestra or opera house. It was first performed in 1994 and recorded the following year. In 1997 it became the first jazz composition to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music, a prize that had famously been refused to Duke Ellington in 1965. That history gave the award a weight well beyond this one piece.","Marsalis maps each stage of the story onto a historical idiom: the scenes on the ship and in the field lean on field hollers and call and response, while the later scenes borrow from swing-era ensemble writing and Ellington's sound for reeds. The journey of the characters is therefore also a walk through the history of the music itself, ending in the language of the big band Marsalis was leading.","The Pulitzer brought praise and resentment in equal measure. Supporters heard a serious extended work in the line of Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige; skeptics found it long, uneven and more summary than discovery, with a libretto some thought heavy-handed. It has been revived by the Lincoln Center orchestra but is rarely heard complete, and its reputation now rests as much on its symbolic place as on the score.","Do not try it in one sitting. Take one disc at a time, follow the story in the booklet, and listen for how Cassandra Wilson's dark, unhurried voice changes the temperature whenever she enters. Then play Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige to hear the model Marsalis was answering."],"cassandra-wilson-blue-light-til-dawn":["A quiet, acoustic record that sounds as if it were made on a porch at night. Cassandra Wilson sings low and slow over spare acoustic guitars, hand percussion, violin and the occasional cornet, with no piano and no drum kit swinging in the usual jazz sense. The songs come from everywhere: Robert Johnson's Hellhound on My Trail and Come On in My Kitchen, Joni Mitchell's Black Crow, Van Morrison's Tupelo Honey, Ann Peebles's I Can't Stand the Rain, and a handful of her own pieces.","Wilson had spent the 1980s in Brooklyn among the M-Base circle of Steve Coleman, recording angular, funk-driven records for the German label JMT. Signing to Blue Note in 1993, she worked with the producer Craig Street, who stripped the sound back to wood and strings. The guitarist Brandon Ross and the cornetist Olu Dara were among the players. The result reached listeners far beyond jazz and helped set a template for the roots-minded jazz singing of the next decade.","Listen to how slow everything is. Wilson often sings well behind the beat and lets phrases trail off into silence, so that the songs from the Delta, the 1970s singer-songwriter shelf and Memphis soul all end up sharing one tempo and one mood. The blues numbers are not treated as relics; the guitars play them in open, ringing tunings that point to Mississippi, where Wilson grew up.","Reviewers welcomed it at once, and it sold strongly for a jazz vocal record. Some jazz purists asked whether an album with so little swing and so much pop and blues repertoire belonged in the category at all. That debate now looks dated: the record is widely treated as one of the defining jazz vocal albums of its decade, and as the moment Wilson found her own sound.","Start with Hellhound on My Trail and then play the Robert Johnson original: notice how she keeps the menace but slows it to a crawl. Then try Black Crow beside Joni Mitchell's Hejira. Play the album late and quietly; it rewards a room with the lights down."],"cassandra-wilson-new-moon-daughter":["The second Wilson record with the producer Craig Street, darker and more varied than the first. It opens with Strange Fruit, sung almost without inflection over a bed of low drones, and moves through Son House's Death Letter, Hank Williams's I'm So Lonesome I Could Cry, Neil Young's Harvest Moon, U2's Love Is Blindness and even the Monkees' Last Train to Clarksville, alongside originals such as Solomon Sang. Pedal steel, acoustic guitar and cornet weave around her slow contralto.","After the surprise success of Blue Light 'til Dawn, Blue Note backed Wilson in carrying the approach further, and she treated rock and country songs as material as open to reinterpretation as anything by Gershwin. The guitarist Brandon Ross returned, with the cornetist Graham Haynes among the guests. The album won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Performance and confirmed her as the most widely heard jazz singer of the mid-1990s.","The Monkees song is the giveaway. Wilson slows a bright pop single into a brooding, almost menacing blues, showing how much of a song's meaning lives in tempo and arrangement rather than in the tune. Her Strange Fruit, by contrast, refuses the dramatic build most singers bring to it; the horror is carried by restraint, and by the sour drone that never resolves.","It was praised on release and won its Grammy, though some critics felt the formula of the first record was being repeated and that a few covers were more clever than deep. Over time its Strange Fruit and Death Letter have been singled out as among her finest recorded performances, and the album sits beside its predecessor as a pair.","Begin with Death Letter and then play the Son House recording: one man shouting over a slide guitar against a singer who barely raises her voice. Then hear Strange Fruit beside Billie Holiday's 1939 version and ask which unsettles you more. Listen on good speakers; the low end matters."],"joe-lovano-from-the-soul":["A quartet record on which an unusually broad, breathy tenor saxophone sound meets three very different partners: the French pianist Michel Petrucciani, the bassist Dave Holland and the New Orleans drummer Ed Blackwell. Lovano's tone is the first thing you notice, a big, furry, slightly husky sound that bends pitches and slides between notes, and his lines move freely between straight-ahead swing, open-ended improvising and tender ballad playing.","Lovano, from Cleveland, had spent years as a sideman with Woody Herman, Mel Lewis's big band and the trio of the drummer Paul Motian with Bill Frisell. Signed by Blue Note around 1990 in his late thirties, he became one of the label's flagship players of the decade. Blackwell, a long-time partner of Ornette Coleman, was gravely ill with kidney disease at the time, and this was among his last recordings before his death in 1992.","Listen to Blackwell. His drumming comes out of New Orleans parade rhythms, and he tunes his kit so that each drum sings a distinct pitch; you can follow his solos almost as melodies. Against him Petrucciani plays crisp, bright, classically trained piano, and Holland holds the middle. Lovano's tenor seems to borrow a little from each, which is why the record feels like a conversation rather than a saxophonist with accompaniment.","Critics welcomed it as proof that Lovano was a major voice rather than a well-schooled journeyman, and it helped launch a run of acclaimed Blue Note albums. It is sometimes overshadowed by his later, more ambitious projects, but players often name it as the cleanest portrait of his sound.","Start with any ballad and listen to the edges of Lovano's notes, the air and grain around them. Then play a track with Blackwell at the front and follow his drums as if they were a second horn. Compare with Ornette Coleman's quartet recordings to hear Blackwell in his original home."],"joe-lovano-rush-hour":["Lovano's tenor saxophone set inside chamber-orchestral writing by Gunther Schuller, who arranged and conducted. Some tracks are small and spare, others full ensemble pieces in which strings, woodwinds and brass weave independent lines around the saxophone rather than simply padding beneath it. Judi Silvano adds wordless vocal lines on some pieces. The repertoire mixes jazz-composer tunes with Schuller's own scores, and the overall mood moves between urban bustle and nocturnal calm.","Schuller had coined the phrase third stream in 1957 for music that joined jazz improvisation to classical composition, and by the 1990s he was a senior figure: composer, conductor, horn player, historian and teacher. Pairing him with Lovano was Blue Note's way of reviving that idea for a new generation. Lovano, the son of a Cleveland tenor player, had a lifelong interest in orchestral colour and welcomed the challenge of improvising inside fully written scores.","Listen to how often Schuller writes a counter-melody for a single instrument, a bassoon or a solo violin, that shadows Lovano's line a beat behind. The orchestra is not a cushion; it is a second improviser in disguise, and in places it is hard to tell which notes were written and which were made up on the spot.","Reviews were warm, and many critics named it among the year's best jazz records. Some felt the orchestral settings softened Lovano's edge; others thought it the most convincing third-stream album in decades precisely because neither side gives way. It is now heard as a high point of the 1990s attempts to reconcile jazz and concert music.","Choose a track with full ensemble and follow one orchestral instrument the whole way through rather than the saxophone. Then go back and hear how Lovano reacts to it. Compare Schuller's own third-stream work, or Miles Davis and Gil Evans's Miles Ahead, for an older version of the same marriage."],"dave-holland-extensions":["A quartet record without piano, in which Steve Coleman's alto saxophone and Kevin Eubanks's electric guitar trade lines over Holland's bass and the drums of Marvin \"Smitty\" Smith. The pieces run in long, lopsided cycles of beats and lock into grooves that feel like funk but refuse to settle into four. Holland's bass is the centre of gravity: a fat, woody sound that carries both the harmony and the hook.","Holland, born in Wolverhampton, England, had played in Miles Davis's band around 1968 to 1970 and in the free-improvising group Circle before making his name as a leader on ECM in the 1970s. By the late 1980s he was working with younger New York musicians from the circle around Coleman's M-Base collective, whose interest in odd meters and dense rhythmic cycles fed into his writing. Extensions was recorded at the end of that decade.","Count along to any tune and you will usually lose your place; Holland writes riffs that run in unusual lengths and lets the drums cut across them, so that the groove seems to tilt. Eubanks, later famous as a television bandleader, plays some of his most adventurous guitar here, using a clean, almost acoustic tone that blends with the alto rather than overpowering it.","It was widely praised on release and is often named among the best ECM jazz records of its period. It has sometimes been eclipsed by Holland's 1972 debut, Conference of the Birds, but many listeners prefer this record's tighter rhythmic language, which points ahead to his celebrated quintet of the late 1990s.","Put on the opening track and follow only the bass for a full minute, then switch your attention to the drums. Notice how the two seem to argue and still agree. Then play Holland's Prime Directive to hear where this approach led ten years later."],"dave-holland-prime-directive":["Holland's quintet at full strength: Chris Potter on saxophones, Robin Eubanks on trombone, Steve Nelson on vibraphone and marimba, Billy Kilson on drums, and Holland on bass. Instead of piano, Nelson's shimmering vibraphone fills in the harmony, which gives the group a lighter, ringing sound. The tunes run in odd meters, but the band plays them with such ease that they sound like dance music, building toward long collective climaxes in which saxophone and trombone trade riffs.","Holland formed the quintet in the late 1990s, and over the next several years it became one of the most admired working bands in jazz, winning polls and touring constantly. ECM, a label associated with spacious, reflective music, gave it room to record a series of albums. Potter, then in his late twenties, emerged from this group as one of the leading tenor saxophonists of his generation.","Listen to the backgrounds behind each soloist. Holland writes interlocking riffs for the other horns and the vibraphone that come in partway through a solo, so the music thickens as it goes rather than staying a solo with rhythm section. The meters are often in fives, sevens or longer cycles, but Kilson plays them with a backbeat you can nod to.","Critics greeted it with enthusiasm and the quintet was voted among the best groups of the period in jazz polls. Some found it polished to a fault, a model of ensemble craft rather than of risk. Musicians, however, still study these records as textbooks of how to write odd-meter tunes that groove.","Start with the title track and tap your foot; when you lose count, listen to the bass, which always shows you where the cycle begins. Then follow the vibraphone behind a saxophone solo. Afterwards compare Extensions from 1990 to hear the earlier, guitar-led version of Holland's rhythmic ideas."],"john-zorn-naked-city":["A record that cuts between surf twang, film-noir cool, country picking, lounge jazz and short bursts of hardcore noise, sometimes within seconds. Zorn on alto saxophone leads Bill Frisell on guitar, Wayne Horvitz on keyboards, Fred Frith on bass and Joey Baron on drums, with the Japanese vocalist Yamatsuka Eye screaming on some tracks. Alongside originals, the band plays film and television themes by Ennio Morricone, Henry Mancini and John Barry, and Ornette Coleman's Lonely Woman.","Zorn was the central figure of the New York downtown scene, a loose network of improvisers, rock musicians and composers gathered around Lower Manhattan clubs such as the Knitting Factory. He had been developing game pieces that directed improvisers with cue cards, and he loved cartoon music, Japanese hardcore and spaghetti westerns. The band took its name and its cover image from the crime photographer Weegee's book Naked City.","Listen to the edits. Many tracks change style at a single drum hit, and the band does it live, not by splicing tape; Zorn cues the cuts with hand signals in the manner of his game pieces. The short hardcore numbers, some under a minute, are the record's real experiment: noise played with the discipline of chamber music.","It confused many jazz critics, who heard parody and racket, and delighted others, who heard a new kind of composition built from the clutter of mass media. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now regarded as a landmark of the downtown aesthetic and of genre-collapsing music in general.","Start with the Morricone and Mancini covers to get your bearings, then try a run of the short noise pieces at volume. Hear the band's Lonely Woman beside Ornette Coleman's 1959 original and notice what survives the assault. Afterwards, play anything by the Boredoms to hear where Eye came from."],"john-zorn-masada-alef":["The first album by Masada, a piano-less acoustic quartet: Zorn on alto saxophone, Dave Douglas on trumpet, Greg Cohen on bass and Joey Baron on drums. The tunes are short, tightly written melodies in the scales of Jewish liturgical and klezmer music, with their characteristic augmented intervals, launched into fast, free-swinging improvisation. The sound is lean and urgent: two horns in unison or close harmony, then breaking apart as Baron drives the band forward.","In 1993 Zorn began writing a book of short tunes he called Masada, setting himself the task of creating a body of explicitly Jewish music in the 1990s, partly in response to what he and others in downtown New York called Radical Jewish Culture. The book eventually ran to hundreds of pieces. The quartet's first record was released by the Japanese label DIW, which was releasing many of Zorn's projects at the time.","The instrumentation is a deliberate tribute to Ornette Coleman's quartet of 1959, with Douglas in Don Cherry's role and Baron playing with Billy Higgins's lightness. Listen to how the two horns often end a tune on a long, unresolved interval rather than a cadence. The Jewish modes and the Ornette model fit together surprisingly well because both put melody before chord changes.","Masada was quickly recognised as one of Zorn's most accessible and durable projects, and the quartet recorded prolifically through the decade. Some listeners questioned the identity politics around it; others simply heard great tunes and one of the most exciting small bands of the 1990s. The songbook has since been played by dozens of other groups.","Start with any short tune and listen for the opening melody in unison; then follow how quickly the band unravels it. Play Ornette Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come next to hear the model, and Naftule Brandwein's klezmer clarinet to hear the other root."],"bill-frisell-have-a-little-faith":["A tour of American music played by a quintet of guitar, clarinet, accordion, bass and drums. Frisell takes on Aaron Copland's Billy the Kid, music by Charles Ives and John Philip Sousa, Stephen Foster, Muddy Waters, Sonny Rollins, Bob Dylan's Just Like a Woman, John Hiatt and Madonna's Live to Tell, treating all of it with the same gentle seriousness. His guitar, swelled and echoed by pedals, can sound like a pedal steel or a church organ.","Frisell, raised in Denver and trained on clarinet before guitar, had become a fixture of the New York downtown scene and of John Zorn's projects. With Nonesuch, a label that also issued contemporary classical music, he began to explore Americana in a broad sense. The band here included Don Byron on clarinet, Guy Klucevsek on accordion, Kermit Driscoll on bass and Joey Baron on drums.","Listen to how he treats Copland and Madonna with exactly the same affection, lingering on a melody's turns and letting the band hold chords longer than expected. The accordion and clarinet give the record an old-time, small-town sound, which makes Copland's cowboy ballet feel as if it were being played on a bandstand in a park.","Reviewers greeted it with delight and some puzzlement about its pop selections. It is now seen as the turning point at which Frisell moved from downtown experimentalist to the patient interpreter of American song he became, and as a forerunner of the broader jazz-Americana movement.","Play the Copland sequence first, then the Madonna, and notice how the band treats them as equals. Compare Copland's own orchestral Billy the Kid to hear what Frisell keeps and what he strips away, and listen for how the accordion takes over the strings' role. The original Nonesuch CD sounds warm and natural."],"bill-frisell-nashville":["Frisell's electric guitar among Nashville acoustic players, including the dobro master Jerry Douglas and the bassist Viktor Krauss. Drums are mostly absent, so the music floats on plucked strings: banjo rolls, mandolin chop and the slide of the dobro around Frisell's gentle, reverberant lines. The tunes, mostly his own with a few covers, are short, plain-spoken melodies that sound like old country songs heard in a dream.","After years of working in New York with improvisers, Frisell, who had moved to Seattle, went to Nashville to record with bluegrass and country session musicians who had no interest in jazz conventions. Nonesuch backed the project. It was part of a wider 1990s turn among American jazz musicians toward roots music, and Frisell treated it as a meeting of equals rather than a jazz player borrowing a flavour.","Listen for the moments when no one solos. The players trade small phrases and fills in the manner of a bluegrass jam, but Frisell slows the tempos so far that the fast picking style becomes a shimmering texture. His guitar often enters with the attack removed, the note swelling up from nothing, which makes him sound like a ghost among the acoustic players.","It was named album of the year in at least one major jazz critics' poll, which provoked debate about whether a record with almost no swing or jazz harmony belonged in the category. That argument has faded; it is now seen as a gentle classic and a key text of jazz Americana.","Put it on in the morning and do not wait for solos; listen to how the instruments fit together like a quilt. Then play Bill Monroe or the Stanley Brothers to hear the tradition Frisell is visiting, and Have a Little Faith to hear where the journey began."],"vijay-iyer-historicity":["A piano trio record that sounds less like a cocktail lounge than a machine room. Iyer, with Stephan Crump on bass and Marcus Gilmore on drums, plays tight, repeating figures that lock into grooves of unusual length and gradually shift. The repertoire mixes his own pieces with Stevie Wonder's Big Brother, M.I.A.'s Galang, Leonard Bernstein's Somewhere, Andrew Hill's Smoke Stack and Julius Hemphill's Dogon A.D., all reworked into the trio's dense rhythmic language.","Iyer, born in upstate New York to Indian Tamil parents, studied physics and wrote a doctoral thesis on music cognition and rhythm in the body before committing to music. He had worked with Steve Coleman and in the cooperative trio Fieldwork. This was the first album with this trio, issued by the German label ACT, and it earned a Grammy nomination and topped many year-end lists.","Listen to the hip-hop and pop covers closely: Iyer keeps their bass lines and rhythmic hooks rather than their melodies, turning a Stevie Wonder song or an M.I.A. track into a loop that the trio bends and stretches. Gilmore, grandson of the drummer Roy Haynes, plays the drums like a sampler, repeating tiny patterns with small changes each time.","The album was among the most praised jazz records of 2009 and established Iyer as a leading figure in a new generation. Some traditionalists found the trio too cerebral and short on lyrical melody; admirers heard a fresh approach to the oldest format in modern jazz, drawn from hip-hop and minimalism as much as from bebop.","Start with Galang or Big Brother, and listen to the bass and drums before the piano; notice how the loop slowly mutates. Then play Andrew Hill and Julius Hemphill to hear Iyer's jazz ancestors, and Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians to hear his minimalist cousin."],"vijay-iyer-accelerando":["The second record by Iyer's trio with Stephan Crump and Marcus Gilmore, built around the idea of music for dancing. The covers include Michael Jackson's Human Nature, Flying Lotus's MmmHmm, Henry Threadgill's Little Pocket Size Demons and Herbie Nichols's Wild Flower, alongside originals whose grooves gradually speed up, overlap or slip against each other. The sound is lighter and more springy than on Historicity, with more space between the three players.","Iyer described the album in terms of dance and the body, drawing on his research into how people feel rhythm physically. By 2012 he was a widely honoured figure, and the record swept major jazz critics' polls that year. His choice of material, from a Los Angeles beat producer to an AACM elder, mapped the wide range of what jazz musicians of his generation listened to.","The title is a classical tempo marking meaning getting faster, and several pieces use metric modulation, a technique in which a subdivision of the old beat becomes the new beat, so the music seems to accelerate without anyone rushing. Threadgill's tune is especially knotty; listen to how the trio keeps its odd, lopsided phrase lengths intact.","Critics praised it as warmer and more open than its predecessor, and it was widely named among the best jazz records of 2012. Some still found Iyer's music more interesting in concept than in feeling, a criticism he has long faced. The record is now seen as the peak of this trio's work.","Start with Human Nature and notice how close the trio stays to Michael Jackson's gentle groove. Then play Little Pocket Size Demons and compare Threadgill's own recordings, listening for the lopsided phrase lengths. Finish with the Flying Lotus original to hear how a beat track becomes trio music."],"jason-moran-modernistic":["A solo piano album that ranges across a century of music. Moran plays James P. Johnson's stride showpiece You've Got to Be Modernistic, a song from Robert Schumann's Liederkreis, Afrika Bambaataa's electro anthem Planet Rock and his own pieces, including entries in his ongoing Gangsterism series. He moves between thundering stride left hand, delicate classical voicings and percussive, hip-hop-style repetition, sometimes in a single piece.","Moran, from Houston, studied with the pianist Jaki Byard and later with Andrew Hill and Muhal Richard Abrams, and signed to Blue Note in his early twenties. By 2002 he had recorded with his trio Bandwagon and with the saxophonist Greg Osby. Making a solo record so early in a career was a statement; Moran wanted to show his whole lineage, from Harlem stride to German Romantic song to the South Bronx.","Listen to his Planet Rock. Moran keeps the robotic pulse of the Roland drum machine in his left hand while his right hand plays shards of melody, so the piece sounds like a stride pianist imitating a sampler. In the Schumann, he lets the song's original harmony sit almost untouched before nudging it into blues inflections.","Critics praised it highly, and some named it the best jazz record of its year. A few found it more a catalogue of influences than a coherent statement. In hindsight it reads as an early, clear map of the eclectic, history-conscious approach that defined Moran's career and that of many pianists after him.","Hear James P. Johnson's original You've Got to Be Modernistic first, then Moran's version, and notice how much faster and harder his left hand hits. Then put the Schumann beside a recording of the Liederkreis with a singer. Finish with Planet Rock and imagine a dance floor."],"jason-moran-ten":["A record celebrating ten years of The Bandwagon, Moran's trio with Tarus Mateen on bass and Nasheet Waits on drums. It ranges from Thelonious Monk's Crepuscule with Nellie to Conlon Nancarrow's Study No. 6, originally for player piano, and Leonard Bernstein's Big Stuff, alongside Moran's own pieces. The trio can sound like a single body: they speed up, drop out and re-enter together, and Waits's drumming is as melodic as the piano.","Moran had formed The Bandwagon around 2000, and by 2010 it was one of the most admired working piano trios in jazz. That year he also received a MacArthur Fellowship, unusual for a jazz musician. His interests had widened to collaborations with visual artists and art galleries, and Ten shows that range in its choice of material from player-piano studies to Broadway.","The Nancarrow piece is the giveaway. Nancarrow punched his studies into player-piano rolls precisely because no human could play their layered tempos; Moran's trio plays a version by hand, letting the bass and drums take parts of the texture. In Big Stuff, a song Bernstein wrote for his ballet Fancy Free and Billie Holiday recorded, listen to how slowly the trio lets it unfold.","Many critics named it among the best jazz albums of 2010, and it was voted top album in at least one major poll. A few found its range of sources restless. It is now widely considered the definitive Bandwagon record, the place where the trio's long-developed group language is at its clearest.","Start with Crepuscule with Nellie, then Study No. 6, and compare Nancarrow's own player-piano version to hear what human hands change. Listen for the moments when the three players speed up or stop together without any obvious cue; that shared instinct is the record's real subject."],"mary-halvorson-away-with-you":["A record for octet in which Halvorson's guitar, with its warped, pitch-bending lines, sits beside the pedal steel guitar of Susan Alcorn. Jonathan Finlayson on trumpet, Jon Irabagon and Ingrid Laubrock on saxophones, Jacob Garchik on trombone, John Hébert on bass and Ches Smith on drums complete the band. The pieces are intricate, long-form compositions that move between brass-band fanfares, grinding dissonance and lyrical passages, with Alcorn's steel adding a weeping, floating colour that no jazz horn section usually has.","Halvorson studied with Anthony Braxton at Wesleyan and became one of the leading guitarists of the New York avant-garde in the 2000s. Her groups grew step by step from trio to quintet to septet; for this record she added the pedal steel, an instrument tied to country music and almost never heard in jazz. The album was issued by Firehouse 12, a New Haven label and venue that documented much of the new improvised music of the period.","Listen to how often Halvorson's guitar dips in pitch at the end of a phrase, an effect she makes with a delay pedal pushed past its normal setting. It is her signature, and Alcorn answers it with the pedal steel, which slides between notes by its nature. In places their lines merge so closely that you cannot tell which guitar is playing.","Critics praised it widely, and it was a fixture on year-end jazz lists. Some listeners find her writing knotty and dry, with too much plotted detail; others find it exhilarating precisely for that density. It is often cited as the moment her ensemble writing caught up with her reputation as a guitarist, and as proof that Braxton's teaching had found a distinctive new voice.","Start with the title track and follow the pedal steel all the way through; then play it again and follow the guitar. Notice how the horns often enter as a block, like a small brass band. Afterwards hear Anthony Braxton's Creative Orchestra Music 1976 to meet her teacher's way with a large ensemble."],"mary-halvorson-code-girl":["A double album of songs, the first for which Halvorson wrote her own words. Amirtha Kidambi sings, with Ambrose Akinmusire on trumpet, Michael Formanek on bass and Tomas Fujiwara on drums. The songs move between delicate art-song passages, jagged rock riffs and long instrumental stretches; Kidambi's voice is clear and exact, sometimes doubled by Halvorson's guitar note for note. The texts are oblique, closer to poetry than storytelling, and the whole has the slow-burning shape of a song cycle.","Halvorson had been known mainly as a guitarist and instrumental composer. Writing words was a new direction, prompted partly by her love of singer-songwriters and art rock. Kidambi, a singer with roots in Carnatic music and experimental improvisation, and Akinmusire, one of the most admired trumpeters of his generation, gave the project its unusual palette. Formanek and Fujiwara had played with her in other groups, so the band already shared a language.","Listen to Akinmusire's solos. He plays long, slowly unfolding lines in which notes crack, smear and whisper, very different from Halvorson's angular guitar, and the contrast gives the songs their emotional range. Listen also for how often voice and guitar share a melody exactly, so that the song seems to come from a single strange instrument.","It was praised as a bold new direction, though some reviewers found the lyrics opaque and the singing cool to the point of distance. It led to a second Code Girl album with a changed lineup and to much wider recognition, crowned by Halvorson's MacArthur Fellowship in 2019. Debate continues over whether the project is jazz at all, which is partly the point.","Pick a song where Akinmusire solos and listen once for the voice alone, then once for the guitar alone. Then go to Akinmusire's own When the Heart Emerges Glistening to hear him lead. For a kindred cross of art song and rock, try Kate Bush's Hounds of Love afterward."],"ambrose-akinmusire-when-the-heart-emerges-glistening":["Akinmusire's Blue Note debut, a quintet record with Walter Smith III on tenor saxophone, Gerald Clayton on piano, Harish Raghavan on bass and Justin Brown on drums. The trumpet sound is the point: breathy, cracked, sometimes barely voiced, then suddenly bright and piercing. Pieces unfold slowly, with long passages of quiet, inward group playing and few conventional head-solo-head shapes; one track, My Name Is Oscar, is a spoken elegy delivered over drums alone.","Akinmusire, from Oakland, California, won the Thelonious Monk International Jazz Competition in 2007 and signed to Blue Note soon after. Jason Moran produced the album. My Name Is Oscar refers to Oscar Grant, a young man shot dead by a transit police officer at an Oakland station on New Year's Day 2009, an event that marked the Bay Area deeply and which Akinmusire chose to address directly on a major-label debut.","Listen to the gaps. Akinmusire often lets a phrase fade almost to silence before the band moves on, and Brown's drums fill the space with small, crisp figures rather than steady time. The cracked notes are deliberate: he uses unstable sounds as expression, the way a singer uses a break in the voice, and he often holds back the trumpet's bright upper register until late in a piece.","It was widely praised as one of the strongest jazz debuts in years and established Akinmusire as a leading trumpeter of his generation. Some listeners found it austere and slow to reward. In hindsight many hear it as a turn in jazz trumpet away from the polished brilliance of the 1980s toward something more fragile and searching.","Start with My Name Is Oscar, then move to one of the long quintet pieces and follow the trumpet's tone rather than its notes. Then put on Wynton Marsalis's Black Codes to hear two generations' ideas of what a jazz trumpet should sound like, and decide which moves you more."],"ambrose-akinmusire-the-imagined-savior-is-far-easier-to-paint":["A long, ambitious record in which Akinmusire's quintet is joined by a string quartet and guest singers, among them Becca Stevens, Theo Bleckmann and Cold Specks. The music moves from chamber-like string passages to art-song ballads to extended group improvisations. Rollcall for Those Absent, in which a child recites names of people killed by police and in other violence, is one of the most direct statements on any jazz record of the decade, all the more powerful for its plainness.","By 2014 Akinmusire had toured widely with his quintet, which now included guitar, and he wanted a broader canvas. The strings and singers brought him close to contemporary classical music and to indie rock. The album appeared as public argument over police violence in the United States was intensifying, and Akinmusire, like several jazz musicians of his generation, placed that argument inside the music rather than in a liner note.","Listen to how the string quartet often plays sustained harmonies that shift very slowly under the trumpet, a texture closer to Morton Feldman than to the lush jazz-with-strings albums of the 1950s. And listen to Akinmusire's own solos for how often he chooses a whisper over a shout, letting the singers carry the climaxes.","It was praised for ambition and moral courage, and appeared on many year-end lists. Some critics found it overlong and uneven, with the guest songs sitting awkwardly beside the instrumental pieces. It is now often cited as a key jazz album of the years when the Black Lives Matter movement took shape, and as a forerunner of the protest-minded jazz records that followed.","Start with Rollcall for Those Absent, then a song with Becca Stevens, and notice how differently the trumpet behaves around a voice. Then play Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, released the following year with many jazz players aboard, to hear the same moment from the other side."],"esperanza-spalding-radio-music-society":["A record of songs meant for the radio, built on Spalding's bass and voice with horns, keyboards, drums and guests including Joe Lovano, Jack DeJohnette, Lalah Hathaway and Gretchen Parlato. It covers Wayne Shorter's Endangered Species and Michael Jackson's I Can't Help It, a Stevie Wonder song, alongside originals such as Black Gold. The sound mixes soul, funk, pop and jazz, with brass arrangements, a big-band feel on some tracks, and Spalding's agile, high, bright voice on top.","Spalding, from Portland, Oregon, had won the 2011 Grammy for Best New Artist, a surprise victory over pop stars that made her famous outside jazz. This album was conceived as a companion to Chamber Music Society, its more intimate predecessor, and aimed squarely at a broader audience. Q-Tip of A Tribe Called Quest co-produced some tracks. It went on to win the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album.","Listen to the bass lines. Spalding sings and plays at once, often with lines that move against the vocal melody in rhythm and direction, a feat very few musicians attempt. The Shorter cover keeps his twisting harmony but adds words, turning an instrumental piece from the jazz canon into a song with a message.","It was warmly received and won its Grammy, though some critics found it too polished and radio-ready, and others missed the chamber intimacy of its predecessor. Over time it has come to be heard as a careful, generous attempt to reconnect jazz with soul and R&B listeners, a project Robert Glasper was pursuing from the other direction the same year.","Start with Black Gold, then Endangered Species, and compare Wayne Shorter's original instrumental. Notice how often the bass line is as catchy as the vocal. Then play Chamber Music Society to hear the paired record, and Robert Glasper's Black Radio for a neighbour from the same year."],"esperanza-spalding-emilys-d-evolution":["A loud, theatrical, guitar-heavy record built around a character named Emily, Spalding's middle name and childhood alter ego. Matthew Stevens's electric guitar and Karriem Riggins's drums push the music toward progressive rock and funk, with Spalding's voice and electric bass at the front. The songs are knotty and quick-changing, layered with stacked backing vocals, and a cover of Pure Imagination from the film Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory sits among the originals.","Spalding co-produced the album with Tony Visconti, best known for his work with David Bowie, and presented it on a staged tour with choreography, costumes and spoken interludes. The move away from acoustic jazz was deliberate, part of her wish to escape the image of a polite jazz prodigy she had acquired after her Grammy win. The record was released by Concord, which had absorbed her earlier label.","Listen to the bass. Spalding plays electric bass with the agility of her upright playing but in rock-style riffs, often in uneven meters, and the guitar locks into those patterns in a way that recalls 1970s progressive rock more than jazz. The vocal harmonies are tracked in many layers, a studio technique foreign to most jazz records.","Some longtime fans were bewildered by the volume and the rock trappings; others were thrilled. Critics generally praised her daring while differing on the songs themselves. The record now looks like the hinge of her career, the point at which she claimed the freedom that produced the experimental 12 Little Spells and her later work with Wayne Shorter on the opera Iphigenia.","Start with a fast track at volume and follow the bass rather than the voice. Then play Joni Mitchell's Hejira, which Spalding has often named as a touchstone, and Jaco Pastorius's debut for a fretless bass ancestor. Finally go back to Radio Music Society to hear how far she moved in four years."],"cecile-mclorin-salvant-dreams-and-daggers":["A double album recorded mostly live at the Village Vanguard in New York with Aaron Diehl on piano, Paul Sikivie on bass and Lawrence Leathers on drums, framed by studio tracks with the Catalyst Quartet. Salvant sings songs from the Bessie Smith repertoire, Broadway and Tin Pan Alley, including Noël Coward's Mad About the Boy, alongside her own pieces. Her voice ranges from a deep, growling low register to a light, almost operatic top, and her phrasing is theatrical, witty and exact.","Salvant, born in Miami to a Haitian father and a French mother, studied classical voice and Baroque singing in Aix-en-Provence before turning to jazz, and won the Thelonious Monk competition in 2010. By the time of this record she had a long-standing partnership with Diehl's trio, honed on the road. The Vanguard, the most storied jazz room in New York, gave the album its intimate, close-up sound.","Listen to how she treats old songs about women. Salvant often chooses material whose attitudes now sound uncomfortable and sings it straight-faced but with a slight edge, so the listener hears the song's assumptions rather than simply enjoying them. The string quartet interludes are her own arrangements and frame the live set like chapters in a book.","It was widely praised and won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album, her second win in the category. A few listeners find her mannered, every phrase a performance; most hear the most gifted jazz singer of her generation, regularly compared to Sarah Vaughan and Betty Carter for range and to Abbey Lincoln for seriousness. The album is often named the fullest portrait of her live art.","Start with Mad About the Boy, then pick one of the Bessie Smith songs and play Bessie's original 1920s recording beside it. Listen for the moments when Salvant drops suddenly from her light top voice to her low growl, and for how Diehl's trio answers."],"cecile-mclorin-salvant-the-window":["A duo record with the pianist Sullivan Fortner, partly recorded live at the Village Vanguard. The songs range from Broadway and the Great American Songbook to Stevie Wonder and French chanson. With no bass or drums, the music is intimate and loose; Fortner follows Salvant closely, stretching and compressing time, adding sudden harmonies and stride flourishes, and the two improvise together as equals. The mood moves between hushed ballads, comic numbers and moments of real darkness.","The album followed Dreams and Daggers and won the Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal Album, Salvant's third. Fortner, from New Orleans, had become one of the most sought-after pianists of his generation, partly through his work with the trumpeter Roy Hargrove. The voice-and-piano duo, rare in recent jazz singing, recalls Ella Fitzgerald's and Sarah Vaughan's recordings with a single accompanist and the cabaret tradition.","Listen to the silences. Without a rhythm section, Salvant and Fortner can pause, slow down or rush at will, and those tempo changes carry much of the drama; a phrase may hang in the air for a long moment before the piano catches it. Fortner often answers her lines with tiny, unexpected harmonies that change the meaning of the words she has just sung.","Critics praised it as her most intimate record and among her best, and it took the Grammy. Some missed the swing of a full band. It is now often cited as a model of the voice-piano duo, and as evidence that Salvant's art depends as much on her choice of partner as on her voice.","Start with a slow ballad and listen once only to the piano, then once only to the voice. Then compare Ella Fitzgerald's duo work, or Bill Evans's Peace Piece for the patience of a pianist alone. Play it late, quietly, as if in a small club."],"makaya-mccraven-in-the-moment":["An album edited from live improvisations by McCraven's bands during a long weekly residency at a small Chicago venue. McCraven recorded the shows, then cut, looped and layered the best moments into short tracks, much as a hip-hop producer builds beats from samples. The result sounds like a beat tape made from live jazz: tight drum grooves, looping bass figures, fragments of horn and guitar solos, with McCraven's crisp drumming at the centre of nearly every track.","McCraven, born in Paris and raised in western Massachusetts, settled in Chicago and became central to its new jazz scene. The album was released by International Anthem, a small Chicago label that grew into a hub of that scene. The approach, which McCraven called organic beat music, drew on the production of J Dilla and Madlib as much as on jazz, and on cheap digital editing that let one musician shape hours of tape at home.","Listen for the edits. Many tracks repeat a short passage of live playing, with small variations, as if a sampler were looping it, and you can hear the seams if you listen for them. The crowd and room noise are often audible, a reminder that the source was a bar on an ordinary weeknight rather than a studio.","It was praised as a genuinely new way to make jazz records and became a cult favourite, launching McCraven's wider career. Some purists questioned whether editing improvisation honours it or betrays it, since the finished tracks never happened in that order. Teo Macero's edits for Miles Davis are the usual counter-argument, and the debate has since become part of the record's story.","Start anywhere and listen as you would to a beat tape, letting the loops settle rather than waiting for solos. Then play J Dilla's Donuts to hear the model, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way, whose tape edits did something similar forty-six years earlier."],"makaya-mccraven-universal-beings":["A double album recorded with different bands in four cities: New York, Chicago, London and Los Angeles, each given its own side and mood. Players include the harpist Brandee Younger, the saxophonists Shabaka Hutchings and Nubya Garcia, the tuba player Theon Cross, the cellist Tomeka Reid, the vibraphonist Joel Ross and the guitarist Jeff Parker. As before, McCraven recorded live sessions and edited the results, but here the playing itself comes further forward and the tracks breathe longer.","By 2018 several young jazz scenes were in conversation: London's players around Hutchings and Garcia, Chicago's International Anthem circle and the beat-influenced jazz of Los Angeles. McCraven connected them in a single project, travelling to record with local musicians in each city. The album was among the most praised jazz releases of its year and was followed by a documentary film of the same name.","Listen to how each city's side has its own character: the London tracks drive hard toward the dance floor, with tuba carrying the bass, while the Los Angeles side is spacious and hazy. The edits often loop a single moment of collective improvisation into a groove, so that a few seconds of spontaneity become the foundation of a whole track.","It was hailed as a landmark of the new international jazz moment and appeared at or near the top of many year-end lists. Some critics wanted longer, uninterrupted performances and felt the editing flattened the players' arcs. Most heard it as proof that the producer's chair could be a jazz musician's instrument.","Play the London side first, then the Los Angeles side, and compare how the same drummer sounds in each. Listen for the harp on the New York side. Then hear Kamasi Washington's The Epic, its west-coast cousin, and Flying Lotus's Cosmogramma for the beat-music side of the family."],"tyshawn-sorey-verisimilitude":["A trio record with Cory Smythe on piano and Chris Tordini on bass. The pieces are long, slow and spacious, unfolding over many minutes through quiet sustained sounds, silences and gradual swells. Sorey's drums are often played softly, with mallets, bowed cymbals and hand percussion, creating textures rather than keeping time. At moments the trio erupts into dense, loud playing, but the prevailing atmosphere is closer to contemporary classical chamber music than to a conventional jazz piano trio.","Sorey, from Newark, New Jersey, is a drummer, trombonist, pianist and composer who studied with Anthony Braxton at Wesleyan and later at Columbia. He works equally in jazz and in concert music, and had recorded with this trio before. Pi Recordings, a New York label devoted to adventurous jazz, released the album in the year Sorey received a MacArthur Fellowship, and it appeared on many critics' year-end lists.","Listen to how long each sound is allowed to last. Sorey writes and improvises with extreme patience, so that a single chord or cymbal roll may hold for close to a minute, and the structure becomes clear only over time. Ideas return much later in altered form, which is why the record rewards listening straight through.","Critics praised it as a major statement from a composer-performer coming into his own, though some found its slowness and quiet demanding to the point of frustration. It helped establish Sorey's reputation beyond jazz circles, paving the way for commissions from classical ensembles and, eventually, a Pulitzer Prize.","Listen in a quiet room without distraction, and resist skipping ahead when nothing seems to happen; the stillness is the material. Then play Morton Feldman's Triadic Memories or Rothko Chapel to hear a kindred patience from the concert world, and Anthony Braxton for Sorey's teacher."],"tyshawn-sorey-pillars":["A three-disc work close to four hours long for a large ensemble of improvisers, with Sorey on drums, trombone and other instruments. The music unfolds as slow sustained drones, quiet scraping textures, sudden eruptions and long stretches near silence, with several double basses giving it a dark low floor. There are no tunes and almost no steady beat. It feels more like an installation or a ritual than a jazz record, a single span to be lived inside rather than followed.","Sorey conceived the work as one long composition for musicians he trusted, mixing written material, cues and open improvisation. It was released by Firehouse 12, the New Haven label that also issued Mary Halvorson's records. It came the year after his MacArthur Fellowship, when he was receiving growing attention as a composer, and it showed how far he was willing to take duration as a musical material.","Listen to how the ensemble moves from near-silence to loud, dense passages and back over spans of many minutes, often so gradually that you notice the change only after it has happened. The bowed basses and sustained brass blur together into a single shifting sound, so individual players are hard to pick out.","Critics praised it as a major, uncompromising statement and it appeared on year-end lists; others found its length forbidding and wondered how many listeners would ever hear it whole. It is now discussed alongside the long late works of Morton Feldman as a test of what duration can do to attention.","Take one disc at a time, in a quiet room, ideally at night, and do not multitask. Notice how your sense of time shifts after twenty minutes. Afterwards, Feldman's String Quartet No. 2 makes a natural companion, and Coltrane's Ascension shows a far louder idea of collective sound."],"henry-threadgill-in-for-a-penny-in-for-a-pound":["A double album by Threadgill's group Zooid: Threadgill on alto saxophone and flutes, Liberty Ellman on guitar, Jose Davila on tuba and trombone, Christopher Hoffman on cello and Elliot Humberto Kavee on drums. The music is intricate, with lines weaving around one another like counterpoint while the drums keep a light, springy pulse. Guitar, cello and tuba make an unusual middle and low register, and Threadgill's sour, keening alto cuts through on top.","Threadgill, a Chicago-born veteran of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, had led Zooid since the early 2000s and was over seventy when this record appeared. It won the 2016 Pulitzer Prize for Music, placing him beside Wynton Marsalis and Ornette Coleman among the jazz musicians so honoured. Pi Recordings had become his regular home and issued the set as a two-disc release.","The players are not improvising over chords. Threadgill developed a system in which each musician is given a set of intervals to work within, so the lines move independently yet always fit together. That is why the music sounds both free and strangely ordered, like several conversations that keep agreeing without meeting.","Critics praised it highly, and the Pulitzer brought Threadgill late recognition well beyond the jazz world. Some listeners find the music cerebral and hard to grasp on first hearing; others treat it as the culmination of fifty years of searching. It has since become one of the most discussed jazz composition records of its decade.","Start anywhere and follow one instrument, the tuba, say, for a full piece, then go back and follow the cello. Notice how each line is complete in itself. Then hear Muhal Richard Abrams and the Art Ensemble of Chicago to meet the AACM world Threadgill came from."],"henry-threadgill-old-locks-and-irregular-verbs":["A four-part suite for Threadgill's Ensemble Double Up, in which he composes and directs but does not play. The band pairs two pianos, played by Jason Moran and David Virelles, with two alto saxophones, cello, tuba and drums. The paired instruments shadow, echo and contradict each other, creating a doubled, mirrored texture. The suite moves through spacious, almost mournful passages and dense, knotted ensemble writing, with the pianos often taking the lead in turn.","The suite is dedicated to Lawrence \"Butch\" Morris, the cornetist and conductor whose system of conducted improvisation, which he called conduction, influenced many New York musicians; Morris died in 2013. Threadgill had known him since the 1970s. The album appeared on Pi Recordings shortly after Threadgill's Pulitzer Prize was announced, which brought it more attention than his records usually received.","The doubling of pianos and altos is the key. Listen for moments when the two pianists play nearly the same thing slightly apart, producing a blurred echo, and others when they split into opposing lines. The tuba and cello beneath them often carry the true melody while the doubled instruments decorate it.","Critics welcomed it as a moving tribute and a strong companion to the Zooid records, often praising Moran's and Virelles's interplay. Some found it austere and short on Threadgill's own alto. It has come to be heard as one of his most personal recent works, a memorial written in his most complex language.","Hear it straight through, about forty minutes, and try to keep the two pianos apart in your ear; on headphones they are usually set left and right. Then play Jason Moran's Ten to hear one of the pianists in his own trio, and In for a Penny, In for a Pound for the Zooid sound."],"garbarek-witchi-tai-to":["Five long performances by the quartet Jan Garbarek co-led with the Swedish pianist Bobo Stenson, with Palle Danielsson on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. Almost nothing here is by the band: there is a Carla Bley tune, a Cuban song in praise of Che Guevara, Jim Pepper's chant-like title piece and, to close, Don Cherry's \"Desireless\", stretched to around twenty minutes. Garbarek's soprano is keening and bright, the rhythm section loose and breathing, the mood ecstatic rather than cool.","Recorded in Oslo in late 1973 for Manfred Eicher's young Munich label ECM, this was the point where Garbarek moved past his early fiery, Coltrane-and-Ayler-inspired records toward a more open, song-led manner. The quartet was a pan-Scandinavian band of the kind ECM was quietly building, and the choice of material shows how widely these players listened: American free jazz, Latin American protest song and a Native American saxophonist's hit tune all sit together.","Jim Pepper, who wrote the title piece, was a Native American saxophonist who built it on a chant he had learned from his grandfather; Garbarek turns that repeated, almost ceremonial phrase into a slow-burning hymn. Listen to how Christensen keeps time mostly on cymbals and toms, never quite settling into swing, so the music floats. \"Desireless\" builds from near-silence into a long, hypnotic ostinato that anticipates much later Nordic jazz.","The album was well received on release and has grown steadily in reputation, often named as the best of Garbarek's early ECM records and a founding document of the Nordic sound. Some admirers of his later, sparer work find it surprisingly hot-blooded; others think that warmth is exactly what his later records lost. Its reissue in ECM's Touchstones series introduced it to a new generation of listeners.","Start with the title track and wait for the moment the melody returns after the solos, now carried with full force. Then give \"Desireless\" its full length on headphones and notice how little changes and how much it grows. Compare with Keith Jarrett's Belonging, which uses almost the same band, and with Don Cherry's own records."],"garbarek-dis":["A duo record for Jan Garbarek's saxophones and wooden flute and Ralph Towner's twelve-string and classical guitars, joined on some tracks by a wind harp, an instrument played only by the air moving across its strings. The pieces are slow, modal and spacious: long held saxophone notes over ringing guitar patterns, or over the harp's eerie, unpredictable drone. There is almost no swing and almost no harmony moving in the usual jazz way. It sounds like weather and distance more than a club.","By the mid-1970s ECM had become a home for a new kind of chamber jazz, and Manfred Eicher liked to pair musicians who had not played together as a regular unit. Towner, an American from the group Oregon, brought folk and classical guitar technique; Garbarek was turning more and more toward Norwegian folk song. The title is an old Norwegian word for mist or haze, and the cover photograph of grey sea set the visual style many listeners still associate with the label.","The wind harp was recorded outdoors on the Norwegian coast, so on those tracks the composer of the drone is literally the wind: the overtones that bloom and fade under Garbarek's line were not played by anyone. Listen for the way he shapes his notes to match them rather than the other way round. Towner's twelve-string patterns are often in odd groupings that make the pulse feel slightly unsteady.","Dis became one of Garbarek's best-known albums and, for many, the record that fixed the cliché of the ECM sound as fjords and fog. Jazz traditionalists asked whether it was jazz at all, a question that would follow Garbarek for decades. Others heard in it a serious attempt to make improvised music from northern landscape and folk melody, and it has long been admired by ambient and new-age musicians.","Listen in a quiet room, not as background. Start with the first track and follow the single saxophone line as if it were a singer. Notice how Towner never fills space, then wait for the wind harp and listen to its tones drifting in and out. Afterwards try Brian Eno's ambient records, then Garbarek's later Officium."],"garbarek-officium":["Jan Garbarek improvises on soprano and tenor saxophone around the Hilliard Ensemble, four male voices singing Gregorian chant and polyphony from the medieval and Renaissance church. The voices sing the old music as written; the saxophone weaves above and between them, sometimes doubling a line, sometimes floating free, sometimes answering a phrase like a fifth singer. The acoustic is huge and resonant. The whole album moves at the pace of a slow procession, with silence ringing after every cadence.","The project was Manfred Eicher's idea, made for ECM's New Series, which he had founded to record composers such as Arvo Pärt alongside the label's jazz. The sessions took place in 1993 in the Austrian monastery of St Gerold, chosen for its echo. The early 1990s were the years of chant on the pop charts and of Górecki's Third Symphony as a bestseller, and Officium arrived in the same climate of hunger for slow, sacred-sounding music.","Most of the repertoire is real early music: Cristóbal de Morales's \"Parce mihi Domine\", from his Office of the Dead, returns more than once, and Pérotin's \"Beata viscera\", a song from Notre-Dame around 1200, also appears. Listen for how Garbarek usually enters after the voices have established the mode, then stays inside it. Where the saxophone is silent, the singers are performing straight; the improvisation is added, never written into the parts.","Officium became one of the biggest sellers in ECM's history and charted in several countries in both classical and pop listings. Early-music purists objected that the saxophone turned liturgy into mood music; some jazz critics found the improvising too polite. Yet many listeners came to chant and Renaissance polyphony through it, and the team made two sequels, Mnemosyne and Officium Novum. Its long success has made it a touchstone in arguments about crossover.","Start with \"Parce mihi Domine\" and listen first only to the voices, then play it again and follow the saxophone. Notice the long reverberation and how the players let each phrase die away before moving. Then hear Pérotin's music sung plainly by a choir, and Arvo Pärt's Summa, to judge what the saxophone adds and what it changes."],"stanko-balladyna":["A quartet without piano: Tomasz Stańko's trumpet and Tomasz Szukalski's saxophones over the American bassist Dave Holland and the Finnish drummer Edward Vesala. The tunes are dark, slow-moving melodies that open into free, smeared, often wailing improvisation, then return. Stańko's tone is the recognisable thing: breathy, cracked at the edges, bending notes downward as if sighing. The absence of a chord instrument gives the music a raw, open feel, closer to Ornette Coleman's quartet than to cool jazz.","Stańko was the leading trumpeter of the Polish avant-garde and had played in Krzysztof Komeda's groups in the 1960s. In communist Poland jazz had been tolerated and sometimes celebrated, but recording in the West remained a privilege. This 1975 Oslo session, made for ECM, was his first for the label and his first wide exposure outside Eastern Europe. The title comes from a nineteenth-century Polish verse drama by Juliusz Słowacki about ambition and murder.","Listen to how often Holland plays a melody against the horns rather than a walking bass line; with no piano he becomes a second voice. Vesala's drumming is full of rattles and washes, closer to free jazz than to swing, but he keeps an undertow of pulse. Stańko frequently plays two or three notes and then lets them decay, using silence as part of the phrase in a way that would become his signature.","Balladyna was respected but not widely noticed at first. Its standing rose after Stańko's return to ECM in the 1990s made him a major international name, when critics went back and heard in it the seeds of his late style. Some prefer its harder, freer edges to the lyrical later records. It remains a key document of how Eastern European jazz musicians absorbed American free jazz and then made something darker of it.","Start with the title track and listen to Stańko's very first phrases: notice how he bends and frays the notes. Then follow Holland's bass as an equal voice. Compare with Ornette Coleman's piano-less quartet of 1959, and then jump forward to Stańko's Soul of Things to hear how the same tone sounds in a gentler setting."],"stanko-litania":["Tomasz Stańko leads a septet through the music of Krzysztof Komeda, the Polish pianist and film composer who had been his mentor. The band is Scandinavian and Swedish-heavy: Bobo Stenson on piano, Palle Danielsson on bass, Jon Christensen on drums, the tenor saxophonists Bernt Rosengren and Joakim Milder, and Terje Rypdal's guitar on some tracks. The music is slow, melancholy and lyrical, with brooding melodies, hushed ensemble passages and Stańko's breathy, grieving trumpet always at the centre.","Komeda had died in 1969, aged thirty-seven, after an accident in Los Angeles, at the height of a career that included the scores for Roman Polański's films, among them Knife in the Water and Rosemary's Baby. Stańko had played in Komeda's quintet as a young man, and Rosengren too had played with him. Recorded in Oslo in 1997, the album was both a tribute and a statement that Komeda's music belonged in the international jazz canon.","Listen for the lullaby from Rosemary's Baby, \"Sleep Safe and Warm\", which appears in more than one version: what was a sinister nursery tune in the film becomes, slowed down, a tender ballad. Komeda's themes often move in modal patterns that recall Polish folk music, and Stenson's piano voicings hint at Chopin. Rosengren's presence links the recording to Komeda's own 1960s band.","Litania was greeted as one of the finest ECM records of the 1990s and helped spark a revival of interest in Komeda, whose own records had been hard to find outside Poland. Critics praised Stańko for honouring the composer without nostalgia. Some felt the ECM production smoothed away the urgency of the originals. It is now regarded as a central work of Polish jazz and of Stańko's late career.","Start with \"Sleep Safe and Warm\", then watch the opening of Rosemary's Baby to hear the original. Notice how slowly the band lets the melody unfold, and how the two tenors blend almost into one voice. Then seek out Komeda's own album Astigmatic, with the young Stańko on trumpet, to hear where this all began."],"stanko-soul-of-things":["Thirteen pieces, all called simply \"Soul of Things\" and numbered I to XIII, played by Tomasz Stańko with a trio of young Polish musicians: Marcin Wasilewski on piano, Sławomir Kurkiewicz on bass and Michał Miśkiewicz on drums. The music is mostly slow and lyrical, with ballads and gently swinging pieces in which the trumpet sings in long, smoky phrases. The trio plays with real delicacy and fluidity, and the whole record has the shape of one long, continuous meditation.","Stańko, by now in his late fifties, chose to work with a trio of musicians in their twenties who had grown up admiring him. He had heard them as a group from Koszalin and adopted them as his band. Recorded in Oslo in 2001 for ECM, this was their first album together; they went on to make Suspended Night and Lontano with him before becoming famous in their own right as the Marcin Wasilewski Trio.","The thirteen pieces are separate compositions, but several share melodic cells and moods, so the numbering is more than a gimmick: it invites you to hear one suite. Listen to how Wasilewski often lays out entirely during Stańko's solos, leaving bass and drums alone with the trumpet, then re-enters softly. Miśkiewicz plays mostly with brushes and cymbals, keeping a pulse that never insists.","Soul of Things was widely praised as one of Stańko's finest records and among the most beautiful jazz albums of its decade. Critics noted a new serenity in his playing after years of rawer, freer work. A few missed the edge of his earlier music. The album also launched the young trio's international career, and their later records on ECM have kept its reputation alive.","Play it from the beginning and let it run; the album works best whole. Notice how Stańko rarely plays fast, and how much feeling he gets into single sustained notes. Then compare with Miles Davis's ballad playing on Kind of Blue, and with the Bill Evans trio's interplay, to hear the tradition this record carries forward."],"kenny-wheeler-gnu-high":["Three long pieces, \"Heyoke\", \"Smatter\" and \"Gnu Suite\", for Kenny Wheeler's flugelhorn with Keith Jarrett on piano, Dave Holland on bass and Jack DeJohnette on drums. Wheeler writes melodies that leap in wide intervals and then fall back with a wistful, almost Romantic ache; his tone is warm and dark, his solos full of sudden flights into the high register. The rhythm section is a dream team, and Jarrett, unusually, plays strictly as a sideman, supporting another composer's music.","Wheeler, born in Canada, had lived in London since the early 1950s and was a central figure in the British free-improvisation and big-band scenes, yet he was barely known in America. This 1975 New York session for ECM was his first album for the label and gave him an international profile. It was also one of the very few times Jarrett, by then a major star, recorded as a sideman after the early 1970s.","\"Heyoke\" lasts more than twenty minutes and is built in sections that return like a rondo; listen for the opening melody coming back in altered form near the end. Wheeler's characteristic leaps of an octave or more are hard to play on the flugelhorn, and he lands them almost casually. Listen also to how Jarrett holds back his trademark long solos and comps with real restraint.","Gnu High was welcomed as a fine record at the time and has since become a cult favourite, often named as one of the best ECM albums of the 1970s and as Wheeler's finest small-group statement. Musicians prize Wheeler's compositions, which are now studied in jazz schools. The record is also cherished for showing a side of Jarrett rarely heard, as a sympathetic accompanist.","Start with \"Heyoke\" and simply follow Wheeler's melody, noticing its leaps and sighs. Then listen again to Jarrett behind him. Compare with Jarrett's own Facing You to hear the pianist in charge, and with Wheeler's Angel Song to hear his writing in a very different, drumless setting."],"kenny-wheeler-angel-song":["A quartet with no drums and no piano: Kenny Wheeler on flugelhorn, Lee Konitz on alto saxophone, Bill Frisell on guitar and Dave Holland on bass. The music is quiet chamber jazz, almost all slow or medium tempo, with Wheeler's melodies passed between horn and saxophone while Frisell's guitar adds soft clouds of harmony and Holland holds everything together. Without a drummer the pulse is implied rather than stated, and the record has an intimate, conversational, searching feel throughout.","Recorded in New York in 1996 and released by ECM the following year, Angel Song brought together four musicians from different generations and traditions: Konitz from the cool jazz of the late 1940s, Holland from Miles Davis's electric bands, Frisell from the downtown New York scene and Americana. Wheeler, by now in his late sixties, wrote all the music, and the group's line-up had never played together as a band before the session.","Konitz is a famously unpredictable improviser who rarely plays a phrase the same way twice, and his lines often run against Wheeler's long arcs in a quiet counterpoint. Listen to how Frisell uses volume swells so that his chords seem to appear from nowhere, with no attack. The absence of drums means every player must keep time inside their own phrasing, and you can hear them listening intently.","Critics praised it as one of the most beautiful records of the decade and a model of chamber jazz; the British press in particular embraced it as a crowning statement of Wheeler's writing. Some found it too subdued. It is now widely considered among his finest albums and is often recommended to listeners who come to jazz from classical chamber music.","Listen at low volume, ideally late at night. Start with the title track and follow the interplay between flugelhorn and alto: which one leads, and when? Then notice Frisell's guitar entering underneath. Compare with Lee Konitz's early records, and with Gnu High to hear Wheeler's tunes with a full rhythm section."],"surman-the-amazing-adventures-of-simon-simon":["A duo between John Surman, playing soprano and baritone saxophones, bass clarinet and synthesisers, and Jack DeJohnette on drums, congas and keyboards. The album is laid out as a loose story in short episodes, each with an evocative title. Surman often builds a repeating synthesiser pattern first, then plays long, folk-like melodies over it on baritone or soprano, while DeJohnette moves between crisp jazz drumming and rolling, tribal percussion. The sound is bright, rhythmic and unusually lyrical.","Surman, from Devon, had been a leading figure in British jazz since the late 1960s, famous for making the baritone saxophone fly. During the 1970s he became fascinated by synthesisers and overdubbing and began working regularly with ECM. DeJohnette had played with Miles Davis and Keith Jarrett and led his own ECM groups. The two had played together before, and this 1981 Oslo session was a chance to combine Surman's electronic experiments with a great drummer.","Many of the synthesiser patterns are simple repeating figures, close to the early minimalism of Terry Riley and Steve Reich, and Surman plays against them in English modal melodies that sound like folk song or church music. Listen to how DeJohnette shifts the pulse under a pattern so that the same loop suddenly feels like a different metre. The story behind the titles is never spelled out: it is left to your imagination.","The album was warmly received and has remained one of Surman's most popular records; it was reissued in ECM's Touchstones series. Some purists disliked the synthesisers, which date the sound to the early 1980s. Others hear it as a forerunner of later fusions of jazz, minimalism and electronics. It is often cited as the best introduction to Surman's work.","Start at the beginning and listen for the moment the baritone first enters over the synthesiser loop: it is deep yet light on its feet. Follow DeJohnette as he changes the feel beneath a repeating pattern. Then try Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Brian Eno's Another Green World for kindred ideas."],"surman-road-to-saint-ives":["A solo album on which John Surman plays everything himself, layering soprano and baritone saxophones, bass clarinet, keyboards and percussion through overdubbing. The tracks are named after places in Cornwall, from Bodmin Moor to Marazion, and each is a short sound-picture: a drone or keyboard pattern sets the scene, then a melody arrives on reed or saxophone, sometimes doubled, sometimes answered by another of Surman's instruments. The music is pastoral, misty and melodic, with an English folk accent.","Surman had been making overdubbed solo records since the early 1970s, and he returned to the format for ECM at the end of the 1980s. He was born in Tavistock, close to the Cornish border, and the album is a portrait of the landscape of his youth. By this time he was living largely in Norway, and the record has the distance of a memory: Cornwall imagined from far away.","Because Surman plays every part, listen for how the instruments are voiced like a small church choir, with the bass clarinet often taking the bass line and soprano saxophones singing the top. Several melodies use old English modes, the scales of folk song and Tudor church music, and some phrases sound like hymn tunes. The keyboard patterns repeat with tiny changes, a technique closer to minimalism than to jazz.","The record was praised for its atmosphere and has a devoted following, especially in Britain. Some critics thought it closer to ambient music than to jazz, with too little improvisation in the obvious sense. Others value it precisely as a portrait in sound, comparing it to English pastoral composers such as Vaughan Williams. It remains one of the most personal records in Surman's large catalogue.","Read the track titles as a map and imagine the road west. Start with a single piece and listen for the layering: how many Surmans can you count? Notice the folk-like turns in the melodies. Then try Shirley Collins's English folk singing, and Steve Reich's Electric Counterpoint, another work built from one player multiplied."],"eberhard-weber-the-colours-of-chloe":["Four long pieces led by Eberhard Weber's bass, with Rainer Brüninghaus on piano and synthesiser, Ack van Rooyen on flugelhorn, Peter Giger on drums and a section of orchestral cellos. The music moves in slow, shimmering layers: repeated piano figures, a singing bass melody, swelling strings and soft percussion. It sounds closer to film music or impressionist chamber music than to bebop, and Weber's bass tone, high, sustained and glowing, is unlike any jazz bass heard before.","Weber, from Stuttgart, had played in the German free-jazz and rock scenes and with the pianist Wolfgang Dauner. In the early 1970s he designed a modified electric upright bass with a slim body and an extra string, which gave him a sustained, cello-like sound. This, his first album as leader, recorded near Stuttgart in late 1973, was one of the early ECM records that defined the label's polished, spacious production. The cellists came from the local radio symphony orchestra.","Listen to how often the piano and synthesiser play short repeating patterns, very much like the minimalism Steve Reich was then developing in America; Weber was one of the first jazz musicians to build pieces this way. The cellos sometimes play long chords that sound like a second, deeper bass. Notice too how Weber uses his bass to play melodies in a high register usually reserved for horns.","The album was a surprise success, establishing Weber as an important new voice. Some critics heard it as soft, more like soundtrack than jazz. Over time it has been recognised as a model of European jazz composition, and many bassists cite Weber's tone as an inspiration. It also helped define ECM's identity in its first years.","Start with the title track and listen for the moment the bass melody emerges above the shimmering piano. Notice the cellos entering beneath. Then compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, with Pat Metheny's Bright Size Life, and with Weber's next album Yellow Fields, which puts the same ideas into a tighter quartet."],"eberhard-weber-yellow-fields":["Four pieces by a quartet: Eberhard Weber on his custom electric bass, Charlie Mariano on soprano saxophone and the South Indian nagaswaram and shehnai, Rainer Brüninghaus on piano and synthesiser, and Jon Christensen on drums. The music is driving and bright, with repeating keyboard patterns, long rising melodies and a pulse that builds steadily. Mariano's Indian reeds give some passages a piercing, nasal colour, and Weber's bass alternates between deep grooves and high, singing lines.","After the success of The Colours of Chloë, Weber formed a working band, later called Colours, to play his music live. Mariano, an American saxophonist who had played with Charles Mingus, had studied Indian music and brought instruments from that tradition. Recorded in 1975 and released in 1976 by ECM, the album came out at a time when jazz-rock fusion was at its height, but Weber's music took a cooler, more architectural direction.","Listen to the title track for the way the keyboards repeat a pattern while the harmony underneath shifts slowly, so the same figure changes colour; it is a technique taken from minimalism. Christensen plays with a light touch, often around the beat rather than on it. Mariano's nagaswaram, a long double-reed instrument from South Indian temple music, has a sound you will not hear on many other jazz records.","Yellow Fields was well received and is often considered the best record of Weber's Colours band. Some critics thought the music too polished, but many have praised its compositional strength and the unique blend of jazz, Indian and minimalist influences. It remains a favourite among fans of ECM's 1970s catalogue.","Start with the title track and follow how the energy builds over its length. Listen for Mariano's switch to the Indian reed, and notice the difference in colour. Then compare with Shakti's fusion of jazz and Indian music, and with Weather Report's Heavy Weather for a contemporary American take on electric jazz."],"stenson-war-orphans":["A piano trio record by Bobo Stenson with Anders Jormin on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. The material ranges widely: Ornette Coleman's title piece, a song by the Cuban singer-songwriter Silvio Rodríguez, Jormin's own compositions and free, open pieces. The trio plays with great patience, letting melodies unfold slowly, then dissolving them into conversational improvisation where piano, bass and drums are equal voices. The sound is clear and resonant, the mood often melancholy and searching.","Stenson had been a central figure in Scandinavian jazz since the late 1960s, playing with Jan Garbarek, Charles Lloyd and Tomasz Stańko. His trio with Jormin and Christensen began in the late 1980s and became one of the most respected in Europe. This was their second ECM album, following Reflections, and it came after a long period in which Stenson was better known as a sideman than as a leader.","Ornette Coleman's \"War Orphans\" is one of his darkest pieces, a lament; listen to how the trio slows it down further and leaves silences in place of the expected harmony. Christensen rarely plays a steady beat, instead using cymbal colours and short bursts; the pulse lives in Jormin's bass. The Silvio Rodríguez song connects the record to Latin American protest music, a recurring interest in Nordic jazz going back to the Garbarek–Stenson quartet's Witchi-Tai-To.","War Orphans was widely praised and helped establish Stenson as one of the great European piano trio leaders. Critics compared the trio's freedom and interplay to that of Bill Evans and Paul Bley. Some found the free sections hard going. The trio's later records, Serenity and Goodbye, have built on its reputation.","Start with the title track and listen to the space between notes. Follow the bass as the anchor while the drums float. Then hear Ornette Coleman's own records to compare, and Keith Jarrett's Belonging to hear Stenson's Nordic colleagues in a quartet setting from twenty years before."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-from-gagarins-point-of-view":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio, Svensson on piano, Dan Berglund on double bass and Magnus Öström on drums, plays original tunes that sound as much like pop songs as jazz: simple, singable melodies, clear verse-and-chorus shapes and grooves that borrow from rock. The title track opens with a hushed, floating theme that slowly builds; elsewhere there are bluesy, driving pieces and tender ballads. The piano trio format is traditional, but the sound is clean, modern and emotional.","The trio had formed in Stockholm in the early 1990s and already had several albums out in Sweden. This was the record that first reached audiences across Europe through the German label ACT, which had begun to promote a new, rock-influenced European jazz. The title nods to the Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin, the first man in space, and the record's mood of wonder and distance matches it.","Listen to how often Berglund doubles the piano melody on bass, a pop arranging technique rarely used in jazz trios. Öström's drumming often follows rock patterns, with a backbeat, but he plays them with jazz brushes and touch. Svensson sometimes plays the same simple phrase over and over, building intensity through repetition rather than through fast runs, a sign of his love of pop and minimalism.","The album marked the start of the trio's international breakthrough. Critics praised its freshness and accessibility, and younger listeners who had never bought jazz took to it. Some purists complained that it was too close to pop. Over time it has been seen as the beginning of a European piano-trio movement that includes bands like GoGo Penguin.","Start with the title track and notice how simply the melody is stated, then how much the trio builds from it. Listen to the bass doubling the piano. Then compare with Bill Evans's trio to hear the tradition, and with Radiohead's OK Computer for the rock mood the trio admired."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-seven-days-of-falling":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio at full strength: piano, double bass and drums, now coloured by electronic effects, distorted bowed bass and studio processing. Some tracks, like \"Ballad for the Unborn\", are slow, aching songs; others, such as the title track and \"Mingle in the Mincing-Machine\", build to rock-like climaxes with crashing drums and repeated piano riffs. The album moves between delicacy and noise, and its emotional arc is clear even to listeners who know little about jazz.","By 2003 e.s.t. had become one of the most popular jazz groups in Europe, playing rock venues and festivals as well as jazz clubs. Released by ACT, Seven Days of Falling was a major commercial success, especially in Sweden and Germany. The early 2000s were the era of Radiohead's Kid A, and many young jazz musicians were drawn to electronics, atmosphere and song form; e.s.t. led the way among European piano trios.","Listen to Berglund's bowed bass on some tracks: he runs it through distortion and effects pedals so that it sounds like an electric guitar. Svensson sometimes plays inside the piano, damping the strings. The trio recorded largely live in the studio, then added layers and processing afterwards, so the album is a blend of real-time playing and studio construction, more like a rock record than a traditional jazz date.","Seven Days of Falling was a critical and commercial triumph and is often named as e.s.t.'s finest album. It drew a young audience to jazz and filled concert halls across Europe. Traditionalists criticised the use of effects and the pop-like structures; many younger critics saw it as the future of the piano trio. It is now regarded as a classic of 2000s European jazz.","Start with \"Ballad for the Unborn\" for the melody, then play the title track loud. Listen for the distorted bass and how the trio builds a climax. Then compare with Radiohead's Kid A, and with GoGo Penguin's v2.0, which carries this approach further into electronica."],"esbjorn-svensson-trio-leucocyte":["The Esbjörn Svensson Trio's last studio album, and its most experimental. Alongside a few melodic, song-like pieces are long passages of free improvisation, electronic noise, processed piano and distorted bass. The four-part title suite at the end stretches into near-ambient soundscapes, with drones and textures that barely resemble a piano trio. The mood is darker and more restless than on earlier albums, as if the band were deliberately breaking apart its own style.","The music was recorded in Sydney in January 2007 during a tour of Australia, in a few days of largely improvised sessions. Svensson died in June 2008 in a diving accident near Stockholm, at the age of forty-four, and Leucocyte was released a few months later. The album therefore became a farewell, though the band had intended it as a new beginning. Further music from the same sessions was released as the album 301 in 2012.","Listen to how much of the album was improvised in the studio rather than composed in advance: the trio would start with a simple idea or sound and let it grow. Some passages use the piano as a source for electronic processing, so that the notes are smeared and stretched. The title, the name of a white blood cell, reflects the band's interest in organic, cell-like growth in music.","Leucocyte's release so soon after Svensson's death made calm judgment difficult. Many critics praised it as a bold step forward; some found the electronic passages self-indulgent. With time it has come to be heard as evidence of where e.s.t. might have gone, and it is often discussed alongside 301. Svensson's death left a gap in European jazz that many musicians have spoken about.","Start with one of the melodic pieces, then listen to the title suite in full, preferably on headphones. Notice how the piano sound is changed by electronics. Then compare with Seven Days of Falling to hear how far the band had travelled, and with Aphex Twin's ambient work for the textures they were drawn to."],"molvaer-khmer":["Nils Petter Molvær's trumpet floats over electronic beats, samples, ambient textures and guitars. Some tracks are slow and spacious, with long, echoing trumpet notes over drones; others lock into drum-and-bass and trip-hop rhythms, with layered guitar noise and pulsing programmed drums. Molvær's tone is breathy and melancholy, often processed with effects. The album sounds like a cross between Miles Davis's electric period, the ECM aesthetic and the club music of the 1990s.","Molvær had played in the Norwegian group Masqualero and as a sideman for ECM before this, his debut as a leader. It was a surprising release for the label, which was known for acoustic chamber jazz. The late 1990s saw drum-and-bass, trip-hop and electronic music dominate European club culture, and a new generation of Norwegian musicians, sometimes called the Nu jazz scene, set out to combine improvisation with electronics.","Listen to how many sounds come from samples and loops rather than live instruments; Molvær treats the studio as a band member. The guitar of Eivind Aarset adds textures that sound more like synthesisers than a guitar. Notice too that the trumpet often plays very little, a few notes repeated or sustained, leaving room for the electronic backdrop. The title refers to the Khmer people of Cambodia, though the music makes no direct reference to their tradition.","Khmer was a major success and is considered one of the founding albums of Nu jazz. It was widely praised for its fresh approach and sold far beyond the usual audience for an ECM jazz record. Some jazz purists objected to the electronics and simple grooves. It influenced a wave of Nordic musicians who combined jazz and electronica, and it is often named as one of the most important European jazz albums of the 1990s.","Start with the title track and listen to the trumpet floating over the electronic pulse. Then try a faster, beat-driven track. Notice the guitar textures. Then compare with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew and In a Silent Way, the electric records it grows from, and with Massive Attack's Mezzanine for its trip-hop cousins."],"molvaer-solid-ether":["Nils Petter Molvær's second album for ECM pushes further into electronic music. The beats are harder and faster, often drawing on drum-and-bass, while the trumpet weaves above in long, cool, echoing lines. Distorted guitar, samples and turntables add layers of noise and texture. The Norwegian singer Sidsel Endresen appears on some tracks, adding a wordless, strange vocal colour. The album moves between dense, driving pieces and quieter, ambient passages.","After Khmer's success, Molvær toured widely with his band and played jazz festivals and club venues alike. The turn of the millennium was a fertile time for Norwegian music, with labels and musicians exploring the boundaries between jazz, electronica and ambient. Solid Ether built on the first album's ideas, with a band that had grown together on stage, and was again released by ECM, which by now accepted that its catalogue could include electronics.","Listen to how the drum programming and live drumming are mixed, so that you often cannot tell which is which. Molvær processes his trumpet heavily on some tracks, adding delay and filtering until it sounds almost like a synthesiser. The vocal pieces use Endresen's improvised, wordless singing, an approach she developed in her own work. The title is a paradox: something that should be weightless made solid.","Solid Ether was well received, though some critics felt it repeated Khmer's formula. Others praised its harder edge and stronger beats. It confirmed Molvær as the leading figure of Nordic Nu jazz and was popular in clubs as well as jazz venues. Today it is often seen as the second panel of a pair with Khmer.","Start with one of the fast tracks and listen for the trumpet riding over the drum-and-bass rhythm. Then try a slower, ambient piece. Notice how the electronics and live instruments blend. Compare with Roni Size's New Forms and Squarepusher's Hard Normal Daddy for the electronic side, and with Khmer for the starting point."],"rava-the-pilgrim-and-the-stars":["Enrico Rava's trumpet leads a quartet with John Abercrombie on guitar, Palle Danielsson on bass and Jon Christensen on drums. The pieces are Rava's own: lyrical, often melancholy melodies that open into free, spacious improvisation. Rava's tone is warm and singing, with a hint of Italian opera in his phrasing, and Abercrombie's guitar adds a cloud of chords and fast, fluid lines. The music moves between gentle ballads and passages of loose, open energy.","Rava, born in Trieste and raised in Turin, had played with Steve Lacy and Gato Barbieri and spent years in New York and Buenos Aires before this, his first record for ECM. It was recorded in 1975 in Germany. ECM was then building a catalogue of European and American musicians playing a new, open style of jazz, and Rava brought an Italian lyricism to it that would become his trademark.","Listen to how Rava often plays short, simple melodic fragments, then repeats them with small variations, a technique that recalls Miles Davis but has a different, more operatic warmth. The Nordic rhythm section is the same pair, Danielsson and Christensen, who played with Keith Jarrett's European Quartet, giving the record a family resemblance to Belonging. Abercrombie's guitar often fills the role of a piano, but with a lighter, more transparent sound.","The album was well received and established Rava as a European voice to watch. It has remained a favourite among fans of 1970s ECM, though it has been somewhat overshadowed by his later, more famous records. Critics have praised its blend of Italian lyricism and Nordic space.","Start with the title track and listen to Rava's tone, warm and singing. Notice Abercrombie's guitar behind him. Then compare with Miles Davis's Milestones to hear Rava's model, and with Stańko's Balladyna to hear another European trumpeter on ECM in the same year."],"petrucciani-pianism":["Michel Petrucciani leads a piano trio with Palle Danielsson on bass and Eliot Zigmund on drums. The album mixes his own compositions with standards, among them Cole Porter's \"Night and Day\". Petrucciani's playing is bright, powerful and lyrical, with crystal-clear runs, strong left-hand chords and a sense of joy in every phrase. The trio swings with ease, and the ballads have a tender, singing quality that recalls Bill Evans, the pianist he most admired.","Petrucciani, born in France with a rare bone disease that limited his height to about one metre, moved to the United States in the early 1980s, where he joined Charles Lloyd's band. In 1985 he signed with the newly revived Blue Note Records, often described as its first European artist, and Pianism was his first album for the label. He was then in his early twenties and already considered one of the most gifted pianists of his generation.","Listen to his left hand: despite his small frame, Petrucciani produced a full, powerful sound and played wide chords, using a mechanical extension device to reach the pedals. Danielsson, a Swede, had played with Keith Jarrett's European Quartet, so the trio has a hint of the ECM sound. Notice how Petrucciani often restates a melody after his solo with fresh harmonies, a habit learned from Bill Evans.","Pianism was warmly received and helped establish Petrucciani as an international star. Critics praised his technique and lyricism, though some felt he leaned too heavily on Evans. Over time his originality has been more widely recognised, and the album remains a favourite among his Blue Note records.","Start with \"Night and Day\" and listen for how Petrucciani reshapes the familiar melody. Then try one of his own ballads. Notice the strength of his left hand. Then compare with Bill Evans's Sunday at the Village Vanguard to hear his model, and with Keith Jarrett's Standards, Vol. 1."],"petrucciani-power-of-three":["A live trio of piano, guitar and saxophone with no bass or drums: Michel Petrucciani with the guitarist Jim Hall and, on some pieces, the saxophonist Wayne Shorter. The music is intimate and conversational, with standards like \"In a Sentimental Mood\" alongside originals by the players, including Shorter's \"Limbo\". Petrucciani supplies bass lines and rhythm with his left hand, Hall adds soft, glowing chords and melodic lines, and Shorter's saxophone floats above, enigmatic and lyrical.","The concert took place at the Montreux Jazz Festival in July 1986, and the album was released by Blue Note the following year. Hall was a veteran guitarist who had played with Sonny Rollins and Bill Evans; Shorter was a founder of Weather Report and a former member of Miles Davis's quintet. Petrucciani, then in his mid-twenties, was the youngest player on stage, and the concert showed how highly his elders regarded him.","Without a bassist, listen to how Petrucciani and Hall share the role: sometimes the piano plays the bass line, sometimes the guitar. Shorter rarely played acoustic jazz in this period, as Weather Report was electric, so his presence here is a rare treat. Notice how the three musicians leave space for each other, with almost no moments when all three play at full volume.","Power of Three was praised for its intimacy and the rare meeting of these three musicians. Some critics found the lack of a rhythm section limiting; others thought it gave the music unusual clarity. It remains a favourite among fans of Petrucciani and Shorter and is often recommended as an example of chamber jazz in a live setting.","Start with \"In a Sentimental Mood\" and listen to how the three instruments share the melody. Then try \"Limbo\" for Shorter's writing. Notice the absence of drums and how the pulse is kept. Then compare with Jim Hall's playing on Sonny Rollins's The Bridge, and with Weather Report's Heavy Weather to hear Shorter electric."],"grappelli-jalousie":["Stéphane Grappelli and the classical violinist Yehudi Menuhin play popular songs of the 1930s, by composers such as George Gershwin, Cole Porter and Jerome Kern, along with Jacob Gade's tango \"Jalousie\". Grappelli improvises freely with his light, swinging tone; Menuhin, less at home in jazz, mostly plays written parts with his rich classical sound. The contrast between the two violinists is the point: one dances, the other sings, and both enjoy the meeting.","Menuhin, one of the most famous classical violinists of the century, had first played with Grappelli on British television and was charmed by his improvising. Their collaboration led to a series of records for EMI through the 1970s and early 1980s, and this was the first. Grappelli, then in his mid-sixties, was enjoying a late revival of his career in Britain, where he had spent the war years, and these records brought him a wider audience.","Listen to how the two violinists divide the work: Menuhin often takes the melody straight while Grappelli weaves around it, and when Menuhin attempts something like an improvised line, you can hear how different his phrasing is. The arrangements were written for Menuhin, who could not improvise, so his parts are carefully notated jazz phrases. Grappelli's violin has a lighter, faster vibrato, an immediate clue to which is which.","The album was a popular success and introduced Grappelli to many classical listeners. Jazz critics were sometimes dismissive of Menuhin's stiff swing, while classical critics found the music lightweight. Yet the warmth of the meeting has kept these records popular, and they are often cited as early examples of classical–jazz crossover.","Start with \"Jalousie\" and listen for the two different violin tones. Notice how Grappelli plays around the beat while Menuhin stays on it. Then compare with Grappelli and Django Reinhardt's swing version of Bach's double concerto, and with the original Bach for two violins."],"grappelli-young-django":["Stéphane Grappelli leads a string quartet of jazz: his violin, the guitars of Philip Catherine and Larry Coryell, and the double bass of Niels-Henning Ørsted Pedersen. The material includes tunes associated with Django Reinhardt and new pieces in his spirit. The two guitarists play with fire and speed, sometimes trading phrases, while Grappelli's violin glides on top with elegant, effortless swing. The sound is the Quintette du Hot Club updated for the 1970s.","Grappelli had co-founded the Quintette du Hot Club de France with Django Reinhardt in Paris in 1934, and after Reinhardt's death in 1953 he carried their music for decades. By the late 1970s he was touring the world in his seventies. This album, made for the German label MPS, paired him with younger guitarists who had grown up with jazz-rock: Coryell from America and Catherine, a Belgian, both deeply admirers of Reinhardt.","Listen to how Catherine and Coryell divide the role Reinhardt once played alone: one keeps the rhythm, the other solos, then they swap, and sometimes both solo at once. Ørsted Pedersen, a Danish bassist, was a virtuoso who could play melodic lines as fast as a guitarist. Notice how Grappelli's phrasing, after more than forty years, still carries the same light swing as on his records with Reinhardt.","The album was well received and is often cited as one of the best of Grappelli's late career. Critics praised the energy of the guitarists and Grappelli's undimmed playing. Some found the more modern guitar styles out of place, but most heard it as a lively tribute that kept Reinhardt's music alive.","Start with one of the up-tempo tunes and listen to the guitars trading phrases. Then notice Grappelli's violin. Compare with the original Quintette du Hot Club records, Minor Swing and Djangology, to hear how the music has changed and stayed the same."],"joachim-kuhn-colors-live-from-leipzig":["A duo concert by Joachim Kühn on piano and Ornette Coleman on alto saxophone. The music is free and energetic, with Coleman's original tunes as a starting point for wide-ranging improvisation. Kühn's piano moves between dense clusters, flowing runs and passages of classical clarity, while Coleman plays his distinctive bluesy, crying alto lines. The two players listen intently, building and releasing tension together, and the concert has the feel of a long, intense conversation.","Coleman had rarely worked with pianists since the late 1950s, when he abandoned the instrument to free his music from fixed harmony. Kühn, born in Leipzig in 1944, had left communist East Germany in the 1960s and become a leading figure in European free jazz. The concert took place in Leipzig in 1996, Kühn's home city, a few years after German reunification, and was released by Coleman's own Harmolodic label through Verve.","Listen to how Kühn avoids playing standard chords under Coleman: he responds with lines and clusters instead, matching Coleman's harmolodic theory, in which melody, harmony and rhythm are all equal. Kühn had studied classical piano in Leipzig, and some of his runs recall Bach. Notice too the symbolism of the concert itself: an exile returning home with one of the founders of free jazz.","The album was praised as a rare and successful meeting of Coleman with a pianist, and it is often considered one of Kühn's finest recordings. Some critics felt the music lacked the earthiness of Coleman's own bands. Others found it a fascinating glimpse of how Coleman's music could work with a European, classically trained partner.","Start at the beginning and follow how the two players react to each other. Notice Kühn's clusters and Coleman's crying alto. Then compare with Coleman's The Shape of Jazz to Come and his Dancing in Your Head, and with Cecil Taylor's Unit Structures for another approach to free piano."],"shabaka-hutchings-wisdom-of-elders":["Shabaka Hutchings's tenor saxophone with a band of South African musicians, billed as Shabaka and the Ancestors. The music is spiritual jazz: long, chant-like melodies over rolling, polyrhythmic drums and percussion, with the voice of Siyabonga Mthembu adding sung prayers and invocations. The tracks build slowly, the saxophone and alto interweaving like two singers, and the whole album has the feeling of a ceremony, both joyful and solemn.","Hutchings, born in London and raised partly in Barbados, had been visiting South Africa and playing with musicians in Johannesburg's jazz scene. The album was recorded there in a single day in 2015 and released by Gilles Peterson's Brownswood label. It came at the start of a boom in London jazz, but it looked beyond London, connecting British jazz with South Africa's long tradition, from Abdullah Ibrahim to the exiled Blue Notes.","Listen to how the melodies often move in short, repeating cycles, similar to South African church and protest music. Tumi Mogorosi's drumming draws on both jazz and traditional South African rhythms. Notice too that the vocals are not lyrics in the pop sense but chants and invocations, giving the album a ritual feel. The title points to the idea of learning from ancestors, a theme in both African spiritual tradition and jazz.","The album was widely praised and helped make Hutchings one of the most talked-about figures in jazz. Critics compared it to the spiritual jazz of Pharoah Sanders and Alice Coltrane and to South African jazz of the 1960s and 70s. It was seen as a bridge between the London scene and the African diaspora, and its success led to a follow-up, We Are Sent Here by History.","Start at the beginning and let the album build. Listen for the voice and the saxophone weaving together. Notice the drums. Then compare with Pharoah Sanders's Karma and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda, and with Abdullah Ibrahim's Mannenberg for the South African roots."],"shabaka-hutchings-perceive-its-beauty-acknowledge-its-grace":["Shabaka Hutchings's first album under his own name, and a striking change of direction: he plays mostly flutes, including the Japanese bamboo shakuhachi, instead of saxophone. The music is quiet, meditative and spacious, with soft harps, piano, gentle percussion and occasional voices. Guests include the harpist Brandee Younger, the bassist and singer Esperanza Spalding, the electronic producer Floating Points and André 3000 on flute. The mood is calm and contemplative, closer to ambient music than to his earlier, high-energy bands.","Released by Impulse! in 2024, the album followed Hutchings's announcement that he would stop playing saxophone in public at the end of 2023, after more than a decade as the leading saxophonist of the London jazz scene. He had been studying flutes for some years, drawn to their breath and stillness. The album was recorded partly in Los Angeles with musicians from the city's spiritual and ambient scene, including the percussionist and producer Carlos Niño.","Listen to the breath in the flute playing: Hutchings lets you hear air and noise, part of the shakuhachi's tradition, where the breath is as important as the note. The album's slow pace and spare textures are a deliberate contrast to his work with Sons of Kemet and The Comet Is Coming. Notice too how André 3000's appearance links the record to his own flute album, New Blue Sun.","The album was widely praised as a brave and beautiful reinvention, though some fans missed his saxophone and the energy of his earlier bands. Critics placed it in a lineage of spiritual jazz and ambient music, from Alice Coltrane to Harold Budd. It confirmed that Hutchings's search was as much spiritual as musical.","Listen in a quiet space, from beginning to end. Notice the breath in the flute and the spaces between notes. Then compare with Alice Coltrane's Universal Consciousness and Floating Points's Promises with Pharoah Sanders, and with Sons of Kemet's Your Queen Is a Reptile to hear how far he has travelled."],"sons-of-kemet-burn":["Sons of Kemet's first album, with an unusual line-up: Shabaka Hutchings on tenor saxophone and clarinet, Oren Marshall on tuba, and two drummers, Tom Skinner and Seb Rochford. There is no piano or guitar. The tuba plays bass lines and riffs, the two drummers create dense, interlocking rhythms drawn from Caribbean, African and New Orleans traditions, and the saxophone or clarinet soars above. The music is loud, danceable and full of energy, closer to a carnival band than to a jazz club.","Hutchings formed Sons of Kemet in London in 2011, drawing on his Caribbean roots, his experience in London's club and jazz scenes, and his interest in African music. Kemet is an ancient name for Egypt. Released by Naim in 2013, the album was an early landmark in what became a London jazz revival, a scene of young musicians mixing jazz with grime, Afrobeat, calypso and dub. The band won a MOBO award for best jazz act that year.","Listen to how the two drummers play complementary parts rather than doubling each other: one may play a straight pulse while the other plays across it. The tuba's role recalls New Orleans brass bands, where the sousaphone plays bass. Hutchings's clarinet playing reflects his classical training. Notice too the Caribbean rhythms underlying many tracks, from calypso and other Barbadian traditions he grew up with.","Burn was praised for its energy and originality and helped put the new London jazz scene on the map. Critics compared it to Fela Kuti, to New Orleans brass bands and to the Art Ensemble of Chicago. Some found the music repetitive, but many saw it as a bold new direction for British jazz.","Play it loud, and listen for the two drummers. Notice the tuba playing bass lines and riffs. Then compare with Fela Kuti's Zombie for the Afrobeat energy, with the Art Ensemble of Chicago's Fanfare for the Warriors, and with Sons of Kemet's later Your Queen Is a Reptile."],"sons-of-kemet-your-queen-is-a-reptile":["Sons of Kemet's third album: Shabaka Hutchings on tenor saxophone, Theon Cross on tuba, and two drummers, Tom Skinner and Eddie Hick. Each track is named after a woman of African heritage the band honours, among them Harriet Tubman, Angela Davis and Doreen Lawrence, offered as alternatives to the British monarchy. The music is rhythmic, urgent and danceable, with tuba riffs, interlocking drums and soaring saxophone. The poet Joshua Idehen adds spoken words on some tracks.","Released by Impulse!, the historic American jazz label, the album came at the height of the London jazz revival and of debates about race, Britishness and empire after the Brexit vote and the Windrush scandal. Hutchings framed the album as a challenge to the monarchy and a celebration of Black women. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2018, bringing the band to a wide audience.","Listen to the track titles as a list of heroes: each was chosen for a reason, from the Jamaican Maroon leader Nanny of the Maroons to the South African activist Albertina Sisulu. The rhythms draw on Caribbean, African and British club music, with grime and dub echoes. Notice how the tuba often plays the role of a bass guitar in a dancehall band. Hutchings has said the title refers to the unelected power of the monarchy.","The album was widely praised as one of the best jazz records of the decade and was a Mercury Prize nominee. Critics hailed its political force and musical energy. Some found the political framing heavy-handed; others thought it was exactly the point. It confirmed Sons of Kemet as one of the most important bands in British jazz.","Start with the first track and listen for the tuba and drums locking together. Read the track titles and look up the women named. Then compare with Fela Kuti's Coffin for Head of State for another political jazz-adjacent record, and with Little Simz's GREY Area for London's hip-hop of the same moment."],"nubya-garcia-source":["Nubya Garcia's debut full-length album, led by her tenor saxophone with Joe Armon-Jones on keyboards, Daniel Casimir on bass and Sam Jones on drums. The music mixes jazz with dub, cumbia and Caribbean rhythms. The title track is long and dub-influenced, with echoing bass and drums; elsewhere there are ballads and driving, spiritual pieces. Garcia's tenor tone is warm and full, her phrases long and flowing. The Colombian group La Perla appears on one track, adding cumbia rhythms and voices.","Garcia, born in London to a Guyanese mother and a Trinidadian father, grew up in the city's youth jazz programmes, including Tomorrow's Warriors, which nurtured many of the London scene's stars. She had played with Maisha, Nérija and Ezra Collective, and released an EP before this, her debut album, released by Concord Jazz in 2020. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and its release during the pandemic meant it reached audiences mostly through streaming.","Listen to the title track's dub influences: the echoing drums and deep bass recall Jamaican sound systems, which were central to Garcia's London upbringing. The cumbia track with La Perla reflects her interest in Latin American music and connections across the Caribbean. Notice too how Garcia uses space in her solos, letting phrases breathe before building to climaxes.","Source was widely praised and shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, confirming Garcia as one of the leading voices of the London jazz scene. Critics praised her tone and her ability to blend jazz with Caribbean and Latin influences. Some found the album long, but most heard it as a confident and personal debut.","Start with the title track and listen for the dub echoes. Then try the cumbia track with La Perla. Notice Garcia's tone. Then compare with Sonny Rollins's Saxophone Colossus for the tenor tradition, and with Sons of Kemet's Your Queen Is a Reptile and Massive Attack's Blue Lines for London and Bristol's sound-system culture."],"nubya-garcia-odyssey":["Nubya Garcia's second album pairs her tenor saxophone and band with orchestral strings she arranged herself. The music ranges from lush, cinematic ballads to driving grooves rooted in jazz, Caribbean rhythms and R&B. Guest vocalists include the bassist and singer Esperanza Spalding on the title track. Garcia's tenor sound is full and warm as ever, and the strings add depth and drama. The album moves like a journey, from contemplative opening to energetic peaks and reflective close.","Released by Concord Jazz in 2024, four years after Source, Odyssey reflected Garcia's growth as a composer and arranger. She had spent the intervening years touring internationally and widening her ambitions as a writer. The album's string writing places her in a lineage of jazz musicians who have embraced orchestral colour, from Charlie Parker with Strings to Alice Coltrane, and it confirmed the London scene's ambitions beyond the club.","Listen to how the strings are integrated rather than simply added: they often play counter-melodies against Garcia's saxophone, or take over the groove themselves. The arrangements are Garcia's own, and they show a composer thinking in orchestral colours. Notice the Caribbean rhythms underlying some tracks, a continuation of Source's themes. Spalding's vocal on the title track blends with the saxophone in a way that recalls her own chamber-jazz albums.","Odyssey was praised for its ambition and Garcia's growth as a composer and arranger. Critics welcomed the strings and the album's cinematic sweep, though some missed the rawer energy of Source. It was widely seen as a major statement from one of the leading jazz musicians of her generation.","Start with the title track and listen for the strings and Spalding's voice. Notice how the saxophone and strings interact. Then compare with Alice Coltrane's Universal Consciousness for jazz with strings, with Esperanza Spalding's Chamber Music Society, and with Garcia's own Source to hear how her sound has widened."],"jimmy-smith-the-sermon":["The whole first side is one blues, the title track, lasting about twenty minutes at a slow, lazy strut. Jimmy Smith lays down a walking bass line with his feet and left hand while a parade of soloists take long turns: Kenny Burrell on guitar, Tina Brooks on tenor, Lee Morgan on trumpet, Lou Donaldson on alto, and Smith himself, who builds from murmured chords to a full-drawbar roar. Art Blakey keeps a shuffle underneath. The second side holds two shorter, faster tracks with smaller groups.","Smith had arrived in New York only in 1956 and was recording for Blue Note at an astonishing pace, sometimes cutting enough material in one session for several albums. These tracks come from sessions in 1957 and 1958 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Hackensack, and the label released them as albums when it saw fit. Blue Note's founder Alfred Lion encouraged long jam-session takes; the long-playing record had only recently made a twenty-minute blues commercially possible.","The title is not decoration. The tempo is that of a slow church march, and the soloists behave like successive preachers taking the pulpit, each beginning quietly and working towards a shout. Listen to how Smith comps behind the horns with short, stabbing organ chords like a congregation answering. The organ's Leslie speaker, a rotating horn inside a wooden cabinet, is audible as a slow wobble on sustained chords; Smith speeds it up at climaxes.","The album was received as a solid blowing session rather than an event, and some critics found the length self-indulgent. Its reputation grew steadily, partly because its lineup now reads like a roll call of Blue Note's golden age, partly because it codified the slow organ blues as a form. It is now often named as the best entry point to Smith's huge Blue Note catalogue, and to the organ-jazz tradition generally.","Give the title track your full twenty minutes, without skipping. Notice that nothing changes harmonically; the drama is all in tone, rhythm and volume. Follow each soloist's arc, then listen for the moment Smith pulls out all the stops. Afterwards play Horace Silver's The Preacher to hear the same churchgoing impulse in a quintet."],"jimmy-smith-back-at-the-chicken-shack":["A quartet record of medium-tempo grooves: Jimmy Smith on organ, Stanley Turrentine on tenor saxophone, Kenny Burrell on guitar and Donald Bailey on drums. The title track is a loping blues with a riff anyone could hum, stated by organ and tenor together. Elsewhere there is a ballad, a standard and a slow-burning minor blues. With no bass player, Smith supplies the bass on the pedals, and the sound is compact, warm and conversational rather than fiery.","The album was recorded in 1960 but held back by Blue Note until 1963, by which time Smith had left the label for Verve and big-band records. The music therefore belongs to the moment when organ combos were the soundtrack of Black neighbourhood bars across Newark, Philadelphia and Harlem, where a Hammond was often a fixture of the room. Turrentine, newly married to the organist Shirley Scott, was the ideal partner for this kind of bar-room soul.","The title names a kind of place, the roadside chicken joint with an organ in the corner, and the music is tailored to it: tempos that encourage dancing and drinking rather than concentration. Listen for how Smith and Turrentine trade short phrases near the end of the title track, finishing each other's sentences. Burrell rarely solos at length; his chunky rhythm chords hold the groove where a bass player would normally sit.","Released after Smith's departure, it might have been a contractual leftover, but it became one of his most loved records and one of Blue Note's steadiest sellers. Critics now generally rank it alongside The Sermon! as his best work for the label. Turrentine's playing here is often cited as the definitive soul jazz tenor sound, and the album is a common recommendation for listeners new to the organ combo.","Start with the title track and hum the riff after one chorus; that is the point. Then notice the absence of a bass player and listen to Smith's feet doing the work. Compare Turrentine here with his much more lavish CTI album Sugar a decade later to hear how the same voice was dressed up for the 1970s."],"jimmy-smith-the-cat":["Hammond organ against a blaring big band, arranged and conducted by Lalo Schifrin. The title track is a prowling minor-key strut where trumpets and trombones stab out riffs and Smith's organ answers with snarling runs. Elsewhere the album draws on film music, including the theme from the French thriller Joy House, which Schifrin had scored. Tracks are short, tightly arranged and loud, more like soundtrack cues than jam sessions. The organ is always in front, the brass a wall behind.","After moving to Verve, Smith was produced by Creed Taylor, who would later found CTI, and paired with orchestral arrangers to reach a wider market. Schifrin, an Argentine pianist who had worked with Dizzy Gillespie, was beginning a Hollywood career and would soon write the Mission: Impossible theme. The album was a commercial success, and the cinematic, spy-movie atmosphere matched the James Bond boom of 1964.","Listen to how Schifrin voices the brass: rather than swinging section riffs in the Count Basie manner, he uses tight, dissonant chords and sudden silences, the language of film suspense. Smith in turn plays like a lead horn, bending notes with the organ's percussion setting and swells. The album is a direct ancestor of Schifrin's later television and film themes, and a sketch for Taylor's CTI production style.","Jazz critics were cool on Smith's Verve big-band albums, finding them commercial and overarranged compared with his Blue Note sessions. Listeners disagreed, and The Cat sold strongly. Opinion has since softened considerably; the record is admired as a piece of orchestration, and its title track became a standard for organists and a favourite with DJs who prize its punch and brevity.","Play the title track loud, and listen for the call and response between organ and brass. Then put on Schifrin's Mission: Impossible theme and hear the same composer's taste for tension. For contrast, return to The Sermon! and notice how much freedom Smith gave up, and what he gained, by working inside an arrangement."],"jimmy-smith-root-down":["A live set from a Los Angeles club in which Jimmy Smith fronts a young funk rhythm section with electric bass, guitar, drums and congas. The grooves are long and hard: the title track rides a churning bass riff for minutes at a time while Smith digs in with clipped, percussive organ phrases. Covers of soul hits of the day, including Al Green's Let's Stay Together, sit beside blues jams. The sound is raw and close, with audience noise and a room you can feel.","By 1972 the organ-trio circuit that had made Smith a star was shrinking, and soul and funk dominated Black radio. Verve recorded him at the Bombay Bicycle Club in Los Angeles with a band of local session players, among them the bassist Wilton Felder of the Crusaders, aiming to put the organ back into the funk conversation. It was one of Smith's last important records for the label.","The Beastie Boys sampled the title track, Root Down (And Get It), for their own 1994 song Root Down, and the album was a staple of the crate-digging DJs who built early hip-hop beats. Listen to how the electric bass frees Smith's feet: for once he is not playing bass, so his left hand comps chords like a funk guitarist and the organ sounds lighter and more stabbing than on his Blue Note records.","It received little critical attention on release, seen as one more live date from a veteran past his prime. Its afterlife came through sampling and the rare-groove scene of the 1980s and 1990s, when it was reappraised as one of the hardest funk records any jazz organist made. It is now often ranked among Smith's essential recordings, though some jazz writers still prefer his earlier work.","Begin with the title track, and listen for the bass riff and the space between Smith's phrases. Then hear Let's Stay Together and compare it with Al Green's original. If you know the Beastie Boys' Check Your Head and its organ-funk instrumentals, you will hear exactly where they learned the sound."],"grant-green-idle-moments":["Four long tracks played by a sextet: Grant Green on guitar, Joe Henderson on tenor saxophone, Bobby Hutcherson on vibraphone, Duke Pearson on piano, Bob Cranshaw on bass and Al Harewood on drums. The title track, Pearson's slow, hushed blues ballad, lasts about fifteen minutes and sets the mood of the whole record: late-night, unhurried, almost whispered. There is also a version of John Lewis's Django and a gentle Latin-tinged piece. Nothing is loud; everything breathes.","Recorded in November 1963 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1965, the album caught Green at the height of his Blue Note period, when he was playing on dozens of other leaders' sessions. Pearson, a pianist and arranger, was becoming central to the label's sound. The record sits between soul jazz and the more reflective post-bop of Henderson and Hutcherson, both then rising stars at Blue Note.","The title track ran so long because the players misread its structure and took double-length choruses; producer Alfred Lion liked the result, so the other tunes were re-recorded at greater length to fit the LP's sides. Later CD issues include the original shorter takes, which let you hear the change. Listen to Green's solo on the title track: he repeats tiny phrases almost obsessively, a habit that becomes hypnotic at this slow tempo.","The album was not a hit, but it became one of the most cherished records in the Blue Note catalogue, often called Green's finest and one of the great late-night jazz albums. The vibraphone and guitar blend has been widely admired. Debate, where it exists, concerns whether the short or long takes of the shorter tunes are better; the long versions remain the canonical album.","Play the title track at night, quietly, and resist doing anything else. Listen for how the vibraphone and guitar share the same soft register without crowding each other. Then compare Henderson's dry, searching tenor with Green's round tone. For the next step, try Hutcherson's own records or Green's livelier Alive!"],"grant-green-alive":["A live club recording of hard, extended funk. Grant Green leads a band with organ, saxophone, conga and Idris Muhammad's drums through long workouts on soul and pop material of the day, including Kool & the Gang's Let the Music Take Your Mind and Don Covay's Sookie Sookie. Green's guitar is the centre: a dry, biting tone repeating short riffs dozens of times, locked to the drums. The crowd is audible and the band responds to it.","Recorded in 1970 at the Cliché Lounge in Newark, New Jersey, a working club on the organ-bar circuit, during Green's return to Blue Note after a period away from recording. By then soul jazz had turned towards funk, and Green chose current hits by James Brown-era bands rather than standards. Blue Note itself had been sold and was chasing a younger Black audience with more danceable records.","A Tribe Called Quest built Vibes and Stuff on The Low End Theory partly from Green's Down Here on the Ground, the record's slow soul ballad. Listen closely to how Green solos: rather than running scales, he picks one short phrase and repeats it with tiny rhythmic shifts until the band locks to him, a technique closer to a funk guitarist's than a bebop player's. Idris Muhammad's drumming is a lesson in tight, swinging funk.","Jazz critics of the time largely dismissed Green's funk period as commercial. Collectors and DJs in the 1980s and 1990s reversed the judgment, and the album is now valued as one of the most physically exciting live jazz-funk records. Some still argue that Green's earlier Blue Note work is deeper, but few dispute that his rhythmic drive was never stronger than here.","Start with the opening Kool & the Gang cover and let it run; the length is the pleasure. Listen for the repeated guitar figure and the moment the crowd starts shouting. Then hear Down Here on the Ground and put on A Tribe Called Quest's Low End Theory afterwards to catch where it went."],"lou-donaldson-alligator-bogaloo":["A party record of the boogaloo era. The title track is a strutting, almost comic groove built on a riff any listener can remember, with Donaldson's alto bright and tart over Lonnie Smith's organ and a crisp backbeat from the drums. George Benson, then a young organ-band guitarist, plays chunky rhythm and quick, clean solos. The rest of the album mixes more grooves with a ballad and a bebop flourish, all compact and good-humoured.","Recorded in 1967, at the height of the boogaloo dance craze, the album was Donaldson's biggest seller and one of Blue Note's. The label had learned from Lee Morgan's The Sidewinder that a catchy groove at the start of an album could reach jukeboxes and soul radio. Donaldson, a bebop veteran, embraced the move wholeheartedly, and organ combos on the East Coast bar circuit were his natural setting.","The drummer is credited as Leo Morris, the name Idris Muhammad used before his conversion to Islam; his New Orleans training is why the backbeat feels so loose and springy. Listen for Benson's guitar comping on the title track, already precise and rhythmically sure, years before he became a star. Donaldson quotes snatches of pop tunes in his solos, a habit that marks his whole style.","Purists treated the record as evidence of jazz selling out to dance music, and it was rarely discussed seriously at the time. It sold, however, and its reputation has grown among DJs and collectors who value groove records of the period. It is now regarded as a classic of boogaloo jazz, and a document of the early careers of Benson and Lonnie Smith.","Play the title track and move to it; that is its whole purpose. Then listen again for the drums, which are doing far more than they seem. Pair it with Lee Morgan's The Sidewinder and Cannonball Adderley's Mercy, Mercy, Mercy to hear how jazz chased the jukebox in the mid-1960s."],"ramsey-lewis-the-in-crowd":["A piano trio recorded live in a club, with an audience that claps, shouts and laughs throughout. The title track turns a current pop hit into a gospel-blues stomp: Ramsey Lewis plays the tune in big block chords, Eldee Young's bass walks hard and Redd Holt hits a backbeat. The rest of the set mixes standards, blues and pop tunes, each treated the same way, with churchy chords and a light, bouncing swing. It sounds like a very good Friday night.","Recorded in May 1965 at the Bohemian Caverns, a basement club in Washington, D.C., and released on Chess Records' jazz imprint Argo. The trio had been together for a decade and was popular in Chicago but not nationally known. The title song had been a recent hit for the soul singer Dobie Gray, and the trio's version, issued as a single, became a crossover hit and won a Grammy.","The audience is central: its clapping on two and four is effectively a fourth instrument, and Lewis plays to it, leaving gaps for shouts. Listen for the moments in the title track where Lewis thins the texture to a few repeated chords and lets the room fill the gap. The pop song is barely changed; the transformation is entirely rhythmic and harmonic.","It was a commercial phenomenon, a jazz trio album selling to a mass audience, and that made some critics suspicious. Over time it has been understood as part of the broader soul jazz moment alongside Mercy, Mercy, Mercy, and as a model for the live, audience-driven jazz record. Debate continues over whether it counts as jazz at all; Lewis himself shrugged at the question.","Play the title track first and listen for the audience's clapping, then the block chords. Notice how little Lewis decorates the melody. Then hear Ahmad Jamal's Poinciana, an earlier live trio hit, to compare two very different ideas of how a club audience can shape a record."],"ramsey-lewis-sun-goddess":["Ramsey Lewis plugs in. The title track is a long, sunny groove of Fender Rhodes electric piano, chattering percussion, wah-wah guitar and wordless voices chanting in the background, built over a light funk beat. Other tracks move between soft-focus ballads, Latin rhythms and harder funk. Lewis plays acoustic piano too, but the record's identity is the electric keyboard and the warm, communal sound of a band in which everyone is a percussionist as well as a melody player.","By 1974 the trio format that had made Lewis famous was out of step with the times. Maurice White of Earth, Wind & Fire, who had played drums in Lewis's trio in the 1960s, produced the title track and brought members of his band to the session. The record was released on Columbia and became one of the best-selling jazz albums of the year, reaching soul radio and a young audience.","White's presence changes everything: the title track sounds like an Earth, Wind & Fire instrumental, with the same kalimba-like percussion textures and chanted vocals, and Lewis becomes a soloist inside a band sound rather than its centre. Listen to how the electric piano's tremolo blurs into the voices. The record is a hinge between the 1960s soul jazz trio and the late-1970s jazz-funk that would dominate radio.","Jazz critics were mostly dismissive, seeing a fine pianist diluted into background funk. Commercially it was a triumph, and it has since been reassessed by listeners who value the sunny, unforced mood of 1970s jazz-funk. It remains contested ground, a record loved by its audience and still sometimes used as a shorthand for the dilution of jazz in the 1970s.","Begin with the title track and let it unfold; it is about atmosphere, not development. Listen for the voices behind the electric piano. Then play Earth, Wind & Fire's That's the Way of the World to hear the shared sound, and go back to The In Crowd to hear how far Lewis travelled in nine years."],"les-mccann-swiss-movement":["A live festival set by a quintet co-led by pianist and singer Les McCann and tenor saxophonist Eddie Harris, with Benny Bailey on trumpet. The opening track, Compared to What, is a churning protest song over a relentless minor-key vamp, with McCann's raw voice and stabbing piano driving it forward. The remaining tracks are long instrumentals, gospel-tinged blues and grooves where Harris's light tenor and Bailey's trumpet take extended solos over a band that plays as if lives depended on it.","Recorded at the Montreux Jazz Festival in Switzerland in June 1969. McCann and Harris had not played together before; they were thrown together for the festival and barely rehearsed, with Bailey, an American expatriate in Europe, joining at short notice. Compared to What was written by Gene McDaniels and attacks the Vietnam War and the hypocrisy of American life. The album was released by Atlantic and sold extremely well.","The excitement is partly nerves: the band was improvising arrangements on the spot, and you can hear moments where players work out who will solo next. Listen to Bailey, a musician little known in America, whose solos are among the highlights. The title is a pun on the precision of Swiss watches, ironic for a set held together by instinct and goodwill.","It was one of the best-selling jazz albums of its time. Compared to What became a soul jazz standard and a protest anthem covered by many artists. Critics have since praised it as a rare example of a live recording that captures the danger and joy of improvisation at full stretch, while some regard its long instrumental tracks as uneven.","Start with Compared to What and listen to how the band keeps the vamp going while McCann pushes against it. Then listen for Bailey's trumpet. Pair it with Nina Simone's protest songs of the same period, and with Eddie Harris's own The Electrifying Eddie Harris, for the saxophonist's studio sound."],"eddie-harris-the-electrifying-eddie-harris":["Eddie Harris plays tenor saxophone through the Varitone, an electronic attachment that could add an octave below the note, echo and tremolo, giving his horn a thick, doubled, slightly eerie sound. The opener, Listen Here, is a funky riff over a loping groove that stretches for several minutes and became his biggest hit. The rest of the album mixes more riff tunes with ballads and fast pieces, often with a brass section punching behind him.","Harris was signed to Atlantic, which was eager to reach soul radio, and the label's producer Joel Dorn encouraged him to experiment. The Varitone, made by the Selmer company and introduced in the mid-1960s, was one of the first electronic devices for wind instruments. Harris was among its earliest and most committed users. Listen Here was released as a single and crossed over to the rhythm and blues charts.","The octave effect makes a single saxophone sound like two playing in unison, and Harris exploits this for riffs that seem to come from a section rather than a soloist. Listen for the moments where the electronic doubling slips slightly, producing a wobbling, phasing tone. Harris was also a technically gifted player in the high register, and his fast, light runs on the ballads show he did not need electronics to dazzle.","Listen Here was a genuine hit and the album sold strongly. Critics were divided: some heard novelty, others real innovation. The Varitone has largely disappeared, but Harris's experiments are now credited as an important step towards the electric jazz of the following years, alongside Miles Davis's turn to electric instruments. The album remains a favourite of DJs and soul jazz collectors.","Start with Listen Here and focus on the saxophone's doubled sound. Notice how the groove barely changes; the interest is in the timbre and the riff. Then hear Swiss Movement to compare Harris's acoustic tenor on stage, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way for where the electric idea went next."],"donald-byrd-a-new-perspective":["A jazz septet joined by a wordless gospel choir. Donald Byrd's trumpet, Hank Mobley's tenor saxophone, Herbie Hancock's piano and Kenny Burrell's guitar play blues and spirituals while the voices hum, chant and swell in arrangements by Duke Pearson. The best-known piece, Pearson's Cristo Redentor, is slow and solemn, the choir carrying a mournful melody while the trumpet floats above. Elsewhere the music is joyful and swinging, a jazz band in church.","Recorded in January 1963 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, the album reflected Byrd's interest in the roots of Black American music; he was studying composition and would later teach at Howard University. The use of a choir in a small-group jazz record was unusual. The civil rights movement was at its height, and gospel and spirituals carried political as well as spiritual weight.","Cristo Redentor, named after the statue above Rio de Janeiro, is by Pearson, and its slow, swaying melody owes as much to a hymn as to bossa nova. Listen to Hancock, then a young sideman before his own fame, who plays spare, churchy chords. The choir never sings words, which keeps the record from sounding like a concert spiritual and makes the voices another section of the band.","The album was well received and Cristo Redentor became a minor hit, covered by Harvey Mandel and others. Some critics found the choir sentimental. Its standing has grown with time, and it is now seen as an important precursor of the spiritual and gospel-inflected jazz of later decades, and of Byrd's own move towards voices in his 1970s records.","Start with Cristo Redentor and listen to how the choir and trumpet interact. Then hear the faster tracks for Hancock and Mobley. Compare it with Mahalia Jackson's recordings to hear the gospel tradition it draws on, and with Black Byrd to hear Byrd's later use of voices."],"donald-byrd-black-byrd":["Smooth, insistent jazz-funk produced by Larry Mizell. Donald Byrd's trumpet, often treated with echo, floats over electric bass lines, clavinet, synthesiser and soft group vocals that chant short phrases rather than sing songs. The tracks are compact, built on repeating grooves rather than chord changes, and the mood ranges from the brooding title track to the airy Flight Time and the laid-back Mr. Thomas. It sounds more like a soul record than anything Blue Note had made.","Byrd was teaching at Howard University and looking for a new direction when he began working with Larry and Fonce Mizell, brothers with Motown experience who ran a production company. Their combination of jazz soloing, soul production and catchy vocal hooks was entirely new for Blue Note. Black Byrd became the best-selling album in the label's history up to that point, reaching soul and pop audiences.","DJ Premier sampled Flight Time for Nas's N.Y. State of Mind, and the album was a deep well for hip-hop producers generally. Listen to how little Byrd actually plays: the trumpet is one colour among many, entering briefly, and much of the record's identity comes from the bass and the vocal hooks. The Mizells' Motown background shows in the tight, layered rhythm arrangements.","Jazz critics were scathing, and Byrd was accused of abandoning jazz for commerce. The public disagreed, and the album sold in huge numbers. Its reputation was transformed by hip-hop sampling and the rare-groove scene, and it is now seen as a founding record of jazz-funk. The argument over whether it is jazz at all has never fully gone away.","Start with Flight Time and listen to the bass and vocal hooks, then put on Nas's N.Y. State of Mind. Then hear the title track. Compare it with A New Perspective to hear Byrd's career in two records, and with Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters, from the same year."],"donald-byrd-places-and-spaces":["The Mizell production style at its most lush. Strings swirl over soft disco-era beats, voices sing short, airy hooks, and Donald Byrd's trumpet floats in and out like a guest at his own party. The opener, Change (Makes You Want to Hustle), is a gliding dance track; the title piece is slower and more dreamlike; Wind Parade is light and buoyant. Everything is polished, warm and designed for movement rather than close listening.","By 1975 the Mizell brothers were among the most successful producers in jazz and soul, working with Byrd, Bobbi Humphrey, Johnny Hammond and others. Disco was rising, and the hustle was the dance of the moment. Byrd was also guiding his Howard University students in the Blackbyrds, a group whose records sold heavily. Places and Spaces was his most sophisticated collaboration with the Mizells.","Listen for how the strings are used: not as a romantic wash but as rhythmic punctuation, short and crisp, borrowed from Philadelphia soul production. The record's sense of space comes from echo applied to voices and trumpet, a studio technique that makes the sound seem to float. Byrd plays fewer notes than on any earlier record, and his solos are almost melodic decorations.","Jazz critics largely ignored or dismissed it. It was a solid seller and has become one of the most loved records of the Mizell sound, reappraised through DJ culture and sampling. Many now regard it as the high point of Byrd's 1970s work. Its sleekness divides listeners: some hear a perfect mood record, others the beginning of jazz dissolving into background music.","Play Change (Makes You Want to Hustle) first and notice the crisp strings and the light beat. Then hear the title track for its dreamlike atmosphere. Compare it with Black Byrd to hear how the Mizell sound smoothed out in two years, and with Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine for kindred warmth."],"bobbi-humphrey-blacks-and-blues":["Bobbi Humphrey's flute floats over grooves produced by Larry Mizell: electric bass lines, chiming electric keyboards, light percussion and soft group vocals singing short hooks. The album is compact, six tracks, and the mood is warm, airy and unhurried. Harlem River Drive is the best known, a gliding groove with a vocal refrain, but every track works the same way, with the flute as the singing voice and the rhythm section never letting the groove drop.","Humphrey had been signed to Blue Note in 1971 and her first records were conventional soul jazz. Producer Larry Mizell, fresh from Donald Byrd's Black Byrd, gave her the same treatment, and the result sold well. Female instrumentalists were rare at Blue Note and in jazz generally, and Humphrey's success opened doors. The album came out in the same year as Byrd's record and shares much of its sound.","Listen to how the flute is recorded: close, breathy and slightly echoed, so that it sounds almost like a voice. The Mizells write the melodies to suit the flute's range rather than adapting horn lines. Harlem River Drive names an actual Manhattan highway, and the music evokes cruising rather than any specific story. The vocal hooks are sung by a small group, not soloists.","It was a solid seller and helped establish the Mizell sound. Critics paid it little attention. In the 1980s and 1990s it became a favourite of British jazz-funk and rare-groove DJs, and its reputation has grown. It is now seen as one of the defining records of 1970s Blue Note, though still rarely discussed in mainstream jazz histories.","Begin with Harlem River Drive and listen for the flute's breathy, singing tone. Then play the title track. Compare it with Donald Byrd's Black Byrd to hear the shared production, and with Lonnie Liston Smith's Expansions for another take on gentle, danceable jazz."],"george-benson-breezin":["Six tracks of polished, relaxed guitar music with string arrangements by Claus Ogerman. The title track, by Bobby Womack, is a gliding instrumental where George Benson's clean, round guitar plays the melody over a light groove. The one vocal, This Masquerade by Leon Russell, has Benson singing in a warm, supple voice and scatting in unison with his own guitar solo. Everything is smooth, tasteful and carefully produced, with space for long, fluent guitar improvisations.","Benson had moved from CTI to Warner Bros., where producer Tommy LiPuma aimed to make a record that could reach pop radio without losing the jazz soloing. The sessions took place in Los Angeles. The album became the first jazz record to reach the top of the Billboard album chart and went multi-platinum. This Masquerade won the Grammy for Record of the Year.","The unison singing and guitar is the trick: Benson sings exactly the notes he plays, a technique he had practised for years, and the combination creates a timbre that sounds like a new instrument. Listen to the solos, which are genuinely complex jazz improvisations, fast and harmonically sophisticated, even when the setting is soft. The string arrangements are subtle, placed far back in the mix.","It was a massive commercial success and earned several Grammys. Jazz critics were divided; some saw a great guitarist selling out, others noted the quality of the playing. It is often cited as a founding record of smooth jazz, which colours its reputation. Many guitarists regard the soloing as among Benson's finest, and the album remains a touchstone of 1970s production.","Start with This Masquerade and listen for the moment the voice and guitar become one. Then hear the title track for Benson's tone. Compare with Wes Montgomery, Benson's model, and with Benson's earlier organ-band work on Lou Donaldson's Alligator Bogaloo to hear how far he came."],"grover-washington-jr-mister-magic":["Four long tracks of warm, sensual jazz-funk. The title track is a slow, sultry groove that stretches over nine minutes, with Grover Washington Jr.'s soprano saxophone singing a simple, insistent melody over electric piano, bass and percussion. Bob James's arrangements add strings and brass in soft layers. The other tracks are similar: relaxed tempos, long solos, and a mood that is intimate rather than energetic, built for late-night listening.","The album was released on Kudu, Creed Taylor's label for soul-oriented records, an offshoot of CTI. Bob James, Taylor's chief arranger, was at the height of his influence. The title track, written by Ralph MacDonald and William Salter, became a crossover hit and the album crossed from jazz to soul and pop audiences. It was Washington's commercial breakthrough. The CTI family of labels gave its records matching gatefold sleeves and glossy photography, so the look sold the mood before a note was heard.","Listen to the percussion on the title track: the congas and shakers give the groove its gentle propulsion, more important than the drums. Washington's soprano tone is round and vocal, without the nasal edge many soprano players have. The title track's length is deliberate; the melody returns again and again, each time slightly varied, and the effect is hypnotic.","It was a major hit and established Washington as a star. Jazz critics were mixed, though many admired his tone. It is often cited as a key record in the development of smooth jazz, and the title track has been widely covered. Its reputation has held up better than much of the genre it helped create.","Start with the title track and let it run; the length is part of the effect. Listen for the percussion and the soprano's singing tone. Then hear Winelight, from five years later, and Bob James's One, to hear the arranger's hand."],"grover-washington-jr-winelight":["Six tracks of polished, intimate music. The title track is a slow, glowing groove for soprano saxophone. The best known, Just the Two of Us, is a love song sung by Bill Withers, with Grover Washington Jr.'s saxophone weaving around the voice and taking a long solo. Marcus Miller plays bass, Steve Gadd drums, and the sound is soft, warm and impeccably recorded. Nothing is rushed; every note sits in place.","Washington had moved to Elektra, and the album was produced with Ralph MacDonald and William Salter, who co-wrote Just the Two of Us with Withers. The song became a huge crossover hit and won the Grammy for Best R&B Song; the album won for Best Jazz Fusion Performance. It defined the sound of what would soon be called quiet storm and smooth jazz radio.","Listen for Marcus Miller's bass, which is unusually melodic and rhythmically playful, foreshadowing his later work with Miles Davis. Withers's vocal is relaxed and conversational, recorded close, and Washington's saxophone answers him like a second singer; the light, Latin-tinged percussion keeps the song moving so it never sags into a slow ballad.","It was a huge commercial success and won Grammys. Jazz critics were mostly dismissive, seeing it as background music. Over time it has become a touchstone of smooth jazz and quiet storm radio, loved by its audience and sometimes mocked by jazz purists. Just the Two of Us has been covered and sampled widely, and remains one of the most recognisable songs of its era.","Start with Just the Two of Us and listen for how the saxophone and voice converse. Then hear the title track for Washington's soprano tone. Compare with Mister Magic to hear how his sound smoothed further, and with George Benson's Breezin' for a parallel crossover."],"roy-ayers-everybody-loves-the-sunshine":["Four minutes of slow, shimmering heat. Roy Ayers's vibraphone and synthesisers lay down a hazy, swaying groove; voices, his own among them, sing a gentle, repeated refrain about the pleasures of a summer day. The tempo is languid, the bass round and slow, and the sound feels like warm air moving. There is no solo to speak of; the song is all mood, a single texture held for its whole length.","Ayers recorded the song with Roy Ayers Ubiquity for an album of the same name on Polydor. He had moved from hard bop vibraphone to soul, funk and early disco, and was working in New York with a rotating band. The song was not a major hit on release but became a staple of DJs and of the community block parties that would shape hip-hop.","Mary J. Blige's 1994 song My Life is built on it, and producers from many genres have sampled or replayed its chords and refrain. Listen closely to the vibraphone: Ayers uses it for sustained, shimmering chords rather than fast runs, blending it with synthesiser so the two instruments become one hazy sound. The song's simplicity is its strength; it barely changes, and that is the point.","It was a modest success on release. Its reputation has grown enormously through sampling and DJ culture, and it is now regarded as one of the defining soul-jazz songs of the 1970s, a symbol of summer for several generations. Some jazz critics still see Ayers's 1970s work as a loss, but few dispute the song's influence.","Play it on a warm day and do nothing else. Listen for the blend of vibraphone and synthesiser, then for the slow bass. Then hear Mary J. Blige's My Life to hear how it was reused, and Donald Byrd's Places and Spaces for kindred warmth."],"lonnie-liston-smith-expansions":["Lonnie Liston Smith and the Cosmic Echoes play gentle, spacious jazz-funk with a spiritual undertow. The title track rides a buoyant, endlessly circling bass line under washes of electric piano and synthesiser, with a soft vocal from Donald Smith, the leader's brother, delivering a message of consciousness and unity. Other tracks lean towards Latin percussion or slower, meditative drift. The textures are soft, the grooves insistent, the mood hopeful and calm.","Smith had played with Pharoah Sanders, whose spiritual jazz albums used drones and devotional moods, and with Miles Davis's electric band of 1972 and 1973. On Bob Thiele's Flying Dutchman label he combined both influences with funk rhythms. Expansions sold well and found an audience well beyond jazz, especially in British clubs where it became an anthem of the jazz-funk scene.","Listen to the bass line on the title track: it never stops, a single circular figure that makes the track as much a trance record as a funk one. The electric piano chords are held and allowed to decay, creating a floating sense of space. The vocal is spoken-sung rather than belted, which keeps the record meditative. It is the Pharoah Sanders influence carried onto the dance floor.","It was a commercial success and became a dance-floor classic, particularly in Britain. Jazz critics were mostly uninterested at the time, but its reputation has grown through DJ culture, and it is now seen as a crucial bridge between spiritual jazz and later dance music. It is often cited by house and broken-beat producers as an influence.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass line, then the electric piano washes. Then hear Pharoah Sanders's Karma, to which Smith contributed, to hear where the spiritual mood comes from. Compare with Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine for another warm, communal groove."],"freddie-hubbard-red-clay":["A quintet record that blends hard bop with funk and electric sound. The title track rides a loping, insistent bass line and a funky drum groove, with Freddie Hubbard's brassy trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor stating a punchy theme before long, fiery solos. Herbie Hancock plays electric piano, Ron Carter bass and Lenny White drums. The album has four long tracks, all adventurous but grounded in groove, with room for real improvisation.","Recorded in January 1970 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, it was one of the first releases on Creed Taylor's new CTI label. Taylor wanted jazz with high production values and wider appeal, and he gathered the best players of the day. Hubbard had been a Blue Note star; here he was moving towards a more electric, funk-influenced sound, in step with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew of the same period.","Listen to Hancock's electric piano, which he was just beginning to explore; its slightly distorted, bell-like tone shapes the record's sound. The title track's bass line is a simple repeated figure, but Carter varies it subtly throughout. The record balances CTI's polish with the raw energy of a blowing session; there are no strings or overdubs, just a quintet in a room.","It was well received and became one of CTI's most admired records, often cited as Hubbard's finest album. Critics generally praised its balance of groove and improvisation. It has been less controversial than later CTI albums with strings, and is regarded as a model of early-1970s jazz that embraced electric sound without losing its edge.","Start with the title track and listen to the bass line, then follow Hubbard's solo, which builds to dazzling high notes. Compare with Herbie Hancock's Maiden Voyage, on which Hubbard and Carter also play, and with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for a more radical take on electric jazz."],"freddie-hubbard-first-light":["Freddie Hubbard's trumpet set against lush orchestral arrangements by Don Sebesky. The title track, Hubbard's own, is a long, slowly building piece where strings and woodwinds lay down a soft, shimmering backdrop and the trumpet rises above in long, lyrical lines. The album also includes a version of a Paul McCartney song and a film theme by Henry Mancini. The mood is warm, cinematic and polished, with the trumpet always in front.","Creed Taylor had built CTI on lush production, and Sebesky was his signature arranger. The album was a commercial success and won a Grammy. Hubbard, a fiery hard bop player, was here presented as a romantic soloist, part of Taylor's strategy to reach a wider audience. The sessions used top New York studio players of the CTI circle. The album was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, as nearly all CTI records were.","Listen to how Sebesky voices the strings: not as a continuous wash but in short, shifting chords that respond to Hubbard's phrases. Hubbard's tone on the title track is unusually soft and rounded, closer to a flugelhorn than his usual brassy sound. The album shows a different side of a player usually associated with speed and power, and that restraint was a deliberate choice.","It won a Grammy and sold well. Critics were divided: some praised Hubbard's lyricism, others found the strings sentimental. It has come to represent the polished CTI sound, admired and criticised in equal measure. Many listeners regard the title track as one of Hubbard's most beautiful recordings, while purists prefer Red Clay.","Start with the title track and listen to how the trumpet floats above the strings. Then compare with Red Clay to hear Hubbard without an orchestra. For context, hear Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain, an earlier and more austere model of trumpet with orchestra."],"bob-james-one":["Bob James's first CTI album mixes funk grooves, orchestral arrangements and classical pieces. It includes a funk version of Mussorgsky's Night on Bald Mountain, with strings and brass rising over a hard backbeat, and In the Garden, built on Pachelbel's Canon. There is also a version of Roberta Flack's Feel Like Making Love and the brooding closing track Nautilus, a slow groove with a chiming Fender Rhodes and a stark drumbeat.","James was Creed Taylor's chief arranger, and this album was his debut as a leader for the label. CTI's records were known for their lush production and crossover ambitions, and James combined jazz, funk, classical and pop in a single package. The album sold well and established him as a leading figure in the emerging smooth jazz and jazz-funk scenes.","Nautilus became one of the most sampled tracks in hip-hop; Ghostface Killah's Daytona 500 is one of many records built on it. Listen to the track's bare opening, just Rhodes and drums, which is what producers wanted. In the Mussorgsky, notice how the famous swirling string figures are given to synthesiser and electric piano, while the brass keeps the original's menace over the backbeat.","Jazz critics were sceptical of the classical adaptations, seeing them as gimmicks. The album sold well and James's career flourished. Its reputation was transformed by hip-hop, which made Nautilus a foundational sample. It is now valued both as a CTI artefact and as a source text for a later genre, though debate over the classical adaptations continues.","Start with Nautilus and listen to the opening; then imagine it looped under a rapper's voice. Then hear Night on Bald Mountain and compare it with Mussorgsky's original. Follow with Two, his next album, which has the Bizet adaptation and Take Me to the Mardi Gras."],"bob-james-two":["Bob James's second CTI album continues the mix of funk, orchestral arrangements and classical adaptation. It opens with Take Me to the Mardi Gras, Paul Simon's song, which starts with a few bars of solo bells and drums before a gentle groove takes over. Farandole is a funk version of Bizet's L'Arlésienne, with the march tune carried by brass over a hard backbeat. The rest of the album is smooth, tuneful and polished, with James's Fender Rhodes at the centre.","The album followed the success of One and used the same team of top New York studio players. CTI was at the height of its influence, though Creed Taylor's empire would soon run into financial trouble. James was arranging for many CTI artists, and his own records served as showcases for his production style, blending jazz soloing with pop melody and classical grandeur.","The opening bells and drums of Take Me to the Mardi Gras became one of the most famous breaks in hip-hop: Run-DMC built Peter Piper, on Raising Hell, on them. Listen to how the bells ring alone before the band enters; that brief isolation is what made the passage usable by DJs. In Farandole, listen for how the stately march tune keeps its dignity even over a drum kit.","It sold well, and like One received mixed reviews from jazz critics. Its afterlife in hip-hop has given it a new reputation, and the Mardi Gras break is among the most widely used samples in rap history. The Bizet adaptation remains divisive: some hear an imaginative crossover, others a cheapening of the classical source.","Play the opening of Take Me to the Mardi Gras and listen for the bells alone, then put on Run-DMC's Peter Piper. Then hear Farandole beside Bizet's original. Compare with One for the Mussorgsky adaptation and with Deodato's Also sprach Zarathustra for another CTI take on a classical source."],"bob-james-angela":["A wistful, gently swinging melody played on Fender Rhodes electric piano, with soft synthesiser and a light rhythm section. The tune moves in a lilting rhythm with a hint of melancholy, rising and falling over a patient bass line. In its full-length version on James's album Touchdown it stretches out with room for improvisation; on television the opening was trimmed to under a minute. It is instantly recognisable from its first phrase.","James wrote it for the American sitcom Taxi, which premiered in 1978 and ran until 1983. The show followed a group of New York cab drivers, and the music, played over the opening shots of a cab crossing the Queensboro Bridge, set a tone of quiet loneliness that the comedy played against. James had started his own label, Tappan Zee, distributed by Columbia, after leaving CTI.","The theme does not hurry: it lets a single phrase hang, then answers it a step lower, a shape that suits the show's bittersweet character better than any upbeat sitcom jingle would. Listen to the Rhodes tone: James uses the instrument's natural bell-like attack and slow decay to give the melody a slightly blurred, nostalgic quality. The rhythm section stays very light, almost brushed, so the melody can float.","It became one of the most famous television themes ever written and introduced James's music to millions who never heard his albums. Critics have praised its economy of mood, and it is often cited as a model of how a short theme can define a show. Its success also tied James firmly to the smooth jazz label, for better or worse.","Listen to the full version on Touchdown and follow the melody through its gentle variations. Notice the Rhodes tone. Compare with Henry Mancini's Peter Gunn, another jazz-inflected television theme, and with Bernard Herrmann's Taxi Driver, a darker vision of the same city in the same decade."],"deodato-also-sprach-zarathustra":["A funk version of the opening of Richard Strauss's tone poem. The famous rising trumpet motif is stated over a hard, Latin-tinged groove of electric bass, conga and drums, then the piece opens out into long, jazzy improvisations on electric piano and guitar. The original's grand fanfare becomes a vamp, repeated and varied. Brass and strings punctuate the groove. The track runs about nine minutes on the album, much shorter as a single.","Deodato, a Brazilian arranger working in New York, recorded it for his CTI album Prelude. Stanley Kubrick's film 2001: A Space Odyssey, released in 1968, had made Strauss's opening world famous, and the track's parenthetical title acknowledged the film. Released as a single, it became a major pop hit and won a Grammy. It was one of the biggest hits CTI ever had.","Listen to how Deodato handles Strauss's famous opening: he keeps the rising three-note motif but replaces the original's organ pedal and timpani with a funk groove, so the grandeur becomes a dance. The Brazilian rhythmic feel, a light, syncopated lilt, is subtle but distinctive. After the opening statement, the track becomes essentially a jazz jam, with Strauss abandoned.","It was a huge commercial success. Classical listeners were mostly bemused, jazz critics mostly dismissive. Over time it has been seen as a defining example of 1970s crossover, alongside other jazz-funk adaptations of classical music. Its kitsch value has grown, but so has appreciation of its groove and arrangement. It also opened the door for many disco-era classical remakes.","Listen first to Strauss's original opening, then to Deodato's version, noticing what is kept and what is changed. Then follow the improvisations after the motif. Compare with Bob James's adaptations of Mussorgsky and Bizet for other CTI takes on classical themes."],"stanley-turrentine-sugar":["Three long tracks led by Stanley Turrentine's big, bluesy tenor. The title track is a slow, sultry minor-key groove where Turrentine's smoky tone stretches out over electric piano, organ and a patient bass line, followed by solos from Freddie Hubbard on trumpet and George Benson on guitar. The album also includes a version of John Coltrane's Impressions, played fast and hard. The sound is CTI-polished but rooted in soul jazz and the blues.","Turrentine had moved from Blue Note to Creed Taylor's CTI, and this was his first album for the label. Taylor surrounded him with stars, including Ron Carter on bass, and the result sold strongly. The title track became Turrentine's signature tune. It was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, whose clean, warm sound defined both Blue Note and CTI. The label's photographic sleeves made it look like a luxury product.","Listen to how Turrentine sits just behind the beat on the title track, stretching each phrase so it seems to lag and then catch up. The organ and electric piano are both present, giving the groove a doubled keyboard texture. The Coltrane cover shows that Turrentine, often thought of as a soul player, could handle modal jazz at speed.","It was well received and sold well, becoming one of CTI's best-loved records. Critics praised Turrentine's tone and the quality of the supporting players. It has been less criticised than later, more heavily produced CTI albums, and is seen as a model of soul jazz updated for the 1970s. The title tune has since become a jam-session standard.","Start with the title track and listen to Turrentine's tone, then the solos by Hubbard and Benson. Then hear the Coltrane cover. Compare with Back at the Chicken Shack, where Turrentine plays with Jimmy Smith a decade earlier. The voice is the same; only the setting changed."],"hubert-laws-the-rite-of-spring":["Hubert Laws's flute plays classical pieces in jazz arrangements by Don Sebesky. The title track condenses parts of Stravinsky's ballet into a groove, with Laws's flute carrying melodies over electric bass and percussion. There are also versions of Fauré's Pavane, Debussy's Syrinx, the solo flute piece, and a movement from Bach's Third Brandenburg Concerto. The arrangements are lush, the flute tone pure, and the mood moves between meditative and propulsive.","Laws was a classically trained flautist who also played in symphony orchestras. Creed Taylor's CTI label was known for polished crossover records, and this album was one of the most ambitious, applying jazz techniques to a classical repertoire. The late 1960s and early 1970s saw a wave of such crossover projects, among them Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach of 1968, which had turned Bach into a synthesiser best-seller.","Listen to the Syrinx, originally a piece for one unaccompanied flute, and notice how Laws's pure, vibrato-light classical tone survives the jazz setting. The Stravinsky arrangement keeps fragments of the original's rhythms but turns its violence into a groove. The Bach is played almost straight, with a jazz rhythm section adding a light swing. The album's interest lies in these contrasts.","It received mixed reviews: some praised Laws's skill and Sebesky's arrangements, others found the classical adaptations superficial. It has become a cult item, valued by flautists and collectors of CTI. Debate over its classical adaptations continues; some see it as a bridge between traditions, others as a gimmick. It remains an unusual listening test of how far a groove can carry a score.","Start with Syrinx and compare it with a classical recording. Then hear the title track beside Stravinsky's original, noticing which rhythms survive. Compare with Bob James's adaptations of Mussorgsky and Bizet, and with Deodato's Strauss, for other CTI takes on the classics."],"larry-young-unity":["An organ quartet that sounds nothing like the soul jazz organ combos. Larry Young plays Hammond organ with long, flowing lines and sustained chords, not churchy blues, while Woody Shaw on trumpet, Joe Henderson on tenor saxophone and Elvin Jones on drums push through complex, modal compositions. The opener, Shaw's Zoltan, begins with a march-like theme before exploding into fast swing. There is also a version of Monk's Dream and a ballad. The energy is relentless.","Recorded in November 1965 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1966. Young had begun as a soul jazz organist but was moving towards the modal jazz of John Coltrane, whose drummer Elvin Jones plays here. Shaw, then twenty, contributed three compositions. Blue Note was recording a wide range of styles, from boogaloo to the avant-garde. Van Gelder's close miking puts the organ and drums right in your face.","Zoltan, named for the composer Zoltán Kodály, borrows a march theme from his Háry János before turning into modal jazz. Listen to Young's left hand: rather than walking a bass line in the usual organ way, he often plays sustained chords, giving the music a floating, open feel. Jones's drumming is dense and polyrhythmic, as with Coltrane, and Young matches him.","It was not a commercial success but has become one of the most admired organ jazz records ever made, often cited as the moment the organ entered modern jazz. Critics praise its originality and the quality of Shaw's compositions. It is regularly included in lists of essential Blue Note records and is a touchstone for organists who want to move beyond soul jazz.","Start with Zoltan and listen for the march theme, then the explosion into swing. Notice how Young's organ sounds unlike Jimmy Smith's. Then hear Coltrane's A Love Supreme for the modal influence, and The Sermon! for the organ tradition Young was leaving."],"richard-groove-holmes-misty":["Erroll Garner's ballad played by an organ trio at a slow, swinging medium tempo. Richard Holmes states the melody with big, swelling organ chords, then walks a deep bass line under his own improvisations, building gradually without ever losing the tune. Gene Edwards's guitar comps softly, and the drums keep a light, patient swing. The result is warm, relaxed and gently grooving, a ballad turned into something you could dance slowly to.","Holmes recorded it for the album Soul Message on Prestige, which specialised in organ combos for the Black jukebox and bar market. The track was released as a single and became a surprise crossover hit, reaching pop and rhythm and blues audiences. Garner had written the tune in 1954 and it had become a standard through vocal versions, especially Johnny Mathis's.","Listen to Holmes's bass line: he plays it with his left hand and feet together, giving it a weight and punch that few organists matched, and that is the reason for his nickname. The tempo is faster than most versions of the ballad, which keeps it from becoming sentimental. The organ's swell pedal is used for dramatic crescendos at the ends of phrases.","It was a hit and made Holmes a star of the organ-jazz circuit. Critics generally praised it as a fine example of the organ trio's ability to transform a standard. It remains his best-known recording and a staple of organ-jazz collections, though some prefer his harder-swinging work. It showed that an organ trio could sell a ballad, not only a blues.","Listen first to the bass line, then the chords. Compare with Erroll Garner's own piano version to hear how the organ changes the tune. Then hear Jimmy Smith's The Sermon! for the organ trio's other side, the long, slow blues."],"the-kinks-face-to-face":["Fourteen short songs, every one by Ray Davies, and the first Kinks album that sounds like a single sensibility rather than a run of singles with filler. The guitars that made 'You Really Got Me' are still there, but the record is dominated by music-hall bounce, harpsichord, and small character sketches: a session musician, a dandy, a family selling up its grand house, a holidaymaker in a fake Hawaii. It closes its first run with the lazy, descending 'Sunny Afternoon'.","Made at Pye in London across 1966, a year in which Ray Davies had suffered a collapse from exhaustion and the band had been banned from touring America after a union dispute. Cut off from the US market that every British group was chasing, Davies turned inward and toward England: its class system, its suburbs, its taxman. The album came out in the autumn, a few months after 'Sunny Afternoon' had topped the British chart, and in the same season as Revolver.","Davies wanted sound effects linking every track, a kind of radio play, and Pye cut most of them; the survivors, a ringing telephone at the start, thunder in 'Rainy Day in June' and surf in 'Holiday in Waikiki', are the fossil of that plan. Listen to 'Fancy', where the guitar holds a single droning note under the voice in the manner of an Indian drone, a quieter cousin of the Byrds' raga experiments the same year.","Reviews were warm but sales were modest, and the album was overshadowed by the louder events of 1966. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often named as one of the first rock albums organised around a point of view rather than a sequence of hits. Arguments continue over whether Pye's removal of the linking effects spoiled a concept album or saved a set of songs from a gimmick.","Begin with 'Session Man', where the harpsichord and the joke about hired-hand musicians are the same gesture, then 'Sunny Afternoon' for Davies's voice at its most deadpan. Listen to how often the tunes come from the music hall rather than the blues. Follow it with Village Green and Waterloo Sunset to hear the idea mature, and with Revolver to hear what else was possible that year."],"the-kinks-arthur":["Twelve songs following one ordinary Englishman, a carpet-layer named Arthur, through the twentieth century: Victorian childhood, two world wars, a suburban semi named Shangri-La, and a son who emigrates to Australia. The music is fuller and louder than Village Green, with brass and strings over a tough rhythm section, and two long pieces, 'Shangri-La' and 'Australia', that stretch out past five minutes. 'Victoria' opens it with a fanfare-like riff that is half celebration, half sneer.","Ray Davies wrote the songs as the score for a Granada television play he devised with the novelist Julian Mitchell; the play was never made, and the album went out alone in October 1969. Arthur was modelled on Davies's brother-in-law, who had emigrated to Australia with Davies's elder sister. It was the first album with John Dalton on bass after Pete Quaife left, and it arrived a few months after the Who's Tommy, which took the attention and the label 'rock opera'.","Listen to how 'Shangri-La' changes speed and mood in sections, from a hushed, almost hymn-like opening to a jaunty music-hall middle and a massive orchestral climax; the structure mirrors a man's pride in his little house curdling into the realisation that it is a trap. 'Some Mother's Son', about a soldier killed in the First World War, sets that scene to the gentlest tune on the record, and the gentleness is the point.","American critics in particular championed it at the time, some preferring it to Tommy, but it sold modestly. Its standing has risen since, largely on the strength of 'Shangri-La' and 'Victoria', and it is now seen as the end of a run of four Kinks albums about Englishness that few British bands matched. The debate is whether Davies is mocking Arthur or mourning him; most listeners now hear both at once.","Start with 'Shangri-La' and follow its sections like movements of a short symphony, then hear 'Victoria' and 'Some Mother's Son' back to back for the record's range. Read about the unmade television play first; it makes the running order make sense. Compare it with Tommy, released the same year, to hear two very different British ideas of a story told in songs."],"the-who-i-cant-explain":["Two minutes of chopped power chords, a lead vocal from Roger Daltrey that sounds tense rather than tough, and a song about a young man who cannot find the words for what he feels about a girl. Pete Townshend's guitar riff, a few ringing chords hit hard and then cut off, does most of the work; Keith Moon's drumming is already busier than anyone else's in British pop, filling every gap with rolls; John Entwistle's bass holds the floor still underneath.","The Who's first single under that name, recorded in London with the American producer Shel Talmy, who had just made the Kinks' early hits. It was released early in 1965 and slowly climbed into the British top ten, helped by a television appearance on Ready Steady Go!. The band were the house group of the London mod scene, playing for crowds of sharp-dressed teenagers on scooters, and their managers sold them as the sound of that tribe.","Townshend has said openly that he wrote it as an imitation of the Kinks' 'You Really Got Me', and once you know, the family likeness in the riff is obvious. Listen to the backing vocals: they are not the band but a hired vocal group, the Ivy League, brought in because Talmy wanted a smoother pop sheen. The tension between that polish and Moon's drums is the sound of the Who being tidied up for radio and refusing to stay tidy.","It was received as a promising mod single rather than an event, and the band's bigger statement came with 'My Generation' later that year. Over time it has come to be seen as the template for power pop and for a whole line of short, frustrated guitar songs, from the Jam to Britpop. It remained in the Who's live set for decades, usually as an opener, which says something about how well the riff wears.","Play it loud and listen only to Moon on the first pass, then only to the guitar. Notice how short it is and how little happens, and how that brevity feels like the song's subject: a boy who cannot get the words out. Then play 'You Really Got Me' and 'My Generation' around it to hear the line from one to the other."],"the-who-a-quick-one":["The Who's second album, a deliberately mixed bag: songs by every member, a cover of Martha and the Vandellas' 'Heat Wave', and a closing nine-minute piece, 'A Quick One, While He's Away', that strings six short sections into a story about a woman whose lover is away for a year, a seduction, a confession and a forgiveness. John Entwistle's 'Boris the Spider', with its growling bass voice, sits alongside Townshend's tender 'So Sad About Us'. The horns heard here are Entwistle's own.","Released at the end of 1966 on the small Reaction label; in America it appeared as Happy Jack, named after the single. The band's publishing deal required each member to supply songs, which explains Keith Moon's instrumental and Roger Daltrey's single contribution. When they ran short of material, their manager Kit Lambert, son of the composer Constant Lambert, pushed Townshend to write something longer and joined together, and the mini-opera was the result.","The mini-opera's final section, the forgiveness, builds a chorus of overlapping voices over a simple repeated chord pattern until it becomes almost hymn-like, and that build is the direct ancestor of the climax of Tommy three years later. Listen also to the cello-like sound in the third section: the band could not afford strings, so they sang the word for them instead, in imitation of bowing.","The album was reviewed as uneven, which it is by design, and the band themselves treated it as a stopgap. Its reputation rests on the title piece, which the Who performed with ferocious energy at the Rolling Stones' Rock and Roll Circus in December 1968, a performance often said to have outplayed the hosts. Historians of the concept album now point to it as the hinge between the pop single and Tommy.","Go straight to 'A Quick One, While He's Away' and treat it as six tiny songs; notice how each section changes tempo and voice. Then hear 'So Sad About Us' and 'Boris the Spider' for the band's other faces. If you can find the Rock and Roll Circus footage, watch it: the live version shows what the studio take was reaching for."],"the-who-live-at-leeds":["Six tracks on the original LP: three rock-and-roll and rhythm-and-blues covers (Mose Allison's 'Young Man Blues', Eddie Cochran's 'Summertime Blues', Johnny Kidd's 'Shakin' All Over'), the band's own 'Substitute' and 'Magic Bus', and a fifteen-minute 'My Generation' that turns into a medley and a long improvisation. It is four players at maximum volume, with no overdubbed rhythm guitar, so Townshend's chords and Entwistle's lead-like bass carry all the harmony while Moon plays continuously around them.","Recorded at the University of Leeds refectory in February 1970, when the Who had been touring Tommy for nearly a year and were as tight as they would ever be. The band wanted a live record that sounded like the real thing rather than a cleaned-up studio product. The original sleeve imitated a bootleg: plain brown card with the title stamped on it, and inside, a folder of facsimile contracts, posters and receipts from the band's early years.","The original label carried an instruction to engineers not to remove the crackles, because the tapes had audible surface noise and the band preferred it to a fix. Listen in 'Young Man Blues' to the moments where everyone stops dead and starts again on a single beat; those breaks are rehearsed but sound reckless. Expanded reissues restored the whole show, including the complete Tommy, and revealed how much the LP left out.","It was received at once as one of the best live rock albums ever issued, and that verdict has barely moved. It is often named as a blueprint for hard rock and heavy metal performance, and a model for how a live record can stand as a work in itself. The only real argument concerns which version to hear: the tight six-track LP or the sprawling complete concert.","Start with 'Young Man Blues' and focus on Entwistle, whose bass is doing what a lead guitar would do elsewhere. Then 'Summertime Blues' alongside Eddie Cochran's original, to hear what volume and Moon did to a 1958 song. Hear the original six-track LP first; come to the expanded editions later."],"the-zombies-shes-not-there":["A two-and-a-half-minute minor-key single carried by Rod Argent's electric piano rather than guitar, Colin Blunstone's breathy, almost whispered lead vocal, and a chorus where the band's voices crowd in close. The song is about a man who was warned about a girl too late. The middle features a jazz-tinged electric piano solo, and the drums play with a light, swinging touch that sets it apart from the guitar bands of 1964.","The Zombies were schoolboys from St Albans, near London, who won a local beat-group competition and with it a contract with Decca. Argent wrote the song for their first session, and it was released in the summer of 1964, when the British Invasion was at its height. It was a modest hit at home and a much bigger one in America, which led the band to tour the United States while barely known in Britain.","Listen to the harmony: instead of the blues-derived chords of most British beat records, Argent uses the kind of shifting minor chords found in modal jazz and in the church music he had sung as a chorister. Blunstone's quiet delivery was unusual for a 1964 beat record, and it makes the song feel confessional rather than performed.","It was admired from the start as an unusually sophisticated pop single, and its standing has never really dipped. Musicians cite it as an early example of jazz harmony inside British pop, and it has been covered many times, notably by Santana in 1977. Critics sometimes wonder whether the band's later failure was a matter of being too subtle for their moment; this single suggests why.","Listen first to the electric piano, which plays the role a lead guitar would in most bands of the time, then to Blunstone's breath-heavy phrasing. Compare it with 'You Really Got Me' from the same year, to hear two opposite ideas of British pop. Then go to Odessey and Oracle to hear where the same ears ended up."],"the-yardbirds-shapes-of-things":["A tightly marching two and a half minutes, with Keith Relf's wary vocal over a pounding bass line, a song musing on war, progress and the future. It builds toward a guitar solo by Jeff Beck that does not sound like a guitar solo at all: a sustained, bending, feedback-laced line with an Indian or Middle Eastern cast, held in place with the controlled howl of an amplifier pushed past its limits.","Released early in 1966, written by Relf, the drummer Jim McCarty and the bassist Paul Samwell-Smith. Beck had replaced Eric Clapton in 1965, after Clapton left in protest at the band's turn toward pop. The Yardbirds were then the most adventurous of the London rhythm-and-blues groups, and they recorded parts of their work in America, including at Chess Studios in Chicago, the home of the blues records they had started out copying.","Beck's solo is built from feedback that he is controlling with his body, moving toward and away from the amplifier to bring the howl in and out. That technique, here only a few seconds long, would become a basic vocabulary of rock guitar within two years, through Hendrix and others. Beck liked the song enough to re-record it in 1968 on his album Truth, with Rod Stewart singing.","It was a British top-ten hit and was soon described as one of the first psychedelic singles. Critics now treat it as a key moment in the history of the electric guitar, the point where feedback moved from accident to instrument. Its status is complicated only by the fact that the Yardbirds have often been remembered for whose guitarists they launched rather than for their own records.","Wait for the solo, then play it again and listen only to the sound of the guitar: the sustain, the bending, the moment the amplifier seems to sing back. Notice how the verses tighten the tension before it. Then hear 'Eight Miles High' and Hendrix's Are You Experienced to follow where that sound went."],"the-yardbirds-roger-the-engineer":["Twelve original songs, mostly short, with Jeff Beck as the sole lead guitarist: blues shuffles sit beside stranger things: 'Hot House of Omagararshid' and 'Over Under Sideways Down' bring in drones, chanting and modal guitar lines. 'Jeff's Boogie' is a jaunty instrumental showcase, and 'Lost Woman' opens the album on a hypnotic bass riff. The sound is raw, live in the room, with Beck's guitar switching between fuzz, feedback and clean blues picking.","Recorded in London in 1966 and produced by Paul Samwell-Smith and the band's manager Simon Napier-Bell; in America it came out on Epic as Over Under Sideways Down. It was the only Yardbirds studio album made up entirely of their own songs. Samwell-Smith left soon after, and Jimmy Page joined first on bass and then as a second lead guitarist beside Beck, a line-up that lasted only months and made few records.","The title and the cover drawing come from the rhythm guitarist Chris Dreja's caricature of the studio's engineer, Roger Cameron, an affectionate joke that became the name the album is now known by. Listen to 'Over Under Sideways Down': its guitar line imitates a sitar's twang and slides, and the chanting, humming backing vocals owe more to Eastern devotional music than to Chicago blues.","It sold respectably in Britain but was soon overshadowed by bigger statements later that year. Its reputation has grown among guitarists, who treat it as the best document of Beck within a band, and among historians of psychedelia, who hear it as a bridge from British blues to the studio experiments of 1967. Some still find it too uneven to rank with the albums it anticipates.","Start with 'Over Under Sideways Down' and 'Hot House of Omagararshid' for the band's strangest side, then 'Lost Woman' for its blues roots. Follow Beck's tone from song to song; he rarely plays the same way twice. Then hear Cream's Fresh Cream and Hendrix's debut, both made within the year, to hear what the Yardbirds set in motion."],"small-faces-itchycoo-park":["A gentle, sing-along song about skipping school or work to spend a sunny day in a park, sung by Steve Marriott and Ronnie Lane in turn. The verses are light and acoustic, almost nursery-rhyme simple; the chorus swells with harmonies and Ian McLagan's organ. Then, in the instrumental break, the whole sound seems to tilt and whoosh, the drums sweeping like a jet engine passing overhead. That whooshing passage is what everyone remembers.","Released in the summer of 1967 on Immediate, the independent label run by Andrew Loog Oldham, after the band left Decca. The Small Faces were an East London mod band who had built their name on hard soul-influenced singles; 'Itchycoo Park' was their move into the psychedelic summer, with a gentler, pastoral sound. It became a top-ten hit in Britain and their only big American success during the band's lifetime.","The whooshing effect is phasing, made in the studio by running two copies of the same recording slightly out of step so that the sound sweeps against itself; the Olympic Studios engineer George Chkiantz is usually credited with the technique. Few British singles had used it so prominently, and its presence on a top-ten record helped spread the technique. The song's innocent tone carries a coy double meaning, which the band enjoyed and broadcasters mostly missed.","It was a hit and has stayed one of the most played songs of 1967 on British radio, but the band later felt it misrepresented their harder, soul-based sound. Critics sometimes dismiss it as lightweight, but the phasing alone gives it a place in studio history. Its cheerful mood is now often heard as the essence of a particular English moment.","Wait for the instrumental break and listen on headphones as the phasing sweeps across the drums. Then compare Marriott's lighter singing here with his full-throated soul voice elsewhere. Follow it with Ogdens' Nut Gone Flake to hear the same band at full stretch, and with 'Waterloo Sunset' for another English pastoral from 1967."],"small-faces-ogdens-nut-gone-flake":["Two sides that could hardly be more different. Side one is six separate songs: a heavy organ-led title instrumental, the soul ballad 'Afterglow', and the cockney music-hall singalong 'Lazy Sunday'. Side two is a fairy-tale suite about Happiness Stan, a young man searching for the missing half of the moon, with songs linked by the comedian Stanley Unwin narrating in his invented, tumbling gobbledygook language. Hard rock, soul, music hall and nonsense sit side by side.","Released by Immediate in 1968 and the Small Faces' most ambitious record, made at Olympic Studios in London with Glyn Johns engineering. The title parodied a well-known brand of pipe tobacco, and the original sleeve was round, folding out like the lid of a tobacco tin. It went to the top of the British album chart, but the band found it impossible to perform live, and within a year Steve Marriott had left to form Humble Pie.","Stanley Unwin was a British radio comedian famous for 'Unwinese', a speech in which real words bend and break into new ones; the band wrote his narration as a framing device for the whole side. Listen to 'Afterglow', where Marriott's voice moves from a hush to a full soul shout in a single verse, a reminder that these were mods who learned to sing from American soul records.","It was a commercial peak and a critical success, but its very richness ended the band; the Small Faces only ever performed it once in public, on a BBC television programme. It is now regarded as one of the key British psychedelic albums and an early concept record, admired for its mix of humour and ambition. Some critics still prefer the plainer first side.","Hear 'Lazy Sunday' and 'Afterglow' back to back to grasp the band's range, then put on side two straight through, letting Unwin's narration carry you between songs. Notice the music-hall accent and piano rhythms. Compare it with the Kinks' Arthur and the Who's Tommy, both of which tried in different ways to make an album tell a story."],"the-byrds-younger-than-yesterday":["Eleven songs in under half an hour, opening with 'So You Want to Be a Rock 'n' Roll Star', a sardonic piece about the pop machine, set against a screaming audience and a trumpet. Roger McGuinn's jangling twelve-string guitar is there, but the album spreads wider: David Crosby's jazz-tinged 'Everybody's Been Burned', Chris Hillman's country-leaning songs, the space-themed 'C.T.A.-102', and a cover of Bob Dylan's 'My Back Pages' that became one of their best known recordings.","Recorded in Hollywood late in 1966 and released by Columbia early in 1967, produced by Gary Usher. The Byrds had lost their main songwriter, Gene Clark, the year before, and the space he left was filled by Hillman, who suddenly emerged as a writer, and by Crosby. The band was moving between Los Angeles folk rock and the new San Francisco sounds, and between pop success and a more experimental studio approach.","The trumpet on the opening track is played by the South African musician Hugh Masekela, then living in America. On 'Time Between' and elsewhere, the guitar of the bluegrass and country player Clarence White appears, a quiet hint of the country-rock turn the Byrds would take on Sweetheart of the Rodeo the next year. Listen to the backwards guitar on 'Mind Gardens', a sign that the band were listening hard to the Beatles' Revolver.","It sold less than earlier Byrds records and was received as a curiosity at first, but its reputation rose steadily, and many critics now rate it as the band's most consistent album. It is valued as a bridge between 1965's folk rock and the country rock of 1968, and as the record on which Hillman and Crosby became writers. Crosby's contributions remain the most debated.","Start with 'So You Want to Be a Rock 'n' Roll Star' and 'My Back Pages', then try Hillman's 'Have You Seen Her Face' and 'Time Between' to hear country edging in. Listen for how short and economical everything is. Follow it with Sweetheart of the Rodeo and Gram Parsons's records to hear where the country thread led."],"the-byrds-notorious-byrd-brothers":["Eleven songs that flow into one another with crossfades, making the album feel like a single drifting suite. There are two songs by Gerry Goffin and Carole King, 'Goin' Back' and 'Wasn't Born to Follow'; there is a hushed anti-war song, 'Draft Morning'; there is a cheerful country song, 'Old John Robertson', with a string-quartet interlude. The record ends with 'Space Odyssey', in which the electronic tones of a Moog synthesizer replace the familiar twelve-string jangle.","Made in Hollywood across the second half of 1967 with producer Gary Usher, during a period of turmoil: David Crosby was sacked from the band in the middle of the sessions, and the drummer Michael Clarke also departed. The remaining Byrds, Roger McGuinn and Chris Hillman, finished the album with session players. It was released early in 1968 by Columbia and sold poorly compared to the band's earlier work.","On the cover four windows in an old stable frame the band's faces, but only three Byrds appear; a horse looks out of the fourth. Fans long took this as a joke at Crosby's expense; band members have given differing accounts. Crosby's song 'Triad', which the others refused to release, ended up with Jefferson Airplane. Listen for the Moog throughout; it was among the first rock records to use the instrument.","Sales were weak and reviews were warm but confused, but critical opinion has turned firmly in its favour, and it is now often rated the Byrds' finest album, admired for its seamless flow and studio adventurousness. It is cited as a forerunner of the album as a continuous listening experience. Arguments continue over how much Crosby contributed before his departure.","Play it straight through without stopping, as a single half-hour piece, and notice how the songs fade into each other. Pay attention to 'Goin' Back' and 'Draft Morning' for their quiet beauty and the moment the Moog enters. Compare it with the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds and the Zombies' Odessey and Oracle for other studio albums of this moment."],"buffalo-springfield-for-what-its-worth":["A slow, uneasy song built on a cool, restrained groove, about young people and police facing off in the street. Stephen Stills sings in a calm, almost detached voice, and the chorus asks everyone to stop and listen. The defining sound is a single guitar figure from Neil Young: two high harmonic notes, chiming like a bell or a warning signal, repeated throughout. The understatement is the point; it sounds like tension rather than rage.","Stills wrote it in November 1966 after the Sunset Strip curfew riots in Los Angeles, when young people protested against a crackdown on clubs and loitering on the Strip. The band recorded it in December and Atco released it as a single within weeks; it became a top-ten hit early in 1967 and was added to later pressings of the band's debut album. Its message about the street soon attached itself to Vietnam protests.","The song is not about Vietnam, though it has become the soundtrack to countless documentaries about that war; it is about a local dispute over a nightclub strip. Listen to how little the drums and bass do, keeping a lazy, steady beat, and how Young's harmonics sit on top like an alarm. The record was made fast, and its looseness is part of its watchful mood.","It was the band's only big hit, and it has become one of the most used protest songs of the sixties, sometimes to the irritation of those who know its origins. Critics praise it for its restraint and its feeling of watchfulness. Its success arguably distorted the band's reputation, since their albums show a much wider and stranger range.","Listen first to Young's two-note figure and count how often it returns; then focus on the calm of Stills's voice. Notice that the band never builds to a climax. Follow it with Buffalo Springfield Again for their fuller sound, and with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young's 'Ohio' for a later protest song from the same musicians, written in anger rather than unease."],"buffalo-springfield-again":["Ten songs by three writers that rarely sound like the same band. Neil Young opens with 'Mr. Soul', a hard, riff-driven song, and contributes 'Expecting to Fly', a lush, orchestral dream, and 'Broken Arrow', a collage that splices in crowd noise and a jazz piano trio. Stephen Stills offers 'Bluebird', which moves from electric guitars to banjo, and 'Rock & Roll Woman'. Richie Furay brings brighter, country-tinged songs like 'A Child's Claim to Fame'.","Recorded in Los Angeles across 1967 and released by Atco late that year, while the band was coming apart: Young left and rejoined more than once, and the members often recorded separately with session players. The result is less a group statement than three solo projects sharing a sleeve. The band had become central to the Laurel Canyon scene, and its members would go on to Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young, Poco and Young's solo career.","'Expecting to Fly' is essentially a Neil Young solo track made with the arranger Jack Nitzsche, who had worked with Phil Spector, and its echo-laden strings come straight from that school. 'Broken Arrow' ends with the sound of a heartbeat, an early sign of Young's interest in sound collage. Listen to the banjo and fiddle at the end of 'Bluebird': country music is already inside this rock band.","It was well reviewed and is often called the band's best album, though its fragmentation is plain. Critics have argued that its very lack of unity is what makes it interesting: a portrait of three major songwriters before they went their own ways. Its influence on country rock and on the singer-songwriters of the early seventies is now widely recognised.","Start with 'Mr. Soul' and 'Bluebird' for the band's electric side, then 'Expecting to Fly' on headphones for Nitzsche's orchestration. Finish with 'Broken Arrow' and listen to how its sections are cut together. Then hear Crosby, Stills and Nash's first album and Neil Young's Everybody Knows This Is Nowhere to follow the band's members outward."],"jefferson-airplane-surrealistic-pillow":["Eleven songs that set the template for the San Francisco sound: folk-rock harmonies, Jorma Kaukonen's piercing lead guitar and Jack Casady's melodic, rumbling bass. Grace Slick's voice, sharp and declamatory, dominates 'Somebody to Love' and 'White Rabbit', a slow crescendo that recasts Alice in Wonderland as an allegory for drug experience. Marty Balin's tender 'Today' and 'Comin' Back to Me' provide the gentle side, and 'Embryonic Journey' is a short acoustic guitar instrumental.","Recorded in Los Angeles late in 1966 with producer Rick Jarrard and released by RCA Victor early in 1967. Slick had just joined from the Great Society, bringing two songs from that band; 'Somebody to Love' was written by her brother-in-law, Darby Slick. The album and its two hit singles helped make San Francisco the focus of national attention in the run-up to the Summer of Love.","'White Rabbit' is built on a steady bolero rhythm and a slow crescendo; Slick has said she was thinking of Ravel's Boléro and of Miles Davis and Gil Evans's Sketches of Spain, which she had listened to obsessively. Jerry Garcia of the Grateful Dead plays on several tracks and is credited on the sleeve as 'musical and spiritual adviser'. 'My Best Friend' was written by the band's former drummer, Skip Spence, who had left to join Moby Grape.","It was a commercial breakthrough and the definitive record of San Francisco psychedelia. Some critics have noted that it is more polished and folk-rooted than the band's live shows, which were looser and louder. Its two hit singles are now among the most familiar songs of the sixties, and 'White Rabbit' in particular is endlessly used in film and television to signal the era.","Listen to 'White Rabbit' alongside Ravel's Boléro to hear how the crescendo works in each, then try 'Comin' Back to Me' for the album's gentler side. Notice Casady's bass, which often carries a melody of its own. Follow it with the Grateful Dead's Live/Dead to hear the San Francisco scene stretched out on stage."],"jefferson-airplane-volunteers":["The Airplane's most openly political album, opening with 'We Can Be Together', a call for youth revolution sung in shouted unison, and closing with the title song, a rallying chant. Between them come 'Good Shepherd', an old hymn rearranged by Jorma Kaukonen, the country-flavoured 'The Farm', the post-apocalyptic 'Wooden Ships', and 'Meadowlands', a brief organ rendering of a Soviet song. Nicky Hopkins's piano runs through much of the album.","Recorded in San Francisco during 1969 and released by RCA late that year, a few months after the band played Woodstock. It was the last album with the original drummer Spencer Dryden and the last before the band splintered into side projects. The political mood reflected the Vietnam War, the urban riots and student uprisings of 1968, and the counterculture's turn from flower power toward confrontation.","'Wooden Ships' was written jointly by Paul Kantner, David Crosby and Stephen Stills, and Crosby, Stills and Nash released their own version the same year; hearing the two side by side shows the difference between the bands. Listen for Jerry Garcia's pedal steel guitar on 'The Farm', a sign of the Grateful Dead's simultaneous turn to country. 'Meadowlands' is a Red Army song, chosen as a provocation.","It was a commercial success and is often regarded as the Airplane's last great album, though some critics found its revolutionary slogans naive even at the time. RCA objected to some of its language, and the original title was shortened after a charity with a similar name complained. It is now valued as a document of the counterculture's most militant moment and as a showcase for some of the band's best playing.","Start with 'Wooden Ships' and compare it with the version on Crosby, Stills and Nash's debut. Then hear 'Good Shepherd' for Kaukonen's guitar and the band's harmonies at their best. Notice how Hopkins's piano fills the spaces. Pair it with Fortunate Son and 'Ohio' for other protest songs from the end of the decade."],"grateful-dead-live-dead":["A double album of seven performances in which songs dissolve into long collective improvisations. It opens with a twenty-three-minute 'Dark Star', where Jerry Garcia's guitar spins slow, searching lines over a floating, unhurried groove. 'St. Stephen' and 'The Eleven', the latter in a rolling eleven-beat cycle, follow without a break. Then comes Bobby Bland's 'Turn On Your Lovelight', sung by the organist Pigpen, the Reverend Gary Davis's 'Death Don't Have No Mercy', a passage of pure feedback, and a closing a cappella hymn.","Recorded at the Fillmore West and the Avalon Ballroom in San Francisco early in 1969 and released by Warner Bros. late that year. The band had spent heavily on studio albums that failed to capture their live energy, and they now recorded with a sixteen-track machine, then a new technology for live work. The line-up was at its largest, with two drummers, Bill Kreutzmann and Mickey Hart, and the keyboard player Tom Constanten.","Both Phil Lesh, the bassist, and Constanten had studied composition with Luciano Berio at Mills College, and Lesh's bass playing, which moves melodically rather than holding down the root, owes a debt to classical counterpoint and to free jazz. 'The Eleven' takes its name from its unusual eleven-beat metre, a feat few rock bands of the time would have attempted, sustained here for minutes at a time.","It was widely praised as the first record to capture the Grateful Dead's real nature, and it remains a reference point for the band's fans and for improvising rock musicians. Critics debate whether its long improvisations are profound or self-indulgent; the band's admirers point out that 'Dark Star' sounds different every time, and that this recording is only one of hundreds of versions.","Set aside time for 'Dark Star' and listen to it as you might a long jazz improvisation, following Garcia's line as it wanders and returns. Notice how Lesh's bass talks back. Then compare it with Coltrane's Live at the Village Vanguard Again or Miles Davis's Bitches Brew to hear what rock was borrowing from jazz."],"grateful-dead-workingmans-dead":["Eight compact songs, mostly acoustic and country-flavoured, a sharp turn from the long improvisations of Live/Dead. 'Uncle John's Band' opens with layered harmonies over acoustic guitars and a shifting rhythm. 'Dire Wolf' is a mock ballad, 'Cumberland Blues' a bluegrass-tinged song about a mining town, 'Black Peter' a slow lament from a dying man. 'Casey Jones' rides a train-like beat to tell the story of a reckless engineer. Robert Hunter's lyrics draw on American folk tales and work songs.","Recorded quickly in San Francisco early in 1970 and released by Warner Bros. in the spring. The band was deep in debt and reacting both to the expense of their earlier experiments and to the violence at the Altamont festival the previous December, which haunts 'New Speedway Boogie'. They had been spending time with Crosby, Stills and Nash, whose close harmony singing they set out to imitate.","Listen to the harmonies in 'Uncle John's Band': the band were not natural singers, and they worked hard to learn harmony from Crosby, Stills and Nash, which is why the singing is careful and slightly fragile. The song changes metre in its instrumental passage, a trace of the band's improvising past inside a folk song. 'Casey Jones' carries a passing drug reference in its chorus, set to the steady rhythm of a train.","It was the band's first commercial success and was immediately recognised as a turning point, opening up a large new audience. It is now regarded as one of the defining country-rock albums of its era. Some longtime fans regretted the loss of the long jams, but most critics see this and American Beauty as the band's finest studio work.","Start with 'Uncle John's Band' and listen to the harmonies and the shifting rhythm, then hear 'Cumberland Blues' for the bluegrass influence. Compare the album's compact songs with the sprawl of Live/Dead. Pair it with the Band's Music from Big Pink and the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo for other records of rock's return to American roots."],"grateful-dead-american-beauty":["Ten songs that complete the turn begun on Workingman's Dead, warmer and more varied. Phil Lesh sings 'Box of Rain', written for his dying father, over a gentle country rhythm. 'Friend of the Devil' is a fast, fingerpicked song about an outlaw on the run; 'Ripple' a near-hymn about the passage of life; 'Brokedown Palace' a farewell; 'Sugar Magnolia' a sunny love song; and 'Truckin'', the closer, a rambling account of life on the road.","Recorded in San Francisco in the late summer of 1970 and released by Warner Bros. in November, only months after Workingman's Dead. The sessions came during a hard year for the band, including the death of family members, and the album's gentleness carries a shadow of grief. The title is a pun on 'American Reality', and the sleeve's lettering can be read both ways.","The mandolin player David Grisman, later a leading figure in new acoustic music, plays on 'Friend of the Devil' and 'Ripple'. Listen to the harmonies of 'Attics of My Life', where the band's three voices move in close chords almost like a church choir, a skill they had developed from Crosby, Stills and Nash. 'Truckin'' became the band's anthem, and the drama of its lyrics was drawn from their own lives.","It was received as one of the band's best records and has been regarded as a classic ever since; many critics rate it as their finest studio album. It is often paired with Workingman's Dead as a single statement. Some fans who preferred the improvising band missed the long jams, but these songs became the core of their live sets for decades.","Start with 'Box of Rain' and 'Ripple', listening to the voices and acoustic guitars, then hear 'Truckin'' for the band's rocking side. Notice the slow tempos and the space between instruments. Pair it with Workingman's Dead and with Crosby, Stills, Nash and Young's Déjà Vu, made at the same time and in the same atmosphere."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-green-river":["Nine songs in under half an hour, played by a four-piece band with almost no studio decoration. John Fogerty's rasping voice and twangy, echo-drenched guitar dominate the title track, a homesick song about a river from childhood. 'Bad Moon Rising' sets a warning of disaster to a bright, almost cheerful country shuffle. 'Lodi' is a weary song about a musician stranded in a small town, and 'Commotion' a fast, rattling rockabilly about modern noise.","Recorded at Wally Heider's studio in San Francisco and released by Fantasy in 1969, the second of three albums the band released that year. Creedence came from El Cerrito, across the bay from San Francisco, but ignored the psychedelic scene around them, playing short, tight songs drawn from rockabilly, Memphis soul and Louisiana swamp music. John Fogerty wrote, sang, arranged and produced nearly everything.","Fogerty has said the title came from the label of a soft-drink syrup, but the song's imagined river comes from childhood summers spent at a creek in Northern California. The 'swamp' sound of Creedence, which sounds as if it came from Louisiana, was entirely invented by a band who had never lived there; they built it from records by Sun artists, Bo Diddley and Louisiana swamp-pop singers.","It was a major success and has remained one of the band's most admired records. Critics praised its economy, a sharp contrast to the long jams of the San Francisco bands. Creedence was sometimes dismissed by the underground press as a singles band, but their reputation has grown steadily, and they are now regarded as one of the great American rock bands of the era.","Start with 'Green River' and listen to Fogerty's guitar echo and the push of the rhythm section, then 'Bad Moon Rising' for the gap between its bouncy tune and its dark warning. Notice how short and lean the songs are. Compare it with the Band's records from the same years for a different take on American roots music."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-fortunate-son":["Two and a quarter minutes of furious, driving rock, with John Fogerty's voice at its most raw. The song is an attack on the privileged families whose sons avoided military service in Vietnam while working-class boys went to fight. It starts with a sudden crash of drums and a jangling guitar riff, then rides a relentless beat with no slow-down, no solo of any length and no softening chorus. The anger is in the speed.","Released by Fantasy in the autumn of 1969 as a double-sided single with 'Down on the Corner', and included on the album Willy and the Poor Boys. Fogerty had served in the Army Reserve and was disgusted by the way the children of the powerful escaped the draft. The song appeared at the height of the anti-war movement, in the same season as the large Moratorium protests across America.","The song is often misheard or misused as patriotic, and has been played at political events in support of the very things it attacks; it is a song against privilege, not against soldiers. Listen to the band's restraint: the rhythm section plays straight, with no fills, so that the force comes from Fogerty's voice and the hard-strummed guitar. It is among the shortest songs in the band's catalogue.","It was a hit and became one of the defining protest songs of the Vietnam era, used in countless films about the war. Critics praise its directness and its class consciousness, which set it apart from more abstract anti-war songs. Its frequent use in contexts that ignore its meaning has become a subject of discussion in itself, and Fogerty has objected to its misuse.","Listen to the first second, the drum crash, and notice how the song never lets up. Focus on Fogerty's voice and the way his phrasing seems to spit the words out. Pair it with 'Ohio' and 'For What It's Worth' for three very different protest songs, and with Pete Seeger's 'Waist Deep in the Big Muddy' for an earlier generation's approach."],"creedence-clearwater-revival-cosmos-factory":["Eleven tracks that mix new John Fogerty songs with covers of the records he grew up on. The originals include 'Travelin' Band', a Little Richard-style rocker; 'Lookin' Out My Back Door', a cheerful, almost childlike song; 'Run Through the Jungle', dark and swampy, with backward guitar; 'Up Around the Bend'; and 'Who'll Stop the Rain'. The covers include Bo Diddley, Roy Orbison, Arthur Crudup and an eleven-minute version of 'I Heard It Through the Grapevine'.","Released by Fantasy in the summer of 1970, the band's fifth album in two years, at the peak of their commercial success. The title comes from the band's rehearsal space, a warehouse in Berkeley that the drummer Doug Clifford, nicknamed Cosmo, called 'the factory'. Within months, the band's guitarist Tom Fogerty, John's brother, would leave, tensions over John's control of the group having grown impossible.","'Run Through the Jungle' is often taken to be about Vietnam, but Fogerty has said it was about gun violence in America. Listen to the long 'Grapevine', where the band stretches out into a slow, groovy jam; it is a rare example of Creedence playing at length, and the result is closer to a Memphis soul band than to the San Francisco jam bands. The saxophone on some tracks is played by Fogerty himself.","It was the band's biggest selling album and is often regarded as their finest. Critics praise its range, from simple pop to swamp-blues menace, and its tribute to the band's influences. Some have found the long 'Grapevine' unnecessary, but others see it as a clue to the band's roots. It is now one of the most played rock albums of its time.","Start with 'Run Through the Jungle' and 'Who'll Stop the Rain' for the darker side, then 'Lookin' Out My Back Door' for the lighter. Then hear the 'Grapevine' and compare it with Marvin Gaye's version. Notice how tight the band is even when stretching out. Pair it with Green River to hear how much the band developed in a year."],"big-brother-and-the-holding-company-cheap-thrills":["Seven tracks, built around Janis Joplin's voice, which moves from a whisper to a scream within a phrase, over a ragged, overdriven band. 'Piece of My Heart' became a hit; 'Summertime' turns George Gershwin's lullaby into a slow, twisting guitar duet and a cry of pain; 'Ball and Chain', from the repertoire of Big Mama Thornton, runs nearly ten minutes and ends in a howl. 'Turtle Blues', a Joplin original, is a quiet, piano-backed blues.","Released by Columbia in 1968 after the band's explosive appearance at the Monterey Pop Festival the year before made Joplin a star. The producer John Simon struggled to capture the band in the studio, and the record was assembled mostly from studio takes with crowd noise added, to give it a live feeling; 'Ball and Chain' alone was recorded at a real concert. The cover was drawn by the underground cartoonist Robert Crumb.","Listen to 'Summertime': the two guitarists, Sam Andrew and James Gurley, weave an interlocking, almost Baroque counterpoint, a strange and touching meeting of classical habits and acid rock. The album's 'live' atmosphere is mostly an illusion, created in the studio. Joplin's voice owed its rawness to her deep study of blues singers like Bessie Smith, whose grave she helped to mark with a headstone.","It was a huge success, topping the American album chart, and made Joplin the leading female voice in rock. Some critics found the band sloppy and believed Joplin was held back by them; others argued their rawness was the right setting for her. Joplin left the band soon after, and the album is now valued as the best record of her with the group that made her famous.","Start with 'Summertime' and compare it with the original in Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, then hear 'Piece of My Heart' and 'Ball and Chain' for Joplin at full power. Notice how she uses breaks and cracks in her voice as expression. Pair it with Pearl to hear her with a tighter band."],"janis-joplin-pearl":["Ten tracks with the Full Tilt Boogie Band, a tighter and more soulful group than Big Brother. Kris Kristofferson's 'Me and Bobby McGee', a song about a drifter remembering a lost companion, starts soft and acoustic and builds to a joyful, rough-voiced climax. 'Cry Baby' and 'Get It While You Can' are soul ballads at full volume. 'Mercedes Benz' is sung unaccompanied, a wry prayer for luxury goods. 'Buried Alive in the Blues' is an instrumental.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1970 with the producer Paul Rothchild, best known for his work with the Doors. Joplin had left Big Brother, struggled with a first solo band, and found a sympathetic group in Full Tilt Boogie. She died in October 1970, before the sessions were finished, and the album was completed and released by Columbia in 1971. Pearl was her nickname.","'Buried Alive in the Blues' is an instrumental because Joplin had been scheduled to record her vocal the day after she died; the band left the track as it stood. 'Mercedes Benz' was recorded in a single take, and you can hear her laugh at the end. Listen to 'Me and Bobby McGee' for how she changes the song's mood from a folk ballad to a soul celebration.","It was a posthumous success, topping the American album chart, and 'Me and Bobby McGee' became a number one single. Critics have praised it as her most accomplished record and lamented the unfinished career. The debate is whether the polished band brought out her best or tamed her; most listeners now regard it as the best showcase for her voice.","Start with 'Me and Bobby McGee' and listen to how the arrangement builds with her voice, then hear 'Cry Baby' for her soul style. Notice the control as well as the power. Compare it with Cheap Thrills to hear how the band changes the singer, and with Aretha Franklin's records of the same years."],"moby-grape-moby-grape":["Thirteen short songs, written and sung by all five members, played with three interlocking guitars. 'Omaha' is a speedy, shouted song that crashes in and out; 'Hey Grandma' a bright, jangling rocker; '8:05' a gentle acoustic country ballad; 'Sitting by the Window' a wistful folk song. The sound is tight and concise, more like a British beat group or a country band than the long-jamming San Francisco bands around them.","Recorded in Los Angeles with producer David Rubinson and released by Columbia in 1967, at the height of the San Francisco boom. The label, trying to promote the band, released five singles at once, which confused radio and became a notorious marketing failure. Legal troubles followed soon after, and the band never recovered its momentum. Skip Spence, one of the guitarists, had been Jefferson Airplane's first drummer.","Listen to the three guitars of Spence, Jerry Miller and Peter Lewis, which play distinct parts that fit together like pieces of a puzzle, a texture very different from most San Francisco bands. On the original cover, the drummer Don Stevenson is making a rude gesture with his finger, which was airbrushed out of later pressings. Spence went on to make a cult solo album, Oar, in 1969.","It received excellent reviews but sold poorly, due in part to the label's mishandling. It has since been recognised as one of the finest debut albums of the sixties, and many musicians cite it as an influence. The band's story is often told as a cautionary tale about the music industry, but the record itself is now praised for its energy and songwriting.","Start with 'Omaha' and 'Hey Grandma' for the guitar interplay, then '8:05' for the country side. Listen to how quickly each song gets to the point. Pair it with Jefferson Airplane's Surrealistic Pillow and the Byrds' Younger Than Yesterday for other West Coast records of the same year."],"van-dyke-parks-song-cycle":["Ten songs that sound like a musical collage of American history, played by an orchestra and studio musicians and sung by Van Dyke Parks in a slightly nasal, playful voice. The arrangements shift constantly: ragtime piano, banjos, string swells, marching bands, accordions, music-box sounds. 'Vine Street', by Randy Newman, opens it; 'Palm Desert' evokes the Californian landscape; 'The All Golden' is a portrait of a friend. The production uses tape speed changes, echo and sudden cuts.","Released by Warner Bros. in 1968 and produced by Lenny Waronker, with engineer Bruce Botnick. Parks had been Brian Wilson's lyricist on the unfinished Smile project, and he made this album with lavish studio resources. It sold very poorly, and Warner Bros. responded with self-mocking advertisements making a joke of how much money it had lost. Parks went on to work as an arranger and producer for others.","Listen to the way Parks quotes and alludes to older American music: a fragment of a hymn, a parlour song, a march. The influence of Charles Ives, who also layered American tunes on top of one another, is everywhere. Parks's lyrics, as on Smile, are dense with puns and wordplay, and his studio tricks owe as much to radio drama as to pop records.","It was greeted with confusion and some admiration, and it sold poorly. Its reputation has grown slowly, as a cult record and a forerunner of later orchestral pop. Critics are still divided: some hear a visionary work about America, others a self-indulgent and impenetrable exercise. Musicians have often championed it, while general listeners find it hard work.","Treat it as a sound collage rather than a set of songs. Start with 'Vine Street' and 'Palm Desert', and listen for the constant shifts in orchestration. Pair it with the Beach Boys' Smile sessions and with Ives's Three Places in New England to hear its roots."],"crosby-stills-and-nash-crosby-stills-and-nash":["Ten songs built on close three-part harmony, a sound in which no single voice leads. 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' opens the album with a seven-minute piece in several sections, played on tuned-down acoustic guitars. 'Marrakesh Express' is Graham Nash's bright travelogue; 'Guinnevere' a slow, modal ballad by David Crosby; 'Wooden Ships' imagines survivors after a nuclear war; 'Helplessly Hoping' a delicate song with tight harmonies; and 'Long Time Gone' a slow, angry response to the assassination of Robert Kennedy.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1969 and released by Atlantic in May, a few months before the trio played Woodstock. Each member came from a successful band: Crosby from the Byrds, Stephen Stills from Buffalo Springfield, Graham Nash from the British group the Hollies. They found their sound singing together at a party, and the album helped establish the singer-songwriter era in rock. Stills played most of the instruments, with Dallas Taylor on drums.","The harmonies are recorded with each voice close to the microphone and carefully blended, so that you hear a single sound rather than three people. Listen to 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' and notice how the guitar tuning lets Stills play rich, ringing chords, and how the song moves through sections with different tempos. The closing section is sung in Spanish.","It was a huge success and became one of the defining records of the late sixties and early seventies. Critics praised the harmonies but some found the songs too sweet or self-satisfied. Its influence on soft rock and on the Laurel Canyon singer-songwriters is enormous, and its harmonies became a model for many bands, including the Grateful Dead.","Start with 'Suite: Judy Blue Eyes' and follow its sections, then 'Helplessly Hoping' for the harmonies. Notice how the voices move together. Pair it with Buffalo Springfield Again and the Byrds' Younger Than Yesterday to hear where the members came from, and with Déjà Vu for what came next."],"crosby-stills-nash-and-young-deja-vu":["Ten songs by four writers, with Neil Young joining the trio and bringing a rougher edge. 'Carry On' opens with a burst of acoustic guitars and harmonies; 'Teach Your Children' is a country song with pedal steel; 'Almost Cut My Hair' a ragged, angry Crosby blues; 'Helpless' Young's slow, aching song about a northern Ontario town; 'Woodstock' Joni Mitchell's song, turned into a hard rocker; 'Our House' Nash's cosy domestic portrait; and 'Déjà Vu' a jazzy, shifting meditation on reincarnation.","Recorded in San Francisco and Los Angeles in late 1969 and released by Atlantic in March 1970. Young, Stills's old colleague from Buffalo Springfield, had joined for live work in 1969, including Woodstock. The sessions were long and difficult, with each member often recording separately. Crosby was grieving the death of his partner, Christine Hinton, in a car accident, and the album's mood carries that loss.","Jerry Garcia of the Grateful Dead plays the pedal steel on 'Teach Your Children', a sign of the close ties between the two bands. Listen to the title track, where Crosby's song shifts metre and key, a nod to his interest in jazz. Joni Mitchell wrote 'Woodstock' without attending the festival, and this version, loud and electric, is very different from her own gentle recording.","It was a massive success and has remained one of the best-selling albums of its era. Critics praised its range but noted the divisions within the band. Young's songs are often singled out as the strongest, and the record is seen as the high point of the four together. Its harmonies and its picture of the counterculture made it one of the defining records of 1970.","Start with 'Helpless' and 'Teach Your Children', then hear 'Woodstock' and compare it with Joni Mitchell's version on Ladies of the Canyon. Notice the different voices of the four writers. Pair it with American Beauty and Neil Young's After the Gold Rush, made at the same time."],"crosby-stills-nash-and-young-ohio":["A three-minute song of grief and fury about the shooting of students by soldiers at a university campus in Ohio. Neil Young's song is built on a dark, menacing guitar riff and a slow, heavy beat. The band's harmonies, usually sweet, are here strained and anguished. Young's guitar and Stephen Stills's trade brief, stinging lines. At the end, the voices repeat a single phrase, building to a cry.","Written by Young in May 1970 after National Guard troops shot four students dead at Kent State University during a protest against the expansion of the Vietnam War into Cambodia. The band recorded it within weeks, live in the studio in Los Angeles, and Atlantic released it in June, with Stills's 'Find the Cost of Freedom' on the other side. It was rushed out while 'Teach Your Children' was still on the charts.","The recording was made in just a few takes, with the band playing together in one room; you can hear the rawness in the slightly rough edges. Listen to the end, where David Crosby's voice breaks away from the harmony into anguished shouts. The song names the president of the day, which made some radio stations refuse to play it. It was the band's most direct political statement.","It was a hit despite radio bans and has become one of the most powerful protest songs of its era. Critics praise its directness and urgency. It is often paired with Fortunate Son and For What It's Worth as a defining anti-war song, and it is now widely regarded as one of the greatest examples of rock responding to current events.","Listen to the opening riff and notice its darkness, then focus on the voices, especially in the final minute. Notice how the song never resolves into comfort. Pair it with 'Fortunate Son' and 'For What It's Worth' for three different approaches to protest, and with Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' for an earlier generation's."],"the-mamas-and-the-papas-california-dreamin":["A song about longing for California's warmth during a cold East Coast winter, sung by four voices in tight harmony over a folk-rock backing. Denny Doherty sings lead, with the others answering in close echoes; Cass Elliot's voice gives the harmonies their warmth. The guitar intro, a descending figure, is instantly recognisable. In the middle, a solo on alto flute adds a soft, jazzy colour.","Written by John and Michelle Phillips in New York during the winter of 1963, before they moved to California. It was recorded in Los Angeles in 1965 with producer Lou Adler and released by Dunhill late that year, becoming a hit early in 1966. The group came from the New York folk scene and the record helped bring the harmony-rich folk rock of Los Angeles to the charts.","The backing track was originally recorded for the singer Barry McGuire, with the group on backing vocals; Doherty's lead vocal later replaced McGuire's. The alto flute solo was played by the jazz musician Bud Shank. Listen to how the harmonies are arranged as call and response, with the backing voices echoing the lead line, a trick borrowed from gospel.","It was a huge success and has remained one of the defining songs of the sixties. Critics have praised its harmonies and its evocation of longing. It is often seen as the start of the Laurel Canyon era and has been widely covered. Its use in films and advertising has made it one of the most familiar songs of its time.","Listen to the guitar intro and then the harmonies, noticing how the voices answer each other. Wait for the flute solo and notice its jazzy colour. Pair it with the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man' and the Beach Boys' 'California Girls' for other visions of California in the mid-sixties."]});